      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <p>uouNTY butwucitt or VVlS.to.muluoi.</p>
        <p>IVIIINICIPAL ELECTIONS, NOVEMBER bia, 1906.—T0 THH BURGHSsit9 OF LATCHFORD</p>
        <p>WARD.</p>
        <p>LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, With the unanimous support of the Conservative Ward Committee and at the request of a isrge and influential body of ratepayers, I once more be to offer myself as the Conservative Workingmen's Candidate for this Ward.</p>
        <p>During my past three years of service, I have made it a point to attend as many meetings AB potsible of every Committee of which I have been a memb,r, with a view to enabling me to follow the business of the town from week to week, and so obtain a thorlugh and true grip of every transaction that might take place I am a determined advocate of economy in the spend- ing of the public money so far as the same is consistent with the general improvement and healthy upkeep of our town. _</p>
        <p>I have strongly advocated Free Bath ing for our School Children, more open spaces in our tt ickly populated districts, also public Bowling Greens in our Parke. I have worked hard and consistently for the reduction of the price of gas to those who consume gas through slot meters. 'This labcrar. lam pleased to say, has not been in vain, for such consumers are now receiving more value for their money since my agitation. I have fought strongly on behalf of Workmen's Tram " Care. The present system of fare,r is not entirely what I desire, and I can assure you that I mean to take fhis question up again at an early date on behalf of the work- ing people, if I once more become your representative. I ion entirely opposed to all Unfair or Immoral Trading and, so far as I am concerned, I would send any person engaged in such trading to the right about at once Ito person, whoever he may be, should be allowed to have mat in the Council for the advancement of his own personal ends. In my public life ev3ry transaction that I have been connected with will bear the light of day, my motto being Justice, honesty and purity." My opponent takes exception to my being paid a nominal salary as the Mayor's Auditor. A,fter doing the work, which entailed weeks of time on the top of my duties as a Councillor, no man with any idea of justice or fair play would object to my reielving the salary which my predecessors have been aid At the same time I may also add that if I had ot taken the fee, there would not have been a saying of one penny to the ratepayers, as the full amount would then have been divided between the two elective auditors, who are Loth in a better financial position than myself. During the audit I examined about 32,C00 accounts and bank cheques. I went into every possible detail of the financial position of the town, and it there had been any- thing at all wrong with the finances, I should at once have brought the same to the knowledge of tte rate- payers. I can assure you that the abstract of the financial statement as published by the llorough Treasurer is a true and faithful account of the financial position of the Town.</p>
        <p>- In conclusion, if you do me the honour to re-elect me as one of your representatives to the Town Council, your confidence will not be misplaced, and it will be my whole ambition to serve you from a workingman's point of view faithfully and well to the utmOst of my ability. Believe me to remain.: Ladies and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, WILLIAM JOHN WILSON. Thelwall-lane, October 23rd 1906.</p>
      </div>
