      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>2 GARYJR"Zit McDOUGALL'S Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR The Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906</p>
        <p>( o;7Yri9hL )</p>
        <p>Bat an evil spirit designed the capital of the world for the abAseinent of literary genius. London, all unconscious 'of the siege, went about its daily business of profit or pleasure, its heart shut, its greaf ears stone deaf.</p>
        <p>Letters reached Keith from the country, eipecially from Nellie' asking when the masterpieces were to appear, and what magazines or papers were to have the honour of publishing them. He felt chill as for the first time in his life he under- stood the cruel irony of will-meaning people. What magazines or pipers wionlcl he °honour I Why should they rub bay-salt under the eyelids in that way,? He grinned the grin which signifies the death of the first-born and most,cherished illusion. At length two of the lyrics atmeared in a paper which lie was ashamed to send either to his old University friends or to Nell; and after a weary speculation about price he received a postal order for ten shillings. Five shillings a lyric! Who lied about the earnings of poets ?. Two guineas had been his rosy minimum. Sitting on the edge of •the bed, his head in a swirl as from a sudden blow, he re-read the editor's letter. Five shillings Two half-crowns for a deathless song. Twice two- and-sir! It was impossible. He wrote humbly to the editor suggesting a mistake had been made; and the editor replied there was no mistake, adding, with cruel bluntness, • that if the terms were not satisfactory Mr. Keith need not send any more verse. And people asked what editor he meant to honour!</p>
        <p>The blighting note reached him on Hallowe'en. For auld lang syne he meant to treat himself that evening; there was to be a little dinner, regardless of extra pence, with the innocent diversion of the play afterwards. He had visions of fun and beaming faces in the simple happy North, and in his ears was a sound of inextinguishable laughter. Some of the merry company were doubtless thinking of him, perhaps envying him. What an Aristophanes was Fate I-</p>
        <p>In his dolour he remembered the tale of village life. It was ragged and travel-stained; nevertheless, it went forth again. Contumely was heaped upon it. as.before ; but at last. after much suffering, it appeared in a paper printed for the delectation of +gallery maids and factory girls, and brought him a guinea. The article on the need of new blood in literature was not wanted anywhere. What had become of his dreams ?</p>
        <p>Like a good Scot he hardened his heart, and moved nearer Heaven, explaining to the landlady that brain-workers need quiet. -He had been up hret stairs; he now went up three more, and toiled in his attic as the desperate toil for fire and food. Torturing letters continued to reach him from the colintry. What was he doing? When were his poetus and stories to appear ? "Can't they give me peace?" he cried, writhing under the sting of misguided friendship. "Can't they give roe peace?" and there broke from him a sob of anger and pain. - Hope forsook him, and soon after sleep went also. His hair grew lank, his cheeks wan and hollow, his eyes wide and strangely uneasy. One night, while he feverishly tramped for fresh air, the street suddenly yawned at his feet. He looked down a moment, marvelling at that unexpectgd opening; then his hair rose and he fled, cold with horror.</p>
        <p>He came to himself, sick and panting, to realise that he must choose between rest and madness. But how could he rest and live? Home in his present condition was out of the question, and elsewhere there was no help. After an agonising night he decided to ask the old folk for a loan. He made the request with a bravado for which he scorned himself. The remittance came by return of post with a letter from his mother rejoicing in the news that he was getting on with the great work. Since it needed such concentration it must be a masterpiece. When would it be ready? The hapless man groaned as he read. Was the spirit of evil in all hearts that he had to endure such stripes ? He took three days at an obscure village on the South coast, then returned to besiege the Press. But it seemed no one wanted poetry, or fiction, or general articles, though all these appeared in a thousand quarters; and there was no vacancy on the staff of any reputable journal. In sheer hunger lie took to Museum work, the denguer ressort of the stranded genius. The occupation was, of course, temporary, he told himself. He would yet prove the stuff that was in him ; yes, he would yet mate the world ring with his praise. Museum work during the day, that would be bread and biitter, and the nights—the long, long nights when Babylon was steeped in sin or pleasure—for the masterpiece. And lie began it in such a fervour of enthusiasm as had not bnrnt along his veins and danced in his pulses since the first golden days of ambition. A man stirred to the depths is capable of surprising things, and the novel grew rapidly under Keith's feverish hand. He had inspiration—oh, yes, the divine afflatus was upon him. It tingled in his brain, rushed from his flying pen. The trivial, the fantastic, the affected, the merely clever, had no longer any fascination, for he was putting into the book the best his Maker gave him. Often in the evening lie returned to his desolate room dead weary and with a racking Museum headache. Ali I that Museuazi headache, if it would only spare him. But 'ssonce safe in his Teufelsdrockli tower lie stoutly shut the door upon the world and was happy with the intense, unspeak- able happiness of the creative artist. His characters lived with him, touched and moved him to unimaginable transports, charmed him even to the forgetting of disappointment and hunger. He glowed and flushed over them, and, flushing, often coughed in his excitement. And one night; in the midst of a great scene, a scene of heart-gripping tragedy, there came with the cough something which tasted salt. A little of it dropped on the sheet before him; it was crimson. Anyone looking on would have marked a face suffused with colour becoming suddenly blanched. The pen dropped from his fingers; he took up the sheet and examined it close to the light. "Arterial blood!" he said, aloud, unconsciously repeating the terrible words of Keats: "Arterial blood."</p>
        <p>Then laying the sheet on the table, he sat on the edge of his bed, his countenance like marble. He thought afterwards of the desperate calm which came over him at that fearful moment. "Walter Keith," he said, quietly, as if to someone else—" Walter Keith, whatever you intend to do, do it quickly, for time and you are going to part." So it had come to that. Destiny was already writing the word, "Finis." Next day he was in the Museum as usual ; next night he was hard at the novel. A frantic desire to make haste possessed him. Let him get this last work finished at all hazards; and then—then whatever the good God willed. So he went 'on full pitch in spite of cough, in spite of weakness and hunger, in spite of more arterial blood. None knew of his design, none guessed his condition, and as long as possible he kept away from the doctors. • "They'd make me give up," he told himself, "and 1 can't afford that. Let's finish before consulting the confederate of death." He had one particular friend, a Bohemian journalist, strong as an elephant, light-hearted and philosophic as a boy, by name Arthur Beveridge. This man began to notice the thinness of Keith's cheek, the unnatural lustre of his eye, and ihe hollowness of his cough ; but Keith only laughed. We're all liable to colds and as to being thint; why, Dickens always got like a scarecrow when working hard," he would say, with an inward chill which to himself made mockery of the words.</p>
        <p>- One evenitn, Beveridge was surprised to find his friend sitting pensively beside a locked and labelled trunk. -</p>
        <p>" have given up my room," Keith explained, nith a nee smile.</p>
        <p>"And where are you going?" Beveridge asked, anxiously. "Oh to capital lodgings—known to the public as the hospital. 1 am going to get rid of this cough, and—and 1 thought you'd take care of this trunk fcr me." _</p>
        <p>.„. " Certainl y. certainly," returned Beveridge, quickly. "But you startle me about the hospital." "I thought IV:r. Beveridga was a philosopher," rejoined Keith. "WhaVs the odds ? Everything in this world is precisely as we take it. I am going where they cure coughs. Oh! you needn't protest or waste words in sympathy. I'm ordered to go—at once. You'll take carelif my trunk, and •if through all eternity there's a chance, I'll repay the kindness. I've envied you that body of yours, Beveridge—especially the lungs, and yet why should I? To-day, to-morrow, or the day after— what does it matter which it is . . 'Though one were strong as seven, He too—' But never mind that. I am going to my new quarters in a Cab with my doctor; I shall leave in a more imposing chariot—with my conch completely cured. Keep you the box; I'll tell you more about it later on. By the way, you'll come to see ; consumption's not catching. "I'll go with you," said Bereridge, his deep voice quavering.</p>
        <p>"No, no," responded Keith, "no need for that. Old Crossbones is sufficient. Besides, he's depressing company. Good-bye; Bohemia's a fine country, though hard on the constitution. Great also is journalism, and as for literature,—lts divine, though, like a vampire, it drinks its victims' blood."</p>
        <p>He was calm and quite resolute; no Stoic ever looked adverse fate more serenely in the face. / He pressed the doctors for information, and tbey answered with light equivocation. His rejoinder was a murmured regret that a dying man could not have the truth. Secretly he thought there was much to be said for the old Roman method of reaching the end. Beveridge clung to him like a shadow. One day when the lusty Bohemian called Keith was very low, and the nurse thought he ought not to be disturbed. But the patient heard, and insisted,, on seeing his friend. "Hemorrhage again this morning," he explained, feebly. "Another, and good-bye for ever.' He put out a transparent wafer hand, which Ceveridge caught and unconsciously began to stroke. Twice the giant made an effort to speak and failed; the third time he succeeded.</p>
        <p>"Is there- anyone you would like to see from home " he murmured.</p>
        <p>The sick man, who had been gazing up in a kind of astonishment and pity at Beveridge's troubled face, gave a little start. "Kb?" he said, as one suddenly roused from sleep. "Is there no one you would like to see from home ?" Beveridge repeated. "No one I would like to see from home ?" said Keith. Then he raised himself a little, looking with a fearful intensity into the ;yes above him. "Could I stand it?" A tide of colour surged intc his wasted face, and his breathing was a frantic pant. "There, you must not excite yourself," said Bevpridge, gently. The sick man gave a kind of gasp, his hot hands like a vice on his friend's.</p>
        <p>"Beveridge, would a man like to see his beloved in Heaven ? There's one—but----" .</p>
        <p>He fell back breathless, and Beveridge managed to whisper: "But what?" "Oh! I couldn't stand it, and she couldn't,' came in a spent voice. "Beveridge, were you evez in love—in downright earnest, I mean ?" Beveridge nodded, his under-lip trembling perceptibly. "You'll understand, then," panted Keith. "We were to be married—married, you know. We made a lot of plans—a lot. Everything was to gc so well here in London, but we forgot about hospitals." He held on tighter to the strong hand. "My head grows dizzy. must be run down but I'll be all right presently." He breathed harder and shorter. _</p>
        <p>"We were to be married, you understand, and von wouldn't bring a bride here, woukl you I This isn't the place for one's promised bride., " He looked up, a piteous slonging in every featurs of his face. _</p>
        <p>"Ohl if it were pos3ibla," he said. "Ten minutes would make up for all—ten minutes. But it cannot be," he added, his voice breaking. "She knows nothing of all this—nothing." He closed his eye, and so swift a change came upon his countenance that Beveridge leaped up in fright to call for help. The movement broughi Keith to himself.</p>
        <p>"Sit down," he said, with a faint smile. "It'i a trouble to look up so far." ,Then, musingly "Eh, Beveridge, what a Hercules you are I Imo] at your fists and mine granite and gossamei —gossamer that the wind sweeps away. And ] was anxious to live and you never cared. Sc things go: the prodigal rich and the miser • bankrupt. Well, never mind, accounts will be fairly balanced some day. There's One, Beveridge, who never errs. After midnight the dawn—a burst of light. On the other side of the ferry be waiting to welcome you. Meantime I have a favoui to ask of you." Beveridge's great shoulders were heaving. "You'll break me down if you don't stop," pro- tested the sick man.</p>
        <p>Beveridger gulped something andjtried to look unconcerned.</p>
        <p>"I've something to ask," repeated Keith. "Ask oh," said Beveridge. "Well, when I'm gone open my trunk and you'll find something there for the publishers. lt's my last will and testament, written, Heaven knows, witlr one hand, while keeping death at bay with the other. I thought it tolerable, but you'll see, and if it's published you'll put my name to it, so—so that it may he known I had something in me. And, Beveridge, lien, for this is very special—if there's anything in the way of money it's to be divided into two equal parts. There ars directions with the manuscript. Promise me." "I promise." "Nay, but as Jacob Paid, swear." "How shall I swear?"</p>
        <p>"Take a dying man's hand and swear before-the God who n,n'-e you and will call you to account if you are false." And when Beveridge had taken the oath: "Don't -imagine I mistrust you, old chap; but I'm anxious, and, you see, I shall not tt., titre. Isn't it strange to think of that ? In future if you come across any young fool wishing to take to literature, tell him from one who has died for it that prussic acid is both.easier, and quicker." A week later Keith left in the chariot more imposing than a cab, a small company of Bohemians doing, him firal honour, and he steeps where the roar of Babylcn comes as a musical hum. In the evening after the funeral a few of his oldest friends went to Deveridge's lodgings by invitation; in their presence he opened the trunk and drew forth the manuscript of 'The Masterpiece," so called, half-playfully, half-satirically, in memory of the past. Beveridge began to read aloud, and his guilats reeked not of the flight of time. For Keith had at last and with his dying hand justified all hopes and expertations. When in themhill grey dawn the visitors rose to go the wind moaned and soughed dolefully as it tried window and chimney. One or two, excited to weird imaginations, thrilled at the thought that it might be the )vailing spirit of Waiter Keith. Who knows ? He may have been grieving over his inability to join visibly in the triumph. But, if temporal things mean aught to the dead he had his reward for many an hour of weariness and toil, and hunger, and neglect, and sore disappointment. :For the critics votrehed out their superlatives in praising "The Masterpiece." Moreover, Babylon, the malccr of fortunes, titillated to a momentary interest, bought and read, with proper reflections on the mysterious decree which pre- scribes the early extinction of genius. And while the story was the talk of dinner-tables and clubs, a man and two women, one old, the oilier young, came and bowed themselves by a new grave. Then, the last tribute of love rendered, they rose and silently departed, none heeding. "The Masterpiece" is still conspicuous in its publisher's list, and has often been imitated, brit with small success, for none of Walter Keith's imitators had Walter Keith's story to tell. [THE END.</p>
        <p>Coughed Incessantly. Was so Choked Up, Could Hardly Breathe,</p>
        <p>CURED IN 'A FEW DAYS BY</p>
