      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"THE MASTERPIECE.*</head>
        <p>"THE MASTERPIECE.*</p>
        <p>By JOHN A. STEUART,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Xolly Pashas," "In the Day o 'Battle," "A Millionaire's Daughter," "Self- Exiled," "Kilgroom," &amp;a-</p>
        <p>Or ever he was breeched, Walter Keith excited romantic hopes and stirred the spirit of prophecy. In the rosy dawn of innocence his wistful blue eyes were admired for their magic lights, and young ladies of a discerning or sentimental turn liked to smooth back his tonsle of golden hair that they might gaze on the fair forehead of a coming poet. Occasionally they sighed, musing rapturously on the love songs he would one day write, and trill- jealously envying the happy one in advance. Throughout his career at the village school Walter kept expectation on the stretch, a much greater achievement than any feat of scholarship. Your common plodders may take prizes; for that perseverance and the lust of gain will suffice, but only the divinely gifted can maintain interest at high pitch without descending to the commonplace performance. . . .</p>
        <p>Many a time the gnarled old dominie looked over his spectacles at Keith, his face a study in perplexity. Many a time his hand was raised to strike, and fell weakly without striking, to the amazement of urchins accustomed to the ferule.</p>
        <p>"We must have patience,", he said once, as in self-defence. "He's hard to fathom. At any moment that boy may ilve the lie to hasty judgment. No, sir, I don't covet the distinction of calling a new Sir Walter booby." At the University, whither he went with innutherable prayers and some hard-saved sovereigns, he cOntinued the traditions of school, piquantly touching curiosity, while savouring life and cultivatinc, poesy. With unconscious irony the maternal 'heart had destined him for the Church.</p>
        <p>"I have dedicated him to the Lord," said his mother the day he left for college, tears of jtoy streaming down her white face. "I have dedicated him to the Lord, even as Hannah dedicated Samuel. He is my only one." But when the time for taking the theological course came his companions laughed. "Watty clastinr, pulpit cushions; Keith beating the drum ecclesiastic," they cried, and exploded in derision. • The echoes reached his mother as she read for the hundredth time the beautiful story of Samuel, and she got to her knees, after the manner of old- fashioned mothers. Perhaps the dedication had been amiss, or her schemes may have been laid in pride. At any rate, her wrestlings and pleadings did not avail to turn the drift of things. With his back to the Church, Walter thought of the law; but a short study proved it dry. Medicine, at which he glanced, was found equally dry; and what has a poet to do with Arynese ? So he took the plunge ; that is to say, his friends were thrilled with the announcement that he had decided to "embrace literature."</p>
        <p>The charge that she is an exacting and cruel mistress he flung aside, for the fumes of glory were already working (prospectively) in the brain. Then, despite a dire scarcity of cash among a/I concerned, Keith the author, the man of letters, the poet, the novelist, was, toasted hilariously by a company of his fellow-students. "Immortals," cried the chairman, inspired by liquor and enthusiasm, "sit yont, if you please; room for your betters." Yon may hear the tale yet from matter-of-fact, middle-aged men whose hilarity has loner departed, whose curly locks are scant and grizzled; and they never tell it without a kindling of the eye and a return of the spirit of youth. Not a soul of them doubted that at last the man had arisen who wa, to accomplish the oft-attempted, often-foiled, enterprise of setting the Thames on fire. At four in the morning one windy spring day, they cluttered about Keith, fighting for the last hand-grip. "Good-bye, old chap," .they cried • "in th- temple of Same don't forget old comrades." O'h, no, he wouldn't forget; if he did might the Muse. forget him, whereupon there was a roar which brought the watchman at the double. • Next day Keith was explaining the golden plait at home.</p>
        <p>"Is writing a money-snaking business, Walter?" asked his father, after listening attentively. "Money-making!" cried Walter. "Just listen " —and he ran over the names of populy favourites whose bankin&lt;, accounts were fabulous. His father owned he had never heard of them, but hoped "it would be-all right." "Oh, yes, it will be all right," chimed in Mrs. Keith, deftly hiding the wound in her heart. "Welter's got the learning and the ability—anci the ability, she repeated, proudly. *Everybody says that. And ye'll write good books, dearie " she pursued, turning to her son. "And not things ye'll wish ye hadn't written, when—when the great time comes. Keep that before ye, dearie." "Youiisp disappointed in me, mother," Walter cried, Alb a sudden pang at his heart. "Oh, no," she protested, wincing at her own insincerity. "I once thought of the pulpit, that's all. But it would be a sad thing to think one could not be a good man without taking tc preaching. • Ye'll write good books, dearie, and that'll please Him just as well." She looked at him yearningly out of moist eyes, and he kissed her, wiping her eyes dry. "I'll mind what, you say, mother," he murmured, unsteadily ; "always—always." Then she kissed him in turn, and her eyes Were moister than ever.</p>
        <p>In the gloaming he slipped out unperceived, leaving the old folks'in conference. "He wants to be alone to think," said Mrs. Keith when his absence was discovered,</p>
        <p>"Maybe," answered Mr. Keith, in a tone which made his wife glance at him curiously. A father tolerantly remembers his own youth; but a mother finds it hard to endure the straying of a son's affections. There was no word between them, however, of Walter lifting his eyes upon the daughters of men. ' Yet the sage lies it that a poet without love is a physical and metaphysical impossibility. Walter, not wholly unsuspected, was going the way of all true sons of the Muses. The love .songs which had been predicted came gushing—aye, even canticles 1 —while one Who was in her cradle when the pre. dictions were made crooned to herself with ineffable thrillings and tinglings. Boy and girl, Walter Keith and Nellie Moffat had played together, though he was four years her senior. Then he passed through the haughty phase in which the young savage disdains maiden- hood. That passed in turn and one day, or rather one soft June evening, in the trysting glen, a bosky Eden especially designed for tender outpourings, he discovered that he was violently and irrevocably in love. In a fever .of fear and joy Nellie owned she was in a like predicament. 'Whereepon with the appropriate rites they broke a silver coin in two, each taking half, and called themselves "pledged " for time and eternity. He was out now for the last stolen interview, the hilt kiss and vow to seal their troth before the resolute assault upon fortune. He reached the trysting-place breathless, and three minutes late, 'to find Nellie awaiting him by the darkling wood, and the young moon peeped shvly (wer the shoulder of a hill at their greeting. What it. was let lovers inisgine. lior tumultuous rapture of the meeting over, they arearged the future with the foresight of destiny itself. lie was to be a great writer, dnd she* meanwhile assistant teacher in the village school, was to stand by his side and share the ovations of London, and as they planned each could:feel the other trembling with joy. "What , pleases me. hest' of all, said Keith, taking her arm tighter in his, "is the thought of having you beside me in my own ccey study ; I writing, and you reading, criticising, approving what I have written. Nell, Nell, it's glorious." ! "And NVO'ii ifiit. care for the great receptions and t',ings after all," responded Nell, softly, for the I gra dsur of them had frightened her. "Oh, ye,t ran take them in too," rejoined lieith,l intrepidly. "I must chew you off, Nellie. They most: see what a pretty, clever, fascinating little wife I have, and if they attempt to honour me with( su honouring you, or put my portrait in itat papers without, yours---" He did not say what{ dire penalties would ensue because their faces got ' too close torether for speech. A drunken fiddler sitting in the shadows by the roadside afterwards swore he heard a long sibilant noise suggiqive of his own courting days; but that may be the fiction a man in liquor. The moon shone Out more .clearly, and they parted with sad, sweet, lingering farewells, the last word being that Walter was to take London by storm.</p>
        <p>Iris sierm muns consieted of a dozen lvriea. a tale Of his native village, prettily touched with senti• inent, a trenchant article on the need of fresh blood in lite:atute, and an immitigable faith in himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Queen Elizabeth's Latin.—Some people who read a paragraph in the newspapers concerning the Bishop of Rochester's residence, Sstis House, may have wondered how it came to receive such a name. The story is, that Queen Elizabeth stayed at the House, and that when , she left, the host thanking her for the honour of the visit, added many apologies for the inadequacy of the accommodation. The Queen, who rather prided herself upon her Latin, offered the somewhat ambiguous reply Satis " (enough). The Destruction of Selby Abbey.—Yorkshire is famous for its many historical abbeys, but next to York Minster, there is none it cofild so ill afford to lose as Selby Abbey, which was founded by William the Con- queror and has now been destroyed by fire. Like Win- chester Cathedral, and most of our ancient Norman buildings, it was at one time in danger from insuffi- cient foundations. Architects say that in the building of York Minster, and also Westminster Abbey, it was intended to crown the central towers with spires, but they could not he added because the foundations would not bear the weight. Selby Abbey had settled so that the pillars on the two sides were of different heights, but it was one of the most beautiful of our Abbey Churches, and the loss is irreparable considering its great antiquity. The Decline of Manners.—Lady Montagu of Beau- lieu has been complaining in one of the papers, of the incurably bad manners of Society. Polite speech and social manners, she seems to think, no longer exist, and nowhere is the R ant of courtesy more marked than at country house parties. There one meets with the rude, unpolished manners of commerce, and scarcely the form that would be expected in a third-rate hotel, if a story told in one of the Society papers may be taken as in any way representative of modern social life. This refers to rr so-called well-brought-up young lady, who was heard to call across the lunch table at a shooting lodge to a man with Wellingtonian features,' "Now th3n bird-faice pass the rabbit food." jlf the story is not apocryphal, it would be interesting to know what the astonished " bird-fake " had to say to such a request. There is, of cowse, danger in exaggeration, but there is no doubt much to justify the Society papers endorsement of Lady Montagu's complaint. Among the lower clauses, speech and social manners are steadily improving, but amongst the uppel-Zlasses, the tendency is in the opposite direction, at any rate as viewed by these who take their standards from the time when manners were regarded, in fashionable society as more important than riches. Gambling at Bridge.—ln America, the anti-gambling laws would appear to be far-reaching, and a new feature has been added to them by a hotess being fined for per- mitting bridge to be played at her house for money. Among her guests was a young girl, whose parents dis- approved of her playing bridge for money, and success- fully brought a prosecution against the hostess for encouraging gambling. Many, no doubt, who regard card playing as one of the vices of Society, wish that the same thing could be done over here, and, if it could be, it would certainly have some astonishing results. Bridge parties are a popular form of entertain- ment for winter evenings. and the game is nearly always played for some stake high or low. Unques- tionably a good deal of high play goes on in society and those hostesses who permit inexperienced girls to lose large sums of money are greatly to blame, but among the public generally, it may be doubted whether there is as much gambling at cards, as those who decry the decadence of the age believe. The Age to Marry.—Men and women marry later in life than was the case a generation or two back and most people will agree that the change must be counted to the credit of the growing wisdom of the age. It is said that the fashionable age for marrying is from twenty-seven, to thirty-two for women and thirty to forty-five for men. Nowadays the girl will not marry before she looks round, and sees what there is to be got out of life before she marries, and the man no longer marries in romantic ardour, hut waits until his settled habits are formed and considers whether he or his fiancee elect is the more selfish of the two. Max O'Rell said that a man should marry a woman half his age, plus seven, and that this rule applied equally to all ages, whether he is twenty or eighty. The general tendency however, is to marry later in life, and perhaps it is as well so, for although youthful ardour may leap at difficulties, it is always better to consider ways and means beforehand. Artificial Fruit.—Grapes of enormous size and impos- sible colouring are being piled upon many of the latest hats. In nearly all instances the quantity is overdone, and the effect makes for heaviness. Two or three huge bunches, which, at the fruiterers would weigh several pounds, sets one wondering how any head could be found to support such a creation though in reality all the best artificial fruit is but a "feather's weight " Grey, blue and brown grapes lead, the nateral colour being set aside for the present. About Caps.—During the past few months, the cap has been slowly but surely cieepicg into favour. First came the dainty scrap of muslin and Ince that was called a nightcap, then appeared the pretty little mob erection usually of sprigged net, of a soft creamy shade, with a ruffle of lace in front and a fall of the same behind, usually donned by convalescents, and the breakfast-lo- bed contingent and now we have arrived at the morning cap which is finding considerable favour among busy housewives, and girls who help about the house. Making beds, dusting rooms, and cooking, all mean a certain amount of dust, to which the girl or woman who takes a pride in her hair, maturally objects. It is for such busy people that the morning cap is intended, and a very fascinating little accessory it can be made to prove. Muslin is the fabric preferred, either plain or spotted, and the simple mob shape finished with two or three neat frills, is the correct style. Coloured muslins are perhaps to be preferred, to the white and cream varieties, but the shades chosen should not be too vivid Tartans and Stripes.—Stripes are modish again, and to all save the very tall eirl or woman they promise to prove more becoming than the 'checks and spots we have had with us so long. At present the stripes are of the narrowest but they will widen as the winter advances. The tartans this autumn have again proved to very many a delusion and a snare, and it seems doubt- ful whether the women of our Islands will ever learn the lesson Parisiennes mastered long ago, namely, that to be effective tartans must in the form of trimming be ' used sparingly and that when an entire costume of such fabric is contemplated it must be trusted only to a first-class dressmaker or tailor. A favourite Fabric.—Marquisette—a material</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>resemblingitke old-world grenadine—is having a great vogue for afternoon as well as evPning gowns. This new fabric promises to wear better than the gauzes we have had with us for several seasons past and is some- what lower in price. Weighted about the 12 cm and trimmed about the bodice with velvet bands, a mar- quisette gown should prove a success.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RHEUMATISM'S GRIP</head>
        <p>RHEUMATISM'S GRIP</p>
        <p>After Acute Influenza. An Athlete Helpless for Months, Could Scarcely Move a Limb. New Activity Gained, with Complete Return of Strength. Cured by</p>
        <p>Or. Williams' Pink Pills!! Even the strongest men are not exempt from sudden and agonizing attacks of rheumatism, as is proved by the experience of Mr. John Havelock, of 7, Chester d- street. South Shields, who is well-known in the North as a splendid all-round athlete. His prowess has won him numerous cups, and he was second in the great 12 stone Wrestling Championship open to all Northumber- land. "I am found of all athletic sports, and my s rength and energy have been remarked anon often," id Mr. Havelock to a Press man recently, "'but a change pidly came over me ; all the life seemed to leave my ody. I could get no rest at night, and in theemoruing felt so tired and worn out that it was only by an effort that , I could dress myself. " My appetite failed completely,and the least exertion , brought on profuse perspirations. These seemed to sap I every atom of strength, and from a healthy, vigorous man I became a wreck. " The doctor I consulted said I was suffering from a severe attack of influenza, and unless soon eured rheumatism would sent in. His words were only too 1 true, and in a few weeks my limbs were simply useless through rheumatism. The joints of my fluters and toes became contracted, with an inflamed swelling in my joints. Every movement of my body caused me intense pain ; it seemed as if my sinews were being torn out. If I attempted to move hand or foot whilst lying down I suffered most excraciating agony, and often screamed out in pain. "For months I lay prostrate and helpless, my nerves unstrung, and unable to bear the least sound. My mother did all she could for mei giving rile medicines and rubbing with embrocation, all without relief. My sufferings were so agonizing that death would have been welcome. . " Day by day I could feel myself growing weaker, and I bad given up all hope, when a very good DR WILL/ 3 ' friend recommended Dr. INK Williams' Pink Pills for ILLS Pale People. I decided /OR . to give them a fair trial. I, AL E , / I wasn't cured in a few 1 EOPLE. \ / weeks—my case was too had for that—but after 'taking a few boxes all after-effects of the influenza I vanished. I felt my appetite improving, and I could take solid food without vomiting or any after-pmgs. I thought this a good sign, and kept on with the pills. Surely enough, such an improvement followed that I could get up for breakfast. " I persevered with Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and the use of my limbs gradually came back until I became as strong and healthy as I had ever been. " When my friends heard that I had recovered and was working again they were dumbfounded. But there I was at work, and there I have been every day since, which is a sufficient guarantee of the permanence of my cure. I still take Dr. William's Pink Pills, and every dose seems to put new blood into my veins. I can undergo any work without fatigue; I have a splendid appetite, and enjoy meals. Truly Dr. Williams' Pink , Pills 'put me on my feet ' ; in fact, I owe my life to them." 1 Thousands of cured men and women speak from experience of benefits derived from Dr. Williams' Pink ' Pills for Pale People in cases of Anaimia, General Weakness, Indigestion, Eczema, Sciatica, Rheumatism, Pains in the Back, St. Vitus' Dance,Nouralgia, Nervous Disorders and Paralysis. Sold by dealers, but always I ask for the genuine pills (seven words as above) ; if in I doubt send to the British Dept, 46. Holborn-viaduct, London, 2s. Pd. for one box, or for six 13s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Stockton Heath Parochial Committee.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Parochial Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Com- mittee was held on Tuesday evening in the Hall Restaurant, when there were present : —Messrs. George Piling (chairman), Clarke, Pearson, Hallowe, Bouchier, Williams, Whitley, H. J. Westbrook, T. R. Westbrook, F. Warburton, J. Swinton (surveyor), and George F. Ashton (clerk to the Rural District Council). —A letter was read from Mr. Haddock (gas engineer to the Corporation), who having replied to complaints with reference to the lighting of lamps, said if the the Committee were not satisfied with the way in which the Corporation maintained the lamps at Stock- ton Heath the Corporation would prefer to supply them with the gas and allow them to maintAin the lamps themselves. They would be prepated to extend the main to Lumb Brook if they could get any extra consumotion from the people en route. If they were unable to get any consumption they would be prepared to extend the main and charge an extra £1 per annum for the lamp, until such times as consumption was increased on the main.—The matter was referred to the Lightlbg Committee.—lt was explained that the delay in the making of Warburton-street and other new streets was owing to the fact that the committee were waiting for the consent of the Local Government Board.—lt was decided to call the attention of the Ship Canal Company to the unsatisfactory state of the footpaths on the swing bridge, as they are occasion- ally flooded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>MY DEAR CMLnavi, This week I can announce the result uf the boys' competition. I am pleased to say L have received a great many drawings and paintings from my members, and I think you have all taken interest in this competition. The first prize I have awarded to Trenwill T. Wills, for his drawings, which he has entitled "His First Rat." I think this excellent and beau- tifully drawn. The second prize has been sent to Arthur :.icragg, for his drawing of a tiger, and the `third prize goes to Henry Mason, for a small landscape drawing. I hope you will all be very pleased with the nice books I have seat you, and I shall be pleased to hear what you think of these prizes. Next week Tshall be able to award the prizes to the girls. I have a great" many very pretty articles of needlework, but more about this next week. _</p>
        <p>I mentioned h short time back that I wanted you all to make. something for the poor this winter, and I shall be pleased to receive your contributions at any time. I will award prizes when anything really nice is sent, and I pro- pose that you should make such things as woollen scarfs, cuffs, petticoats, little pinafores, and socks. I hope everyone will send me something, not so much for the sake of a prize, but to please some poor child. Pach week the " C.C.L. " grows apace, and in this issue I have very great pleasure in introducing three new menibers, namely: Florence Bartlett, Bessie Parnell, Amy M. Hearn—all lady members, as you perceive. What are the' gentlemen up to by letting the ladies be so lonesome? A thought has just struck me—why 1. chestnut time, of course, and what boy would shut himself up indoors writing to Uncle Ned when he could be romp- ing about in the lanes after the nuts. I appre- ciate them, because I know that it's the bracing fresh air and exercise that produces sturdy and vigorous nephews and nieces. All three new members have opened the ball with a _contri- bution, which I have inserted below:—</p>
        <p>A LESSON IN LIFE. • A boy was once sitting on the steps of a house. Ho had a broom in one hand and a large piece of bread and butter in the other. While he was eating, he saw a poor little dog not far from him. He called out "Come here, poor fellow!" Seeing the boy eating the dog came 'near. The boy held out a piece of his bread and butter. As the dog stretched out his head to take it, the boy drew back his hand and hit him a hard rap on the nose. A gentleman who was looking from a window on the other side of the street, saw what the boy had done. Opening the street door, he called the boy over, at‘the same time holding sixpence between his finger and thumb. " Would you like this?" said the gentleman. "Yes, if you please, sir," said the boy, smiling. Just at that moment he got so severe a rap on the knuckles, from a cane which the gentle- man had behind him, that he cried out with pain. "What did you do that for'?" said he, making a long face, and rubbing his hand. "I didn't ask you for the sixpence." "What did you hurt that dog for just now?" asked the gentleman. "He didn't ask you for the bread and butter. As you served him I served you. Now, remember hereafter dogs can feel as well as boys." FLORENCE BARTLETT. And a little anecdote entitled:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WASN'T SELFISH.</head>
        <p>WASN'T SELFISH.</p>
        <p>Mother: "I want Bobby to take some medicine now. Will he take it like a good boy?"</p>
        <p>Bobby (aged five): "'Es, moiler, is It nice?" Mother: "Yes, very nice." Bobby Does oo like it, mother?" Mother &lt;making believe to sip it): "Yes, very much." Bobby: "Then oo d'ink it, mother, instead of me; l3obby'not selfish 'ittle boy." R _ESSIE PARNELL. The third is an original story-,- with the title of "HOW RONALD GOT HIS NEW BOAT." Mr. and Mrs. Fairchild and their two children, Roald and Maisie, had taken apart- ments at Hyde Park Farm, for their summer holidays. In the grounds belonging to the farm was a very large duck pond. One after- noon, Maisie, being tired of reading, espied her brother's little boat lying on the lawn, so, taking it up, she went to sail it in the pond. For a time she managed the little boat fairly well, then somehow or another, it got beyond her reach. Having a long stick with her, she stretched out over the bank to reach it. The bank being very slippery she fell into the water. Her brother Ronald, with some other boy friends, was playing cricket in an adjoin- ing field, when he heard a scream. On look- ing round to see where the sound came from, he saw his little sister struggling in the water. He immediately dropped his bat and ran to her assistance, and with some difficulty suc- ceeded in getting her out of the pond. On reaching the bank she told Ronald how it all happened, and he very soon forgave her for the loss of his little boat. On reaching/the farm she told her father and mother the story over again, and her mother was greatly dis- tressed to see what a state she was in but her father only laughed. After Maisie had come downstairs from changing her dirty and wet clothes, they all sat down to a merry tea. During the Meal Mr. Fairchild told Ronald of his intention of buying Eim another boat,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>_ . and better in every way than one he had lost. This delighted Maisie, as knew. Ronald was very fond of boating, Id if he could not have had another boat, would not have half enjoyed his holiday. is needless to add that Maisie never ait. nted to sail a boat again. 1 ea Y M\ AM.\llE.ta'N. tulnk I must close my letter for this</p>
        <p>;k3 with all</p>
        <p>id wishes from LJLNCLE :LC F.D.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—Having read the Children' Corner for a long time, I thought you would allow me to introduce myself into your circle of competitors. I am twelve years of age, and shall celebrate my thirteenth next Fehrmay. I have written a small story, composed com- pletely by myself, entitled "The Violets of Life.'.. ELSIE CONGEAT E. THE VIOLETS OF LIFE. Once upon a time the aged king of Orychus fell ill, and, all thought he would die. Only one thing could save him, and that was the scent of the violets of life. As he grew worse„ the country got into great disorder and riots• abounded everywhere. Now, many of his people wished him to die, and some were cruel enough to send him poisoned medicines, but happily, the king's daughter knew their elan to kill her father, and did away with tilee: before he partook of them. When the king heard this he was full of wrath, and anxious to get bettef, so that he might punish hi.; wicked subjects. At last, the, king said quit whoever could bring him the violets of lif! should have half the-kingdom and the hand ••:' his beautiful daughter in marriage. . stirred the people. The prospect of having L • the kingdom and the beautiful maiden tl ,oughly roused them, and many set off to t- 'the wonderfil medicine but all came ha despair, and without the violets of life: lie king also gave up hope, when one day a stranger appeared at the castle, and bega-cd see the king. He was shown into the • . chamber, whom he told that he alone c; ' get the violets of life, the scent of which v,-;e: : save him. The king, overjoyed at hear, this, asked the wonderful stranger 'his ac-.: tions, in reply to which the stranger asked f• r the hand of the beautiful princes. The Iziir,; readily promised him that, and after a feast prepared in his honour, the stranger set ttff en the expedition. All awaited his return great eagerness, the king especially: One c. the stranger appeared carrying "1071101'11i, , wrapped in a leaf. He was ushered inte king's chamber without ceremony, all b;;:.; eager to hear the reaults of the expeditio•,.. He gave the violets to the king, telling him to smell them strongly. The effect was wonder- ful, the king sat up, and rubbed his eyes, and then seemed to forget that he had ever been • ill. The princess canie in to thank the stranger, and at once consented to her Tila V- riage with the prince, who had saved hcr father's life, for he was a prince, but had disguised himself. The marriage was (Tie bra ted with the ringing of bells, after which they went to the prince's home, where they w.-re ; received with great rejoicings.</p>
        <p>THE DISOBEDIENT 11011 SE. One bright summer morning, a fat little mouse, Who lives in a hole at the back of our house. Washed her face and her whiskers, and brushed her brown hair. And said she wculd go and enjoy the fresh air. But before she went out, she called Jimmy and Jack-, • And told them to watch till their mother Cfl.lllo</p>
        <p>back. "Now, children," she said, "I'll be home in a</p>
        <p>trice, Do try to be good little dutiful mice. "-Don't squeak and don't scamper or kick up a riot, But run about softly and try to, be quiet: And when/ I conic back I will bring you some</p>
        <p>cheese, And then you may eat just as much as YOU</p>
        <p>please.' So she wilisked up her tail and pattered away, And Jimlily and Jacky were left to their play. But oh! my dear children, it grieves me to tell That these little mice did not act very. well. For as soon as their mother had fastened the</p>
        <p>door, • They squeaked and they scampered about on the floor, ran here and there, and fought with each other, Jack pulled the tail of his poor little brother. At last they grew breathless and tired with their play, And then only think what I heard Jacky say, "Corns, Jimmy," he said, "I am tired of this</p>
        <p>hOuse, It's not fit for me, a young gentleman mouse. "Come along, and creep under the dining-room</p>
        <p>• door, Perhaps we may find some sweet crumbs on</p>
        <p>the floor; But creep very softly for fear of the cat, You know she is often asleep on the mat." But Jimmy was quaking and shaking with fear, "Oh, no, brother Jacky, I'd rather stay here. That terribie cat I've seen only twice, But mother has told me she dines on young</p>
        <p>_ _ But poor silly Jacky would not do as told, You see he was growing so knowing and bold, He laughed at his brother, and ran through the door, But, poor little mouse, he was never seen more. NORAH HADDEN.</p>
      </div>
