      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.'</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.'</p>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES AND EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Naturally the Free Churches take more than an ordinary interest in the • hducation struggle which now after the summer lull . will be developing. • The question asked is Whether the House of Lords will uphold or reverse the West Riding decision now that the Government have decided to appeal. Free Churchmen of course, were . quite satisfied • with the West Riding decision, but while •it is felt that the Government could well let the matter rest there, there will be much advantage in taking the me to the highest Court and getting the final decision on the matter. As to what the decision of the House of Lords will be no one can say. Should they adopt the ruling of the lower Court, the point will, ise take it, still have to be decided as to whether the local authorities may, or must, refuse to pay for denominational teaching, so that we are apparently not yet in sight of the end of litigation. If the House of Lords re- verses the decision of the Court of Appeal, the matter will simply stand where people thought it stood since 1902, and the Government's Bill will ho the only' solution of the question. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FREE CHURCH CAMPAIGN</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH CAMPAIGN</p>
        <p>As to what the House of Lords in its legislative capacity will4lo to the Government's Education Bill, no one can say. Rumours of all kinds are current—simple rejection, drastic amendment and destructive postponement are all mentioned. The best thing to do is to wait, and then we shall see precisely what our hereditary peers will decide to do. In the meantime, as far as the Free Churches are concerned, there will be action. The National Free Church Council is carefully watching the issues at stake, and arrangements have been made to immediately summon an adjourned-meeting of the Birming- ham Assembly if it Should . be necessary. , November 5 has been tentatively fixed for this meeting- if it should be called, but in view of the • uncertainty as to when, and how, the House of Lords will deal with the Bill, arrangements are subject to alteration up till the last moment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>PROVINCIAL DEMONSTRATIONS.</head>
        <p>PROVINCIAL DEMONSTRATIONS.</p>
        <p>The National Free Church Council has already initiated an Education Campaign. The principal centres in England and Wales have been chosen for the holding of huge demonstra- tions to . explain the Free Church attitude on Educational matters: The first of these .de- monstrations was held at Derby on October 8. Mr. J. Compton Rickett, M.P., D.L., and Dr. Clifford being the principal speakers. Another great demonstration followed at Plymouth, when Dr. Clifford and Rev. Thomas Phillips, 8.A., were the speakers. Other meetings tire to be held at Brighton, when the President of the National Council, Rev. J. Scott Lidgett, is to speak together with Rev. Thomas Law; on November 8 and 9 at Sheffield and Hull re- spectively with the same gentlemen as speakers; Dr. Clifford will speak at Cardiff on Novembew9 with Rev. C. Silvester Horne, M.A. ; at New- castle-on-Tyno -on/November 12' with Rev. J. Hirst Hollowell; at Preston on November 13 with Rev. A. T. Guttery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>GIPSY MIMI IN AMERICA.</head>
        <p>GIPSY MIMI IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>News has been received at the hemignarters of the National Free Church Council that their missioner, Gipsy Smith, has started his Ameri- can Mission Campaign under auspicious circum- stances. He is -holding United Meetings in New York and proceeding from thence to Boston. Gipsy Smith is well known on the other side of the Atlantic, this being his sixth visit. He is accompanied by his wife and daughter.</p>
        <p>LOCALISING cinnwit mAGAzINES. What has now become an ordinary depart- ment of Church work is the running of a local. magazine by an individual Church or group of Churches. By this means the Church is able to communicate with its members and reach out- siders, and universal testimony points to the great value of this work. For the purposes of localising the National Free Church Council produces the li•ee Churchman, In issuing this magazine the aim is not only to attain purity an d excellency of contents, but special care is taken to give it a first place in attractiveness both in reading matter and illustrations, • An advance copy of the January issue is now ready, and it is admitted: on all hands that it even exceeds previous issues. To show the success and popnlarity of the Free Churchman the pub- lishers claim that during the last three or four years it has increased its circulation by no less than 100,000 copies per issue. This surely is a unique record. With the January number is included the programme and arranffements for 1907, and a splendid Art Gallery of portraits in colours well worthy of framing. The contri- butors for 1907 include rractically all the lead- ing Free Church speakers and writers, and the plogramme is thoroughly varied and interest- ing. All who are concerned With localising will do well to write for a specimen cony to the Publisher at the Memorial Hall, E.C.</p>
        <p>• A NEW FEEE cnt.Tr.m HYMNAL. dreat strides have been made in Free Church hymnology of recent years, several of the de- nominations having produced really tip-to-dato and excellent collections. The need has very constantly been felt for .a collection of hymns smaller than the •usual exhaustive denomi- national books, and suitable for such united gatherMgs as Free Church Councils, &amp;c. For some considerable time the National Free Church Council has had the midter of such a hymn-book in hand, and early in November "The Free Church Council Hymnal" is an- nounced to appdar. It is edited by the Rev. S. F. Collier, and combines strongly the Evan- gelistic and Devotional elements. There are some 300 hymns in the collection, and the price will range from a popnlar•edition at ld. to a larger type edition at 9d. net, With tune book from is. 6d. net upwards in various bindings.</p>
        <p>GIIILS' GUILD WORN.</p>
        <p>The Girls' Guild, which is under the auspices of the National Free Church - Council, is doing a good work in a quiet way all over the country. At a recent National Conference held at the Mem)rial" Hall representatives were present from 'various parts, and interesting accounts were given of Guild work in various conditions, such as in the villages and seaside towns, sitburban districts, and among factory girls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>YOUNG MEN'S INSTITUTE.i</head>
        <p>YOUNG MEN'S INSTITUTE.i</p>
        <p>Whilst the girl's life of the country is being looked after the young men are -Tfiat,being neg. lected. The first of what it is hoped to be a seriespf Free Church institutes has been opened in South London at 273, Lewisham High Road, under the auspices of the Deptford Free Church Council and the Young Free Churchmen's League. The Free Church Urotrile this month gives a well illustratea account of this splendid club for young men. •</p>
        <p>TFIE 'EPEE UHURCHES AND SCCIAL QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>One of the results of the increasing attention devoted to social work by the Free Churches is the publication early next month of a volume eltitled ." The Social Mission of the Church." The hook is intended to he an exposition of 'Free Church opinion on this important subject. At the request or the Social Questions Com- mittee of the National Free Church Council the Rev. C. Buser Waltem hss kindly edited the, velum). The contritiut( rs inolude such em'nent Flee Church leaders as Boys. J. Scott Liclgett, F. B. 'Meyer, Dr. ail-Ford. Dr. Horton, iDr. Brook. Dr. Townsend, Dr. Gibson, R. J. Camp- bell. Pro•essor Peake. etc. The volume will be published by the National Free Church Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MEALS BETWEEN MEALS.</head>
        <p>MEALS BETWEEN MEALS.</p>
        <p>Everyone knows that mild appetite between meals. You don't want to spoil the next meal by eating too much, but must have something. A little light, tasty snack is just what you want. A slice of Currant Bread (white or Hovis), cut thin, either plain or toasted, with butter or cheese, will satisfy you. There is no mom wholesome and nutritious food than Currant Bread.</p>
        <p>..,=.4.4.i.•-•:',."."'''r." ~,,A..4'..t...2.,:••:,4..%e'</p>
        <p>v</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOl .</p>
        <p>THE BARONESS BCIRDETT-COITITS.</p>
        <p>How many are aware that the Baroness Bur- dett-Coutts, who has lately been among the visi- tors to Ramsgate, was at one time seriousl/ dis- cussed as a second wife for the, great Duke of Wellington! Iyeed, Punch had a picture on the subject representing the Duke's statue outside Ansley House shaking hands with the Duke him- self. while underneath the legend ran, "So glad to hear the good news—don't forget to send me cake and gloves!" The Baroness, now in her ninety-third year. shares With the Earl of Cran- brook and Sir Andrew Lusk the distinction of having been born before the BattlOof Waterloo. Like them she haslilived in five reigns, viz., those of George 111. and IV., William IV., Queen Victoria, and his present Majesty. A girl 'of thirteen when Princess Victoria ascended the throne, the Baroness has had a singularly inter- esting life:.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LONG COATS AGAIN 'POPULAR.</head>
        <p>LONG COATS AGAIN 'POPULAR.</p>
        <p>The long coat bids fair to reinstate in our favour the complete dress, which, of late winters, has had to take a back scat in favour of blouses and skirts. Our sketch shews an afternoon or evening dress, which can be made all in one piece on a peNvrly boned and fitted lining, or can be treated s a pinafore costume. having the little bodice loose and worn over a blouse. The Idea of the straps over the shoulders, united by</p>
        <p>the two bows in front, is a dire‘lt copy of the Swedish national corselet, and the silver buckles on the belt are taken from Swedish models. Tle• frock is made in a very supple cashmere in a de- lightful shade of petunia, the strappings being of velvet in the sun.) shade. The under blouse, with its short, single-puffed sleeves, ie of tucked crêpe-de-chine in a. ,slightly lighter shade than the dress material.</p>
        <p>RIDING MAN'S FASHION.</p>
        <p>Lady Castlereagh is devoted to all kinds of outdoor spoils, and is a first-rate horsewoman, and the Gentlewoman hears that she is an advo- cate of women riding men's fashion for safety in the country. Her own very long shaped riding coat comes 60 low over the riding top boots and trousers that tlie whole arrangement in her case is really very workmanlike, . and no doubt it is a fashion , which, especially for little girls, is tak- ing on apace. Safety in the hunting field after all is the great desire:hum; and if the habit can be arranged .to look well it is still more likely .be gradually adopted by most well-known sportswomen •in the \ Shires. Lady Castlereagh has lately been photographed on horseback in the new riding dress, which in her case is mightily becoming and safe-icoking.</p>
        <p>ON DOWDINESS.</p>
        <p>Dowdiness is a disease, and a deadly one, says a writer in M.A.P. It has its chief seat in the head (inside and outside) and in the neck; and is also a result of our too faithful fondness for old and shabby gartnents. To escape it, one must avoid the Scylla of a badly-arranged neck and the Charybdis of an untidily, unfashionably coiffured head. A smart woman, a friend Of mine, has all her frocks made at home, and yet she is never a dowdy. And why? Because she knows how to put them on. Her hair ,is well done, her neck smartly swathed, and her veils, gloves, shoes, and boots are always fresh, new and perfect. Dowclies are born—not made: all undue humility has much to answer for, as the typical woman of no importance soon goes to pieces on the rock of clowclyism.</p>
