      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TREE PLANTING AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>TREE PLANTING AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE UP IN ARMS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Parker's Outburst.</p>
        <p>"This meeting having seen the result of the Parish Council deputation to the District Council with regard to the regula.tions as to the planting of trees, records its regret that such regulations have not been relaxed so as to assist the operations of the Tree Planting Com- mittee, especially as to the:requirement in respect to the width of footpaths—which practically stops all future planting—and hopes that the District Council may yet see its way to *educe the width to 6 feet, the width required by the Council's bye-laws." Such was the resolution which wal adopted by a unanimous vote at the annual meeting of the Stockton Heath Tree Planting Committee on Monday evening. There was a fairly large attendance, over which Mr. C. Plinston pre- sided. and those present were in hearty agreement with the scheme of the Committee, at least if any of its opponents were in attendance neither by vote nor voice did they show their disapproval of the resolutions which were adapted. At the outset some formal business bad to be dis- posed of and this was quickly aczomplished. A lengthy and able report on the year's work was presented by Mr. A. Fawcett who also submitted the financial state- ment. In this some of the items were worthy of note. The total income, with the exceptio-. of a few shillings bank interest, was accounted for by subscriptions and was £274 us. 9d., and after meting all expenses there was left a balance in hand of £l5 Hs. IN. Trees cost £72, but the principals item was for the iron guards which involved an expenditure of £95 Us. The actual planting cost £36 Gs. In view of the fact that the District Council has recommended the removal of the guards and the substitution of stakes the item came in for considerable criticism but it. was pointed out that the decision to provide the guards was not arrived at,without due constderation and that it had been greatly affected by the fact that the County Council only gave permission for trees to be planted on the main roads on condition that they were protect id by guards. Mr. Henry Hallard urged that a great deal of the opposition to the trees arose from tha fact that umbrellas, &amp;c., _had been damaged by contact with the guards and said he thought that if a little more discre- tion had been exercised by the committee in the matter of protecting the trees it would have obviated a great deal of the objections. .11r. H. J. Westbrook criticised the action of the Rnncorn Rural district Council in allowing the Com- mittee to spend £95 before they expressed their opposi- tion.</p>
        <p>The gift of a donation of £5 to the Stockton Heath Garden Society was also questioned, but the explora- tion that it was to assist in the encouragement of window boxes by providing plants. was accepted es satisfactory, and the reports were adopted. The election of officers was then proceeded with. The meeting was detained for a few minutes whilst Mr. Plinston ex- plained the harmoniors working of the committee in the past and emphasised the fact that so great had been the support which the inhabitants of the village had accorded to the movemenkthat the original scheme had proved totally inadequat4-,, They had to thank the County Council and the Parish Councils of Stockton IHeath b 4 Latchford Without for their courtesy and assistance in the work. Unfortunately another authority had not treated them in the way which had been anticipated. That authority had been approached and had sanctioned their pro- posals subject to the wishes of its surveyor being com- plied with, and that had been done, but, owing to the discordant cries raised by one or two individuals in the village, they had got a little across with the District Council. Mr. Plinston proceeded to speak as to the decision of the District Council "that in future no trees were to be planted on footpaths of a less width than ifeet." He argued that considering the amount of traffic in certain streets, six feet or even five feet foot- paths were ample. The cbairman at this stage began to hint darkly that. certain "Heathens "—no suggestion was offered which would lead to their identification— had not confined their opposition to oratorical activity but had resorted to less commendable methods. "It is extraordinary "he said" that more than half of the trees which we hale planted in Whitefield-road and Walton-road have died. I don't wish to insinuate but at the same time it looks fishy." The audience were in hearty agreement with this sentiment. Considerable amnsement was occasioned when Mr. Fletcher, a resident of Whitefleld-road, asked the chairman who he was throwing stones at? Mr. Plinston denied the impeachment, and said he was only stating a fact which required explaining. He also declined to accept the suggestion that the moving of the trees from their original positions accounted for their untimely end. The losses he said were altogether out of proportion, and he thought the residents of those two streets should form themselves into a Vigilance Committee, and if they found any trees being interfered with make an example of the offenders. - After this interlude, the business of the meeting was resumed, and Mr. Plinston' treasurer, and Mr. A. Faw- cett, secretary, ware re-elected without opposition. In answer to an enquiry, Mr. Fawcett gave the names of the gentlemen who constituted the committee, together with their attendances 4 Twenty-one meetings bad been held, and out of these Mr. Broady had attended 14, Mr. Edden 15, Mr. Moores 10, Mr. Plinston 16, Mr. Ryan 17, Mr. F. Timperley 13, Mr. W. A. Timperley 18, Mr. A. J. Willett 0, Mr. W. H. Woodcock 13, Mr. E. Vernon 10, Mr. J. W. Bouchier 6, Mr. J. H. Clarke 4, Mr. G. TandrlB, Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins 4, Mr. H. J. Westbrook 7, Mr. J. Hallows 18, Mr. W. Mclntyre 4, Mr. J. Mynekyme 12, Mr. F. Warburton 5, and Dr. Parker 11. It was decided that all those who had not attended at least one-third of the meetings should not be eligible for re-election, and it was explained that Mr. Ryan wished to retire. A suggestion was made that obis of the opponents of the scheme should be asked to serve on the Committee and whilst the Chairman said he was prepared to welcome even Mr. Whitley or Mr. Baxter the meeting declined to accept the proposal as serious and it was not put to the vote. The committee was then elected as follows : Messrs. Broady, Edden, Moores, Plinston' F. Timparley, W. A. Timparley, Woodcock, Vernon, Tandy, Hallows, Mynekyme, Dr. Parker, W. Hickmar, G. J. Brown, J. H. Fletcher, H. N. Houghton, E. Bindloss, W. Skelton, W. Mclntyre, and E. G. Wright. Mr. H. J. Westbrook though elected asked to withdraw and was allowed to do so. This concluded the formal business and expressions of opinion were intiited. There was a hush of expectancy as Dr. Parker rase to his feet to move the resolution given above. On the last occasion when be addressed himself to the question of tree planting he established a reputation of being a wit and on Monday he sustained it. Addressing his hearers" as children wrapped in the swaddling clothes of Dutton," he said he wished to sup- port them their determination to early tree plantinF to its triumphyt conclusion and to say" Hands off ' to all foreign interference. " Pull well I know," he con- tinued, " that had it been a crime to have been a party with the Tree planting Committee in what they have done for the village ; if our judges had been born and bred at Dutton, and the penalty proportionate to the crime in their eyes, this poor body of mine would long ago have dangled from a gibbett and this head have perched upon a pike to point a moral or adorn a tale." Dr. Parker warmed down to his work and having urged that the real judges—the inhabitants of the village— had pronounced their verdict by the hearty' enthusiasm with which they bad supported the committee, pro- ceeded to flights of poetical imaginings. "Behold," he said, " the avenue in Grappenhall-road which will rival the Paris boulevards." Whitefield-road he desctibed as assuming the aspect of a sylvan glade and Ellesmere- road and Fairfield-road he regarded as likely to afford a I sight which would equal any in the length and breath of England. The audience were hugely amused but they roared with laughter when the doctor expressed his delight at their having" like father Adam and his spouse clothed themselves with leaves rather than expose their nakedness any longer." Dr. Parker, after this burst of eloquence, proceeded to deal with the opposition,maintaining that none of the gentlemen who spoke against tho scheme could adduce a single instance where the trees were an obstruction. Mr. Hickman followed in a somewhat similar strain. He argued that for the District Council to be consist. ent in their determination to prevent obstructions they must remove the tramway standards. From this stage much of the humour disappeared from the proceedings. Mr. Pendlebury suggested that the Rural District Council having made the stipulation with regard to 7 feet parapets, ought to be asked to take steps to secure that width. (Hear, bear.) The Chairmaa explained that a resolution to that effect had been forwarded to the Parish Council by the Tree Planting Committee. Mr. E. Bradshaw, clerk to the Parish Council, denied baying received the missive and it transpired that though the resolution had been approved by the Committee it had not been forwarded. Information as to the decision of the District</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>You cannot possibly have a better Cocoa than</head>
        <p>You cannot possibly have a better Cocoa than</p>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>You will find it the very Cocoa you want.</p>
        <p>Council was sought by several of those present and Mr. Bouchier, chairman of the Parish Council, said that f whilst not at liberty to communicate the resolutions I they were, substantially, what he had previously stated. Tne tree; on the North side of Whit ;field- road would have to come up and the r. me fat-) probably awaited every alternate tree on the South side. With regard to the order that no trc 3S were to be placed on footpaths less than seven feet in width, Mr. Bouchier explained that the whole of the by: laws were under consideration, and it might be possible to incorporate a seven feat limit for foot r ,ths. He next referred to the difficulty as to the removal of the tr -s stipulated by the District Council. Tne Parish Coracil could not do the work, the Parochiro Committee would not, and the Tree Planting Commits- -e 1-11 no legal liability, and iie therefore thought the best thing they could do would be to adopt an attitude of pa iver, ist- once. The policy of doing nothing would perhaps help them out of the difficulty. Mr. H. J. Westbrook pointed out that under existing bye-laws not a single street to be constructed 'a the future would have seven feet footpath Mr. Westbrook also advised the policy of waiting, at least until after the March e,ections, when they could show that they -ally meant to have trees on lift. foot- paths. The District Council delegated such imporl'ant powers as street lighting, street making and super- vision of san.tary arrangements to the Parochifo Com- mittee and yet declined to allow them to decide such a trivial matter as tree planting. If the District , Council persisted in the decision he, after the election whould have a comae to suggest whic t world make the Council do the whole of the work themselves or else allow the Parochial Committee to do it in their own way. Mr. Fawcett asked if the residents of Whitefield-road were going to remain quiescent whilst the trees were pulled up. One gentleman ventured to assert that he was not but the other residents in the " sylvan glade " (adopting Dr. Parker's title) remained discreetly silent Mr. G. J. Brown also urged a policy of mauerful iractivity. They had not done r •v.bing to ingratiate themselves with the District Council and it seemed to him that they had therefore, for the time being, better let well alone. After the March election they could sea how opinion was going. Mr. H. J. Westbrook thought it little use trying to ingratiate themselves at Dutton. He was positively " slimy " when he went before the Council but it did not work.</p>
        <p>IMr. Woodcock asked what they 'atended to do when the District Council was pulling up the trees and Mr. Westbrook expressively replied " We shall be there." Dr. Parker's motion wr "approved. Mr. Woodcock then moved :—" That this meeting intimates to the District Council that the inhabitants of Stockton Heath, and particularly of Whitefield-road, object to any trees being moved " At this stage there was a minor difference of opinion between Mr.We3tbrook and Mr. Bouchier. The first gentleman urged that the resolution was not necessary, land expressed his belief that the trees were not to be removed. The Chairman of the Parish Council, how- ever, took the opposite view, and as the meting agreed I with him the motion was approved. The meeting was then adjourned for a fortnight. In his annual report Mr. Fawcett stated that in all over .C 270 was subscribed by about 4: ) persons irrespec- tive of the amount raised by some 300 school children, and at the close of the seasan 393 tress had been plar.ted. It was with regret that the committe after- wards learned that a small minority of the inhabitants did not approve of the scheme, their opposition being largely fostered by an r ihappy newspaper correspon- dence, and carried to such lengths that the Rural Council interposed. The final action of that authority was awaited with intsrest, but the committ: thought it could not run counter to the evident wish of the Parish Council Dad the majority of the in',abitaats. (Applause.) They hoped everything would be done to foster the movement for the beautifying of Stockton Heath and that the work so well begun might be con- tinued from year to year marked by sven greater enthusiasm. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PAROCHIAL CONIAIITrEEIS PROPOSALS.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL CONIAIITrEEIS PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Stockton Heath Pf-t,r-o-c-hia7l-Com- mittee, held on Tuesday evening, Mr. George Pilling in the chair, Mr. Bouchier asked for information with reference to the position of the Surveyor (Mr. Swinton) with regard to the planting of trees. The Clerk explained that the District Council had passed a resolution to the effect that the trees could be planted if they were placed tojhe satisfaction of the Surveyor. Mr. H. J. Westbrook: It is thee, a question of fact whether the Surveyor has passed them. Proceeding Mr. H. J. Westbrook criticised the recommendations of the District Council, with respect to the planting of trees. The resolutions were: (1) That, except by the special sanction of the District Council. no tre2s be planted in future upon parapets of a less width than seven feet, but that, subject to the approval (of the undermentioned Sub-committee, the planting of trees in Cawdor-street be completed. (2) That subject to the approval of the said Sub-committee, and to dangerous points being made less dangerous, ' tree guards in wide open spaces be allowed to remain. (3) That with a view to giving the utmost possible space for foot passengers, all trees which can with safety be moved nearer to the edge of the pappet be so moved, and all tree guards, with the exception of those referred to in recommendation No. 2, be removed; end stakes, set in line with the street, be substituted therefor. (4) That, with tee exception of such trre or trees, as the said Sub-committee consder may be safely left or placed in altered positions, all the trees on the north side of Whitefleld-road be removed. (5) That, subject to the conditions set out in recommendation No. 3, all the trees in Fairfield-road be allowed to remain, (6) That Messrs. Pilling, Baxter, and \Vhitley be appointed a Sub-2ommittea to early out the above recommendations in conjunction with the Parish Council of Stockton Heath. Having criticised the constitution of the sub-committee, Mr. Westbrook raid it would appear that the sub-committee were to do all the dis- cretionary work and that the Parish Council were not to have a voice. Mr. Ashton explaine that it was intended that the Parish Council should have a voice.</p>
        <p>_ As, however, there was a difference of opinion in the Committee on the question it was resolved " boat the Rural District Couneg be asked to allow the Parish Council to nominate three members to serve with the sub-committe3 to regulate not only the trees which have been planted but which may -be plant)d in the future."</p>
        <p>An amendment by Mr. Clarke thst three members of the Parochial Committes be nlso nomirsted, was negatived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>INDIGESTION AND HEADACHES. ---- Young Lady's Acute Suffering Ended and Health Restored by Bile Beans.</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION AND HEADACHES. ---- Young Lady's Acute Suffering Ended and Health Restored by Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>__— Headaches and indigestion are inseparable partners. If ;our stomach is in perfect order your head will be clear and bright, but when things go wrong with the digestion dull, heavy headaches are inevitable. The surest and safest cure for headaches is Bile Beans for Biliousness; surest because they remo 70 toe cause of the trouble, and safest because they are compounded only of natural herbs, roots, and barks. Miss Christy Monk, who lives at Benbecula, a little island in the Outer Hebrides peopled by about a thous- and hardy fisherfoll-s and stock-raisers, says:—" Some time ago I began to suffer from headaches and pains in the chest. Always after eating I was tormented with pain, until I became afraid to take any food. At times my head was so bad that I had to keep to my bed all day.</p>
        <p>"I went to ever so many doctors, but none of them did me any good. One doctor advised me to go home and have a long rest. I did so, but still I didn't get well. Then I was persuaded to try Bile Bears. I bought a box of Beans and commenced to take them straight away. The pain in my head loft me, and I could eat without feeling those awful pains afterwards. I continued with Bile Beans until I was quite cured." Don't be without your box of Bile Beans ; they are always useful when you feel out of sorts or run down. All chemists stock them at Is. 11d. per box, or 2s. 91 for box containing three times the quantity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>" Excelsior " Bazaar.</head>
        <p>" Excelsior " Bazaar.</p>
        <p>The concluding day of the " Excelsior" Bazaar, in connection with the Methodist Free Church, Buckley- street, was witnessed on Saturday, when the opening ceremony was performed by Miss Bessie Dutton, the proceedings being presided over by Mr. John Taylor. —The Chairman congratdlated the Buckley-street friends upon the efforts they were putting forth hi order to wipe out the debt on their church.—Miss Dutton, in declaring the sale open, congratulated all. upon their work in connection with that bazaar. She was sure they would have blessing in their work because they were looking to God and denying self, knowing that the reaping time would come. (Applause.)—On the proposition of Xfr. Plant, seconded by Mr. Pester, the Chairman and Miss Dutton were cordially thanked for their senices.—The day's busi- ness was then commenced, and a sum of £5O was ultimately realized. This added to the 1,60, the result of the first day's sale, dna raised on the second day, and .F.,30 from coupons made the total proceeds .-EllO. NATIONAL FREE CHURCH. COUNCIL AND T FIE CONGO. Rev. Thomas Law the Secretary of the National Free Church Council,has forwarded an urgent letter wall the local Free Crairch Councils in the Kingdom to direct public attention to the terrible shte of things on Ole Congo. The letter points out that this is more than a Free Church question and suggests the calling of a "town's meeting "by the Mayor of each borouk;h,where possible. In response to the appeal action is being taken already, by many towns. It is probable that at an early date a strong deputation will wait upon the Foreign Secretary Sir Edward Grey on this question. It is felt that the Congo atrocities are as serious if not more serious than the Boulgariann atrocities of years ago and that national enthusiasm ought to be roused on the question.</p>
