      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MR. HAVELOCK WILSON, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR. HAVELOCK WILSON, M.P.</p>
        <p>Receiving Order Made.</p>
        <p>Tuesday night's"Gazette"states that a receiving order under the Bankruptcy Acts has been made against Mr. J. Havelock Wilson, of Maritime Hall, West India Dock-road, and now or late of 44. Fairmount-road, Brix- ton, London, secretary and member of Parliament. The date of the filing of the petition is given as July 30 and the date of the receiving order August 23, on a credi- tor's petition. Mr. Wilson attributes the present state of his affairs to the enforcement of an old judgment obtained against him by an official of the Shipping Federation for issuing a circnlar that was held to be libellous during a trade dispute. The movement which was started a few weal's- ago by Mr. Wilson's friends with the object of raising the money, and so preventing him from losing his seat in Parliament. has so far made good progress as nearly £5OO of the £54.) required has been raised. In conversation with a press man 111 r. Wilson says : " I have to meet my creditors on December the 2nd, and what follows must depend upon any arrangement then made with regard to the settlement of their claims. My total liabilities amount to about £l,lOO. I under- stand that about £503 has already been subscribed by friends of mine towards the fund, and I am told that further übscriptions are promised. No private arrange- ment is now possible, and the matter must be settled through the Bankruptcy Court. If lam unable to come to an arrangement with my creditors on December the 2ud, I would be declared bankrupt, and in that case would at once lose the right to vote in Parliament, although I might still attend and address the House of Commons, and the seat need not be vacated for six months afterwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>HOW TO CURE,A COUGH. ---- The Dangers of Cough Mixtures.</head>
        <p>HOW TO CURE,A COUGH. ---- The Dangers of Cough Mixtures.</p>
        <p>A cough is rather a symptom than an ailment. It may be anything from the peculiar cough of a child suffering from whooping cough to the hacking cough of the consumptive. If the cough is associated with pains and tightness about the chest.and much fever following upon exposure to cold, we may suspect pleurisy or pneumonia. Sudden difficulty in breathing in associa- tion with a cough may he evidence of asthma ; while long fits of coughing that are preceded by "tickling in the throat," huskiness, and tightness in the region of the breast bone, indicates one of the worst forms of bronchitis. Bronchitis in a milder form gives the nasty irritating little cough that distracts attention in church ' and in a public meeting. There is little or no expectora- tion with the throat cough, but the harassing trouble continues from week to week. It is easy to understand why so many "cough mix- tures " fail to cure, when they are merely swallowed into the stomach an organ that has no direct connec- tion with the larnyx and lungs. Obviously the only method of reaching the tissues which are the seat of the ordinary cough is by inhalation. The Peps treatment,. consistinr,' of a few Peps crushed in the month or merely dissolved on the tongue, as the rich pine fumes are breathed into the system, affords most striking relief. Peps produce an agreeable gargle for the throat, and give rise to a most grateful, soothing sensation. They ease the throat, assist in expelling any irritating sub- stances or accumulation of mucus, and extend their healing influence to the furthest corner of the respira- tory organs. A still werse feature of liquid "cough mixtures "and "bronchitis cures " than their failure to reach the actual tissues affected in lung trouble is the amount of opium and other dangerous narcotics they often con- tain. Pep 3 contain neither opium, cbloral, nor any other other nerve-wrecking drug. Peps are equally free from the impurities which are present in medicated gums, and none of the irritative substances (such as ammonia-compoinds, tartar-emetic, cubebs), which occur in many cough-cures and lozenges. The most delicate lady and the youngest child will find nothing in Peps repulsive either to palate or stomach. Peps are a doctor" in themselves, end no mother should fail -to administer them to herself and her children upon the first sign of wheezing, sneezing, tightness about the chest, chill, or nasty coughing. They bathe the throat and lungs in their soothing and healing influence, destroy the infectious germs that are breathed in, and they promote health as no ordinary cough or cold medicine ever claims to do. Peps are equal to a "Pine Forest in every home." A box of Peps daintily packed, may be had of all chemists and stores for Is. lid., or post free for same price ditect from the Peps Pastille Co., Canton Hill, Leeds.</p>
      </div>
