        <p>105 Suites of Bedroom Forni- tura In oak, mahogany. and walnut, br the beet linglish makers. 150 Pairs of Washstands and Dressing Tables with marble slabs and back, swing bevel plate Toilet glasses ot the latest designs and finish. 15 Chests ot Drawers in waiut and mahogany. 65 Sideboards In mahogany walnut and oak. 30 Telescope Dining Tables In ezes from 611., Btt , and 141 t, long, with massive legs and loose leaves, in walnut and oak and mahogany. 100 Kitehen Tables. 20 Kitchen Dressers with sycamore tops, 630 Kitchen Chairs. 700 Bedroom Cane-bottomed Chair. 1400 American and Austrian Bentwood Chairs. 153 flocking &amp; Arm Chairs, Onnmode Chairs, arc. 150 Taproom Stools, Bent- wood and Piano Stools. 200 Suites of Dining-room, Drawing-room Sitting- room. 8; Parlour Furni - tore In Sheraton, Chip- pendale, antique, and modern styles uphol- stered in morocco, roan, leather, saddle bags, velvet, silk. tapestry, and hair seating. 90 Cabinets iu mahogany, Chippendale, 8r walnut. 105 Pier Glasses A Chimney Glasses. 36 Brass Bedsteads, French aud Psrislan. 250 Iron and Erase Bed- Mads. 20 Wood Bedsteads in Sheraton and Chippen- dale styles, also oak, walnut, and mahogany. 500 Wire Mattresses and Hair Mattresses. 100 Wool Mattresses and Feather Beds. 160 Wool Flock Beds Bolsters. Pillows,,to.,&amp;c. 250 Overmantels with bevelled plates, in mahogany. oak, and walnut, !,herat on and Chippendale patterns.</p>
        <p>Such a discussion as that THE PLURAL which occupied almost the VOTING BILL, whole time of the House of Commons on Wednesday is almost wholly unfruitful, and yet it has its distinct uses because it illustrates in a rather striking way what are the essential differences between parties. The depleted Opposition was contending f )r hours on end, in every variety of manner, and with every possible ingenuity of device, for the retention of class privileges. What the Government intends is to establish the principle of one man one vote. The Opposition does not meet the proposal Point blank. One of the characteristics of the enormous change in the point of view which has been brought about by the general election is the now timidity of what was only a few months ago a most bluster- ing and self-confident patty. The idea really has struck root that the country has expressed its mind on pending political questions in a manner and with a decisiveness that are at least entitled to lip-service, and to a formal respect. The great issues are not to be made the battlefield of groat I convictions. The Conservative party has no great convictions left, It expends a not inconsiderable energy in feints, in pretences, and in what we feel sadly compelled to call dodges. To waste time in a belated Session, when almost everybody would rather be anywhere else than at Westminster,seems wildly unreasonable, and yet this is what the little party occasionally led by Mr. BALFOVR it 3</p>
        <p>The Investigation Corn- WHO TOLD? mittee has laboured hard and brought forth nothing. It is still a public mystery how the garbled version of Counsel's opinion on the Edelsten action was conveyed to our contemporary the "Guardian." The newspaper will naturally not tell, and the evil-doer sits tight like Cronge at Pardeburg and says " nuffin." All the Committee can do is to deprecate the giving of information in a haphazard way by members of the Council and suggest that all press communications go through the head officials of each department. There is much wisdom but little practical common-sense in the suggestion. Most information already comes from official sources, and with regard to the members of the Council themselves all that can be done is to trust to their sense of honour and , discretion. The most hopeful circumstance is that itilh the betrayer of Corporation secrets will have had a sufficiently a.zvefe fright to deter him from such . . , reprehensible conduct in the future. ---- ' The Library Committee had CAHNEGIE's Cam almost given up all hope or CoNIING. receiving a grant from ]ft Andrew Carnegie, but the truth of the saying " everything comes to him. who waits" has been exemplified once more. Mr. Carnegie has offered £3,500 to builill a branch library on condition that the Council provide e a site and impose an additiona i l rate of id. n the E for maintenance. The committee has accepted the offer provisionally, and we shall probably have more information as to the Council's intention at the meeting on Tuesday next. Opinions have changed somewhat during the last two Mr. Carnegie e ill-gotten g years and there does not seem to be quite as much objection to good to account as there was at the time of Councillor Wright's first outburst. Local authori- oati ties all over the country have been chtmouring for aid from the purse of the Pittsburg millionaire, and seldom have they gone empty away. Hope rose again in Warrington when the Bold-street Wesleyans received over £4OO for the new organ fund,and the offer has come at last. Though we did not approve of Wartington's application in the first place, precedent has modified our views. Mr. Carnegie's millions have to be distributed somehow, and if the manner of their accumulation was not altogether estimable in English eyes perhaps the nature of their disbursement may atone for much. Now that the ice has been broken, we see no reason why the Infirmary Governors should not try to get a " thousand " or two for the extension of their noble institution. If in the making of millions Mr. Carnegie caused pain it is only fitting that he should now pay for its alleviation.</p>
        <p>Few subjects can be more THE RISE OF fascinating to political DEMOCRACY. thinkers than the rise of democracy, but dealt with by such an earnest and able exponent as the Rev. C. Harvey Cook, it becomes exceptionally enthral- ling. The members of the Junior Liberal Associ- ation have reason to congratulate themselves on being privileged to hear the powerful and ' informing address which Mr. Cook delivered at their meeting on Monday. The lecturer evidenced a complete mastery of his theme and showed that democracy—the rule of the people and not of any particular section—had always been opposed to privilege. He sketched the more striking epochs of democratic progress in the great nations from the days when Rome and Greece were in their glory, pointing out that our modern repre- sentative government was but the natural develop- ment of the primitive systems. Mr. Cook is a man of ideals. His does not object to being called a visionary—in fact he rather glories in the designa- tion, recognizing that idealists always prepare tne way for the practical reformer. Toe vision he is at present cherishing is of a time when govern- ment "by all for all" will be the guiding principle of our national life and, presumably, party strife will be no more. The realization of the dream must be very remote though we are certainly making headway on the lines of recognizing the rights and duties of all classes of the community. By making the best use of our party systern,which has its undeniable advantages, the masses can give effect to their aspirations and it is an encouraging sign that men like Mr. Harvey Cook are to be found on the pro- gressive 'side, ready to help on measures of social amelioration and progress..</p>
