      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>doing at present. It will even repeat a bad joke, as it did on Wednesdayf in order to delay the pro- gress of a measure which, if it did not believe in its reasonableness, and if did not believe to even a greater extent in the public opinion by which it is supported, it would be eager to hand over to tender mercies of the House of Lords. Somebody "spied strangers " again. The bulk of us believed that the "spying" of strangers was an obsolete pastime, but nothing, it appears, is obsolete to an obsolete party. What was a poor comedy on Tuesday was repeated on Wednesday in the degraded character of a farce. This is all by way of converting the progress of the Plural Voters' Bill into a Parliamentary obstacle race. There is no belief that the Bill will not get through, or that it will be very materially amended, but there is a most hearty dislike to it. The measure is one that will make a very great change in the character of Parlia- mentary representation, and to the Conservative party it must necessarily be a disagreeable change. The Bill will make all the difference between the representation of the interests and the representation of the people. But this, surely, is a reason why it should be fought on principle and not merely by subterfuge. Yet it is something to obtain from men like Mr. BALFOUR and Sir EDWARD CARSON, and, indeed, the rank and file of the Conservative party in the House of Commons, the practical admission that plural voting can no longer be defended on its merits. The discussions of Wednesday can hardly be called discussions, for the great bulk of the speeches came from the Tory benches, and all tended to the making of exceptions. Mr. Baisona, fresh from being feted at Manchester where he was so badly defeated at the General Election, adduced the case of the Manchester merchant who lives outside the ciby,, and trans- acts all his business within it. Why, asks the late PRIME MINISTER, should he not vote in two constituencies when he has such vital interests in each ? Mr. AsourTies reply is complete ; it is that it is impossible to devise tests which would meet such a case,even if the principle were a sound one. The Government proved its extreme willing- ness to meet real cases of hardship. Sir EDWARD CARSON showed that a man qualified to vote in two constituencies might under certain circum- stances, lose the privilege of voting in either if he were not prepared to tell an untruth to a voting clerk. Mr. ASQUITH acknowledged the difficulty and the possible wrong, and promised to provide against it. Such was the total harvest of a day's work.</p>
      </div>
