      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington Ragged School and Mission. The second of a series of lantern services was given at the above place on Sunday evening and was very well attended. Mr. S. Gough was the speaker and the pictures were clearly described by him. —The " Chil- dren's Home" a song illustrated by slides was well ren- _ .</p>
        <p>dered by Mr. W. Wright. Personal. Lord Lathom was unable to preside at the Pro- vincial Grand Lodge of Mark Master Masons of Lanca shire at Blackburn on Saturday, owing to an attack of influenza, and Colonel Murray (Wigan) was installed Deputy Grand Master and invested the Grand officers. Bro. William Pierpoint, of Warrington, was appointed J. W.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Whether tobacco, books, or soap, the thing is the same. If the trust system ever gets firmly planted in England, as sure as night succeeds day we shall get Socialism.—" Observer."</p>
        <p>Local horseowners are requested to beware of Pink-eye, which has broken out in Lancashire. The danger is understood to menace their equine servants and not themselves. * * Victoria Park is developing, and permission has been given for the early provision of a bell, a clock, and a gate to the bowling green. By and by a bridge • over the river to Howley, we hope. * * * Lovely-lane and Green-street may:, perhaps some day prove worthy of their euphemistic titles. In both these thoroughfares hanging baskets are to be ,Dlaced on the lamp-posts by the Beautiful Warrington oociety and maintained by the Corporation head- gardener. . .</p>
        <p>• PUBLIC BENEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. 1 A Rare Plant. ' A very rare and unique plant has been discovered by a member of the Warrington Field Club, Mr. Frederick Fuller, of Latchford, growing in the neigh- bourhood. The plant—the hairy rupture-wort, herni- aria hirsuta, has only been found twice elsewhere in Britain, at Coventry and Christchurch, Hants. A specimen has been added to the local herbarium in the Museum. " Y " Branch B.W.T.A.</p>
        <p>In open meeting of the above society was held at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Wednesday evening. Mrs. Moores took the chair and,after a short address, asked Miss Fieldhouse for e song. Miss Trirton accom- panied her. Mrs. Moores called on the Rev. J. Yonge, who spoke a few words of encouragement to the mem- bers. He then read various selections from the poets, varying from grave to gay, which greatly delighted his audience. The meeting closed with the usual vote of thanks. Stockton Heath Rates Reduced. The overseers of Stockton Heath are pleased to announce a reduction of 2d. in the rate for the half- year, amounting to 4d. in the year. There have been similar reductions in the two previous half-years, making a total reduction of 6d. in eighteen months. J, R. WOOD Piano Dealer and Tuner, 47, Old London-road.: Stockton Heath.—Advt. The Infirmary Extension. At a meeting of the Board of Management of the Warrington Infirmary held yesterday afternoon under the presidency of the Rev. H. V. Pigot, it was decided to accept the recommendation of the Extension Com- mittee, advising the acceptance of Mr. C. W. Daven- port's tender for the erection of the new pavilion and nurses' home. We are informed that Mr. Davenport's tender was the lowest sent in. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good Boors. Advt.— Gipsy Wedding at the Parish Church. . A gipsy wedding which took place at the Parish Church on Monday afternoon attracted a large number of spectators. The wedding party were due to arrive at two o'clock, but they were early on the sceno ,having walked from the camp at Howley, dressed in their best with the usual gay colours with white chrysan- themums pinned to their breasts. The names of the happy couple were Edgar Lovell (21), and Mary Ann Dorsey (19). The Rector performed the ceremony. After the ceremony was over, the bells rang out a merry peal while the bride and bridegroom walked arm-in-arm, with several others of their company back to the camp. Warrington P.S.A. Society. There was a good attendance at the weekly meeting of the Warrington P.S.A.. Society on Sunday when Mr P. Green presided. Mr. John Gill's address dealt with some of the dangers that beset young men. He deprecated the carelessness which existed respecting spiritual things. He also warned all present against the tendency so prevalent these days to simply drift down the stream of life allowing circumstances to carry them along instead of each man being deter- mined to overcome circumstances and live the life God would have him live. —Hr. Percy Deady rendered a solo, Councillor Clegg reading the scripture lesson. At the close the President (Mr. E. Wynne Jones) extended a welcome to 12 new members. Shorthand Writers' Association. The weekly meeting of the above was held at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Wednesday night last, there a being an excellent attendance. A.fter speed practice, * * a Eleven suffragettes have gleefully chosen martyr- Miss Connolly ably read the Shorthand Federation's dona in one of his Majesty's prisons this week ratfier ' L naf idol " which was listened to with than find surety to keep the peace. After attemptima lecture on ' o 02. v , great attention. The poet's simple, but touching and to raid the House of Commons and creating a lively soul-inspiring vwle‘rve dctommented upon, and in the discussion whers ich es f ol o e _he reading of the paper, disturbance, they roused the echoes at Westminster it was generally acknowledged that the author of "The Police Court, \vivre there was a magistrate deter- Psalm of Life m a ined on enforcing the law. Most of the ladies twice . " and other well known poems, had underwent the thrilling; and romantic experience of voctleiaromfinthgasnlkmsplicity of style peculiarly his own. A was accorded to the Reader. The being carried out bodily in the arms of staltaart City ,- President o f the Association, Mr. G. J. Brown,F I.P.S , Policemen. It is whispered that the men in blue would is down for next week's paper, his subject be