      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</p>
        <p>Knowsley to the workhouse, and he saw the motor-waggon and deceased coming along by I the side of it. He sounded the horn about a hundred yards away from deceased. He blew it four times, but he did not take any notice, and when witness found he was getting too near to him he shouted and put on the brakes. Deceased never looked up. Witness drove the car right up to the kerbstone, but he could not drive it on the kerb. Powell was riding two and a half or three yards from the motor-waggon, on his wrong side, but if he had looked up he cauld have easily steered between the motor-waggon and the car.—By Mr. Tyrer : He could have pulled up in three or four yards at the speed he was travelling at the time of the accident.—Mr. Tyrer : Of course, if you had pulled up altogether. the accident would not have hapoened.—Witness : If I had pulled up altogether he would have come straight into me, because he did not seem to know where he was.— Dr. Haslam said Powell was suffering from a com- pound and depressed fracture of the skull. The brake had caught him right in the middle of the forehead. It was almost equivalent to pole-axing, only not quite so severe.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and attached no blame to Dr. Mercer.—Mr. Swift, on behalf of Dr. Mercer, expressed the deepest regret that the accident should have happened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CANDIDATE FOR LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. F. Clarke, clerk to the Newton-in-Makerdeld Education Sub-committee and member of the Newton Urban Council, and who as a chartered accountant has an office in Liverpool, is a candidate fora seat on the Liverpool City Conned in the representation of the St. Peter's Ward. He will stand in the Conservative interests.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>TRADESMEN AS FOOTBALLERS.</head>
        <p>TRADESMEN AS FOOTBALLERS.</p>
        <p>The other day a team representing the tradesmen of Earlestown journeyed to Present, where, in a football match, they were the opponents of the Preseot trades- men. The visitors were Messrs. Hockenhall (goal), Siddeley and Eaton (backs), Bennett, Ireland, Sergeant- Major Smith (half backs), Sergeant-Major Hargreaves, Jones, Whiteman, J. Barrow, F. Summers (forwards), Sergeant-Major Lane (linesman). A hotly contested game—and one bristling with humorous incidents— resulted in a draw of one goal each. LOCAL CRICKETER'S WEDDING.</p>
        <p>At Tormorden Unitarian Church, last week, William Huddleston, of Ea,rlestown, the Lancashire County cricketer, late professional to the Church club and formerly at Tormorden, was married to Miss Ellen Whitehead, the elder dart .ter of Mr. Howorth White- * head, tinplate worker, T rden. The wedding was a quiet affair, though it att ted many to the church. Mr. Huddleston's deeds in the cricket world are too recent and well known to need reiteration here, and it is sufficient to say that his many friends in Earlestown I wish him good luck and a long innings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>CHILDREN'S GUILD.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S GUILD.</p>
        <p>Last Thursday the Children's Guild, which meets in Legh-street Mission Hall, Earlestown,daring the winter opened for the session, an interesting programme being being submitted. Jessie Traylor officiated as the presi- dent and delivered a neat address. Amongst those taking part were May Peers, Charles Thane, P. Appleton, E. Sharp, Maggie Halton, Nellie Thane, Nellie Birchen, Alice Hill, Esther Bailey, Hilda Seddon, Ethel Thane, Winifred Seddon, Jessie Jones, Grace Ellis, Gladys Jones and others. Mr. T. Martindale trained the chil- dren.</p>
        <p>NEWTON CRICKET CLUB. The prizes for the best averages daring the past cricket season amongst the members of the Newton Cricket, Bowling and Tennis Club were presented on Saturday evening on the occasion of the annual dinner. In connection with the first team batting average prizes were presented to D. Cordingley (2),and J. Pendry (Mr. Russell's special), and bowling average prizes to 1. R. Howarth (club prize),and C. W. [Lindley (Mr. Longton's special). In the second team, batting prizes were pre- sented to F. Harrison (2), and H. Lyons (Mr. Russell's special),and bowling prizes to T. Wilkinson (club prize) and C. F. porter (Mr. Longton's special).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>CONGREGArIONAL SOCIAL.</head>
        <p>CONGREGArIONAL SOCIAL.</p>
        <p>A very successful social was held in the Congrega- tional School Earlestown last week under the auspices of the married ladies connected with the church 'lt was estimated that about 250 were present. Tea was provided, and the usual table and parlour games kept the company lively until time for dispersing. During I the evening solos were rendered by Mrs. Clarke (" An Irish lullaby ") and Mrs. Turner (" Granny's story "). Mrs. Marsh was responsible for a reading from the renowned " Curtain Lectures" of Mrs. Candle, and Mrs. Bailey for an extract from the equally funny " Throe men in a boat." The Rev. J. Davidson, in the absence of a chairwoman, was called npon to preside. The pro- ceeds are to be devoted to the fund for a sale of we rk which is in prospect.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH. Successful Sale of Work. The Wycliffe Memorial Hall in Edgeworth-street was on Thursday afternoon the scene of a very successful sale of work in aid of the renovation scheme which has been so admirably carried out at the Church, and the liquidation of the debt on the Memorial Buildings. The ladies of the congregation had entered into ale sect with great enthusiasm, and the result was a line</p>
        <p>,proje,_ gretu, e-. collection of useful and fancy wares, which were very ' tastefully arranged. The following acted as stall- holders : Fancy Work Stall, presided over by Mrs. Armitage. Mrs. E. S. Garland, Mrs. I. Longland, Miss Harrison, Miss Walker, Miss Slack, Miss Helen Armi- tage, Mrs. Yonge. Plain Work, presided over by Mrs. Walker Mrs. W. Wallington, Mrs. Winskill, Mrs. J.W. Jeffery, Miss Ellison, Mrs. Slack, Mrs. Southerden, Mrs. Batty, Mrs. Podmore, Mrs. Porritt. Refreshment Stall, presided over by Mrs. Pearson, Miss Porritt, and other members of the Choir, very ably assisted by Airs. John Atkinson and her daughters. There was a large attendance at the opening of the sale by Mrs. Rylands, of Massey Hall. The Rev. J. Yonge presided, and among those present were the Revs. H. Wall, C. Harvey Cook and J. F. Matthews, Miss B. Dutton, Mrs. NV. Dutton, Mrs. P. , Silcock, Miss Monks, Mrs. F. W. Monks, Mrs. H. Roberts, Mrs. Bedell, Mrs. Howard, Mr. and Mrs. H. Barker (Buxton), Miss Morris. The Rev. Joitx YONGE said the sale had been arranged for the purpose of endeavouring to reduce their lability with regard to the recent renovation of the Church, which had been an expensive business. They thought that while they were doing that, they had better com- bine the entire extinction of the remaining debt out he memorial buildings—a sera of ,V 209. In addition to that S2OO they found they must necessarily incur heavy expenditure upon repairs,both externally and internally, ,to the church and its rooms and the renovation _and</p>
        <p>):".,.:?. 1..;.4.7..,',;.",,,,'"' ' .f..',.',::.4:;.t'-'-;,F4,.`,.;.'e:0';''..;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WHO TOLD P</head>
        <p>WHO TOLD P</p>
        <p>Investigation Committee's Fruitless Inquiries.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that the Town Council recently appointed a special committee" to investigate a report which appeared in the Warrington Guardian' on the 15th September, 1900, relating to the action pending between the Corporation and Colonel Edelsten." Their inquiries, however, have been fruitless. In their report issued last evening the committee state : In accordance with the terms of the above resolution we, the undersigned members, beg to report that %e have to the best of our ability carefully investigated all circumstances which might tend to throw any light upon the publication in the " Warrington Guardian on the 15th ultimo of a certain paragraph relative to the above action.</p>
        <p>We regret to say that we are unable to ascertain the manner in which information was conveyed to the Press.</p>
        <p>_ We would, however, deplore the haphazard way in which individual members of the Council give informa- tion to the Press at times, and we would suggest whether it would not be wise for the Council to con- sider the advisability of letting all Press communica- tions go throueh the head cabials of the varibns departments who would then be responsible to the Council for the veracity and expediency of the statement.</p>
        <p>In conclusion we may say that we have met with the utmost courtesy and assistance from all parties during the investigation. JOHN' WILKINSON. THOS. BARTON. JAS. EVAN. JOHNPENTBERTON. CEO. T. WILD (NC. JAMES BALL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>SOLDIERS' MEMORIAL.</head>
        <p>THE SOLDIERS' MEMORIAL.</p>
        <p>Prince of Wales Unable to Come.</p>
        <p>A letter has been received from the Private Secre- tary to the Prince of Wales, expressing the regret of His Royal Highness that his engagements will not permit his accepting the invitation of the Warrington Soldiers' Memorial Committee to unveil the statue which is shortly to be erected in Queen's Gardens in memory of the men of the South Lancashire Regi- ment who fell in the late war. The committee have met and decided to invite Sir Redvers Buller, V.C. to perform the interesting ceremony. The statue has been successfully cast and is ready for delivery at any time. The Borough Surveyor is proceeding with the construction of the foundations.</p>
