      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>TEMPLE CILiMBERS, LONDON, E.C.</head>
        <p>TEMPLE CILiMBERS, LONDON, E.C.</p>
        <p>NAIMERCIAL FAILURES.—ACCOrding to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, October 19th, was 141, viz. New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, Si; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 64. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 93 ; Deeds of Arrangement 95—total 193; being a decrease of 48. The total num- ber of Commercial Failures recorded during the 42 weeks of the present year is 6,719 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 42 weeks of last year was 7,202, showing a decrease of 433.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS.</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>--..•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LATEST FROM THE WARDS.</head>
        <p>LATEST FROM THE WARDS.</p>
        <p>Seven Contests.</p>
        <p>Nomination day is passtd and gone, and the electors now know where they are—at any rate they know definitely the names of the men who are seeking their vote and interest. A few days ago the position of affairs was chaotic, so many names of probable and possible candidates being in the air. The nomina- tions have cleared the atmosphere somewhat. There will be seven contests on Thursday next—an addition of three during the week. The newest wards to be drawn into the fray are Howley and Bewsey and Town Hall, the respective candidates being Councillor Sankey and Mr. W. Worthy (flowley Mr. J. A. J. Morton and Mr. Owen Toole (Bewsey) and Councillor Lord and Mr. A. Browne (Town Hall.) Two gentlemen who were expected last week to stand for election have withdrawn, viz. : Mr. J. Hughes Hill (Latchford) and Councillor T. Wright (Bewsey.) The latter is at present confined to the Infirmary as the result of an operation on the throat, and much sympathy will be felt with him in his inability to defend his seat. In only two wards are there un-opposed returns and the lucky candidates are Councillor Barton (Whitecross) and Mr. J. J. Neild, (Fairfield), the latter of whom enters the Town Council, for the first time. The contested wards and the candidates in each are as follows : THE CANDIDATES. Town Hall Ward Coon. Lord (N.-T.) Mr. A. Browne (I) Bewsey Ward Mr. J. Morton (L.) Mr. 0. Toole (I.) Whitecross Court. T. Barton (L.) St. Austin's Mr. J. Halliwell (L.) St. John's Coon. J. Magian (1.) Mr. T. S. Steel (C.) Latchford Conn. W. J. Wilson (C.) Mr. J. Dodds (N.-T.) Fairfield Mr. J. J. Meld (C.) Howley Coon. J. Sankey (C.) Mr. W. Worthy (I.) Orford Conn. E. Cunnington (L.N.-T.) Mr. W. Pook (Lab.) Mr. W. McNeil Sloane (C.) SOME BIG CONTESTS. LITCTIFORD.</p>
        <p>Chief interest a ttacii;;;-&amp;-; the contest in Latchford Ward, where Mr. James Dodds (the leader of the Non- Trading Agitation) challenges the return of Councillor W. J. Wilson, a Conservative nominee. The reasons which have inducod Mr. Dodds to make a welcome re-appearance in the electoral field are sufficiently well- known to obviate our recapitulating them at length. Mr. Wilson as Mayor's Auditor, has accepted the fee of £3O for the o like, which the Non-Traders contend it is illegal and immoral for him to touch as a mem- ber of the Council which votes the money. On this point the Non-Traders pledged themselves to test the feeling of the electors of Latchford, and Mr. Dodds has elected to bear the banner of civic purity and municipal disinterestedness, and in all parts of the Ward his advent is hailed with delight. There are, of course, determined opponents of Non-Trading in the Ward, and these men are supporting Mr. Wilson's , candidature independent of political considerations. It is a matter for regret that some of them are Liberals who are prepared to once again throw over the prin- ciples of a lifetime at the dictates of personal feeling. We have do doubt, however, that the electors of Latchford will return Mr. Dodds by a triumphant majority. He is the type of man the Council sorely needs and can be relied upon to serve the ratepayers with an eye to their interests alone. A meeting in support of Mr. Dodds' candidature will be held at the Parish Room, Grappenhall-road, Latch- ford, on Tuesday evening, with Alderman Pierpoint in the chair. _ _ ST. JOHN'S.</p>
        <p>Here Councillor Maginn and Mr. T.S.Steel—two use- ful men in public life—are fighting a strenuous battle. A grelt thing in Mr. Maginn's favour is the good service he has already rendered in the Council, and to this must be El dded his independence of all party ties. During the week Mr. Maginn has addressed three enthusiastic meetings, and his fearlessness in the face of monopoly and vested interests was apparent in all he said. Mr. Maginn is essentially a tighter and the right sort of man to look after the interests of St. John's Ward. He means to "keep the pot boiling" by holding a meeting in the Ward every night prior to the elec- tion. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>TOWN HALL.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>The third big fight will certainly be in Town Hall Ward where Councillor Lord, one of the ablest of the Non-Trading members, is opposed by Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, who stands as an Independent. Mr. Browne would have made a welcome addition to the Council, but the town cannot afford to lose the valuable services of Mr. Lord, who has made sacrifices in the interests of the ratepayers, which few men could have been willing to do. Appearances point to the Non- Traders retaining the Ward,which was first won by Mr. Dodds, and has since pronounced strongly in their favour at other elections. Sr. AUSTIN'S.</p>
        <p>The sitting member for St. Austin's, Councillor Ashcroft (Non-Trader) will have a difficult task to hold the seat against Mr. James Ha:liwell, a popular trades- man who is backed by the Liberal Association and at the same time expresses himself as favourable to the Non-Trading principle. In either event the party of reform does not stand to lose much. Mr. Halliwell believes he is a better man than Councillor Ashcroft and intends to persuade the electors to the same view if possible. The basis of his opposition is largely political, as Councillor Ashcroft was a strong sup- porter of 111 r. R. Pierpoint at the general election. The Non-Traders stand loyally by the retiring member who has always supported them through thick and thin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>BEWSEY.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY.</p>
        <p>A contest has only just been arranged in this ward, Mr. 0. Toole having come forward at the last minute. Both men are Liberals but as Mr. Morton has the official support of the party and is also accepted by the Non-Traders his return seems assured. Mr. Morton is best known as the indefatigable secretary of the Progressive Labour League. ORFORD.</p>
        <p>After all there will be—a— throe-corned fight in this Ward, with the odds in favour of Councillor Cunning- ton, who is personally popular and a loyal Non-Trader. His opponents are Mr. W. M. Sloane (C), and Mr. Pook (Labour). There is always something unsatisfactory about a triangular "duel," but we are hopeful of seeing no change in the representation of the Ward. ' HOWLEY.</p>
        <p>Here one candidate is Worthy and the other isn't—at least not by name. Councillor Sankey (C) has been assailed at the last minute by Mr. W. Worthy (E) who has always a chance seeing that his opponent is not one of the strong men of the Council, if unbroken sjlence is aaything to go by.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's meeting of the St. Helens Health ' repair of the organ. Altogether the amount required, Cominitte, Dr. Buchan, medical officer, reported on including the debt on the memorial buildings, was a several blocks of slum property, and asked for orders little over 000. It was a matter for some congratula- calling upon the owners to put them in proper repair, tion that they had been able, by the great generosity of and to make back-to-back houses into through houses.— their own people and the kindness of friends who had Alderman Massey said he understood that according to helped them in other directions, to raise a sum of £6OO. the by-laws, they did not allow back-to-back houses in (Applause). They hoped by that sale of work to make the town, on the ground that there was no free access a considerable hole in the remaining sum required. It of air and proper ventilation. All those houses ought was seventeen or eighteen years since Wycliffe Church to be altered and two thrown into one—The Chairman was previously renovated. He could look back now said the order that was suggested would have that over thirty years in connection with the church and the effect.—Alderman J. C. Gamble asked if there were i present renovation was the third they had had during many more houses of this kind in the town.—Dr. Buchan Ihi ii time of ministry. He thought he could say for their; said there were not many compared with the size of the , people that they were as generous in their disposition! town, but there was still a considerable nutiber. The j to-day as they had been in the years past, but owing to I proportion of back-to-back houses in St. Helens was i , circumstances over which none of them had any control 1 Councillor Naorfcst opened his campaign on Monday small.—Alderman J. C. Gamble said that back-to-back they • they did not possess some of those exceptionally capable ! evening when a well-attended meeting was held in houses had been bought up from time to tim