he congregation, not of her own work which had been done was entirely his own, but he ment. He was sorry that such a position should exist, knowledge, but her husband frequently told her of the maintained that his speeches of three years ago had He thought it was a great mistake, and that the time old times when he was a boy and went to brought the disgraceful state of the Ward before the would come when the question would be asked, in order the old Flag-lane chapel. He often talked 2f electors. (Applause). Councillor Maspinn then pro- to secure the full support of the electors," Are you a , those days. At one time when there was no resident ceeded to state his views on the subject of the scaveng- Socialist ?" (Applause.) • minister it was his duty to go and find supplies for the lag of the streets and said that in this matter he KILLED BY A MOTOR-CAR. 1 Sunday work, and he had told her of curious and , agreed with the views which had been expressed by amusing experiences he had on those expeditions. The ,the Chairman. It was a shame for young and strong , PRESCOT FATALITY, INQUEST. minister used to stay at Bewsey House, and occupied a men to take up old men's jobs. (Applause). Alluding Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at room known as" prophet's chamber." It never lost its to his action with reference to the increase in the salary Whiston Workhouse on Monday on the body of John name up to the end of the time that she and her husband of the Borough Surveyor, the candidate explained that Morgan Powell, aged forty-five years, of The Holt, lived there. Continuing, i Mrs. Rylancls said the stalls Mr. Longdin had the responsibility of looking after 20 Rainhill, who died on Thursday morning, at Whiston holders and helpers had done their work well. and *t schools under the new Education Act. It was more Workhouse Infirmary, from injuries to his head sus- now rested with the buyers to second their efforts an d profitable to the ratepayers to pay £lOO extra sal