      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>COUNCILLOR PAAGINN S CANDIDATURE. Enthusiastic Meetings. IN BRICK. STREET.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PAAGINN S CANDIDATURE. Enthusiastic Meetings. IN BRICK. STREET.</p>
        <p>Mr. Magian conclueerl by emphasising the fact that he was still an independent candidate. His candidature was heartily approved. IN HAMILTON STREET.</p>
        <p>Another largely attended and enthusiastic meeting in support of the candidature of Councillor Magina in St, John's Ward was held in Hamilton-street schools on Thursday evening. Mr. W. Beswick presided, and was supported by Councillor Maginn, Messrs. B. Farrar, S. Wright, G. Walker, Sexton and others. The CIL% lELMAN said he believed Connelllor Maginn to be the right man in the right place. ,lie did not belong to any clique or party. He the (chairman) was not a great political man but he liked to be on the right side and he al ways liked to help the man who was in the right and who would act fearlessly. The task of the Council was to regulate the principle of public and private owner- ship so as to secure the highest welfare of the individual and the community. He believed Mr.Maginn would see that the ratepayers' money was spent legitimately and in the right channels. €Applause.) Mr. B. FARRAR said he considered Councillor Maginn , was one of the best men in the Council. (Applause). I His actions in the Council Chamber must commend I themselves to every right-thinking man. They particu- larly admired his independence. (Applause). He (the sneaker) was sorry Hint the opponent of Councillor Machin was one of his (the speaker's) dearest friends whom he had know from infancy. He was blaming the party that had brought Mr. Steel out and had urged him to fight. He was certain that Mr. Steel would not I-have set himself against Councillor Mae'..inn at this time. I Although he was confident that Mr, Steel would be I defeated he believed that if Mr. Steel had kept to himself as an independent candidate be would have honoured the Council Chamber as well as any man could. (Applause). ; Councillor :11AGINN said it had been stated that he I refused to pay the scavengers of the town a living wage. It was nothing of the kind and the electors mnst not be gulled by such statements. He stated in the Council and he stated now that it was a disgrace to young men to take old men's jobs. They saw in the streets daily men who if they had pride at all would I take ups position better than sweeping the streets. He I believed that the scavenging should be a sort of reserve Position for the honourable old men who instead of throwing themselves on the rates wanted to earn a living. It was stated at Mr. Steel's meeting the previous night that he (Councillor Magian) was a Iturncoat,that he was once a Conservative. Many tne'h had called him many things, hut he thought no one had called him that before. (Laughter.) He might have been a Conservative without knowing it—(laughter)— but up to now he had never known one of the breed of them to be a Conservative, and be had not much admira- tion for the Irishman who was. (Laughter.) While they were talking of turncoats they must bear in mind whether Mr. Steel had turned his coat or not. The chairman of Mr. Steel's meeting was Councillor Fulton- Smith. He (Councillor Maginn) would point out that he secured Councillor Fulton-Smith's return as a repre- sentative of that Ward without opposition, through the action he took against his former opponent, Mr. Thorn- ton. In return for that Councillor Fulton Smith pre- sided at the meeting on Wednesday night and, Ihe believed, made a brilliant speech. (Laughter.) He made the first speech that night that he had made in the interests of St. John's Ward during his service in the Council, with one exception, and that was when he said he had examined the account of Lawson with reference to that notorious scandal the waterworks, and he wes quite satisfied it was correct. That was the only speech he (Councillor Maginn) knew Councillor Fulton Smith to have made during the two years. (Laughter.) The correctness amounted to this—the contract was about £25,000 and the bill to-day was over £70,000. (Laughter.) He (Councillor Maginn) was twitted by his opponents with the fact that he gave a picnic three years ago and that he had given none since. Let him remind Mr. Steel that the Conservative party gave two picnics, it, might be easy for Mr. Steel to forget those, but if he had paid the bill he would have remembered the tune. (Laughter.) He (Councillor Maginn) gave one picnic to the little ones when he failed to gain election. That picnic was brought about by a peculiar coincidence, a workingman sending him £lO towards his election expenses. When he won he (Councillor Maginn) gave another, and he would have continued the picnics if his pocket had been deep enough. (Applause.) Lie was well repaid by the hapPiness which those outings afforded the children and he hoped none of the electors would vote for him merely because he gave the picnics. Before his oppo- nents criticised, let them at least make an attempt to do the same. (Applause.) Dealing with the question of cheap tremfares, Councillor Maginn explained that his contention was that there should be a circular tram system through Battersby-lane and Orford-lane for the convenience of St. John's Ward before they gave more concessions in the way of cheap fares in other parts of the town. if not a tram service, let them have motor buses. (Applause). If is man was not able to pay his ordinary tram fare when he was working over-time and receiving time and half pay, what about the poor people in St. John's Ward who had to walk? (Applause). Councillor Maginn then dealt with the education question, and said that for the rates they were paying every man and woman who had a child and sent it to school should be able to send it free of charge. (Applause). He (Councillor Maginn) was not a teetotaler and he would be the last to expect workingmen to drink cold water,becanse he did not like it. (Laughter). He however, failed to see why workingmen should pay twice over for their glass of beer and that was what they were doing now. An attempt had been made for many years to put as many publicans in the Council as possible. The rates of the borough had to be paid accord- ing to the valuation of property with one exception, and that was the tied public-house. Before he had done with this campaign he would open the eyes of Warringtonians to the way they were being robbed. Dealing with the Bridge-s treet i improvement, Councillor Maeinn said the Corporation bought the Feathers Hotel and the property surrounding it for an enormous sum. The land was put up for auction and the Corporation were offered £5 per yard but refused it. It was then decided by the wisdom of the Council to re-build the hotel, and that cost, including land, £1,017 12s.' 6d. What did they think that house was rated at to-day? The enormous sum of £76 10s. The hotel had 16 yards of street frontage' and yet the Public Benefit Boot Store, which had the same frontage, was rated at £159. (" Shame.") When the hofel was not tied it was rated up to a recent period at £llO, and when a tied monopoly, which wanted representation now on the Council, bought it why should the assessment be reduced to £7O 10s. ? Then again, Bradley's shop with 2S ft. frontage was rated at £,119 and the Roebuck Hotel with 21yds.frontage was rated at £93 10s. The Assess- ment Committee was composed of ten men and War- rington which naid three-fifths of the poor rate had four representatives. Was it any wonder that the tied houses were going scot free when tbree of the ten vere interested in the drink traffic in one brewery ?Councillor Mswinn declared his intention of Retina independently end said he would not he sant back with a string on his leg whether by Catholizs Protestants, Liberals, Con- servatives or Socialists. (Applause.) Mr. E. ENGLAND said Councillor Magian was one of the best members of the Council. He regretted that Mr. Steel had allowed himself to be a tool in the hands of the Conservative party' because they knew very well ' he had allowed himself to be handled in that way because Councillor Maginn had the courage of his 'political opinions and did his best to get Mr. Crosfield ' returned. Every man had a right to express his political ' views at the General Election. (Applause.) Mr. G. WAT,KER also spoke strongly in favour of Councillor Maginn's candidature. _ . . -</p>
        <p>A AI-EMBER of the audience asked Councillor Maginn if he was in favour of a uniform wage of 255, per week for able-bodied men who were qualified to do a fair day's work and a guinea per week for old men who had got past labouring. Councillor 11rAcINN said be would givA able-bodied men who did an honest day's work more than 255. per week. but he would not favour paying a guinea a week to old men whom no one else would enaploy,because the Corporotion were not philanthropists. Questioned as to what he would do for old and faithful servants, Councillor ArAurgN said he was in favour of the Corporation setting apart Money to pro- vide old-age pensions for those who became enfeebled in their service. Beath of Mrs. Bowen Rowlanis.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mrs. Bowen Rowlands, widow of the late Judge Bowen Rowlands K.C., which took place at Waterloo, Liverpool, on Wednesday, after a long and painful illness. She was in her 59th year.</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER AND CO, PRINTERS. BOOKBINDERS. LITROGRAPHERS.</p>
