      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS BOY'S SHOCKING DEATH. Alfred Johnson aged eleven, living in Royle-street Sutton, met with a shocking accident on Tuesday afternoon, which resulted in his death some hours later at the Providence Hospital. The boy was going to school when he was knocked down by Messrs. Moore's mineral water lurry, from Garston, and had several ribs broken. Dr. Casey attended the lad, but his case was hopeless from the first. LANCASHIRE FRONTIERSMEN. There has been a branch of the National Service League at St. Helens for more than two years, and the town is now to have its Legion of Frontiersmen. At a meeting last week at Manchester, where the movement is very strong, Mr. Claude Young, of Brynn-street, St. Helens, was appointed organiser for the St. Helens district with the rank of lientenant. Mr. Young has seen active service, attached to the Imperial Light Horse, in South and West Africa. The Legion is com- posed of men who have been trained and qualified by previous service in any capacity as a soldier, sailor, hunter, &amp;c. It is hoped that qualified men will come forward. Steps are to be taken shortly to establish a branch in Liverpool. THE ST. HELENS COLLIERY TROUBLES. The disputes at the St. Helens collieries and Sutton Heath and Lea Green collieries are still under negotia- tion. In respect of the St. Helens colleries the manage - meat have made certain offers to Mr. T. Glover M.P., and Mr. Twist, which were submitted to the men this week. The chief difficulty is in securing fair terms for the men who are at work in difficult places, where they cannot earn a fair day's wage at, the ordinary prices. In regard to Sutton Heath the management have offered a minimum day's wage of Gs. Sd.for colliers and ss. id. for drawers engaged in difficult places, but, the men ask for 7s. fez. colliers. Questions of "crabb- ing "up the brows, 87. c., and free explosives under certain conditions in the Queen mine are matters under discussion, and the whole question will no doubt have to be decided at the meeting of the Miners' Federation on November 3rd.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS MAN'S DISTRESSING DEATH. A sensation was caused in the neighbourhood of West- field-street, St. Helens, on Sunday morning when it became known that Mr. James Fishwick, dairyman, aged twenty-nine, had been found in his stable dead, with a terrible wound in his throat. Mr. Fishwick and his family were widely respected, but for some time he had been in indifferent health. He had suffered from insomnia, and on Saturday he visited Liverpool with his mother to consult a specialist. The report as to his condition was by no means encouraging, as he was told that it would be necessary for him to go to the Delamere Sanatorium for the winter. Deceased was always very quiet, but this added to his depression. He did not say anything, but after family prayers, went to bed shortly after ten o'clock. Although he slept with his nephew Walter George Lathom, the boy does not remember deceased getting up, but at half-past six on Sunday morning, when the cowman, Michael Lydon, went into , stable he found Mr. Fishwick dead, with a razor lying beside him. Dr. Lathom expressed the opinion that death had taken place about three hours before. Deceased was a greatly respected member of the Primitive Methodist Church, and assisted his father in the busi- ness of dairyman. One of his brothers was the only I member of the St. Helens Volunteer Company who died in South Africa. ST. HELENS SLUMS, BACE-TO-BACE HOUSES TO BE ABOLISEIED.</p>
        <p>•ar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>DON'T DELAY IF YOU FEEL "OUT OF SORTS."</head>
        <p>DON'T DELAY IF YOU FEEL "OUT OF SORTS."</p>
        <p>Or if you have a friend whose constitution has been weakened, whether by overwork of brain or body by .exposure to the weather, by working long hours in, ill-ventilated rooms, by recent sickness, or by any cause tending to weaken the bodily powers ; in a word, if he is one who suffers from any form of complaint which may be the result of any of the above or similar causes; if, for instance, you can see that he is troubled with Indigestion, the bane of our Country and of modern civilization; or, if you think he has Low Spirits, that sure sign of some Nervous Disorder, bringing in its train a host of other complaints such as Melancholy, Lassitude &amp;c., or, perhaps, Neuralgia, and frequently, Sleeplessness, which of itself is sufficient to weaken the trongest constitution, and to make life a burden; or, if being free from these,be is regarded by his friends or himself as being of a delicate constitution, subject to Bronchitis, Coughs, Colds, a tendency to Consumption, or other Chest Affections, and if he, like thousands of others, failed to get relief from other remedies,tell him he need not be disheartened, that there is one Remedy whose virtues are so great, that it has succeeded where others have failed, and that there is hope he may be cured,as thousands of others have been cured before him by making a sensible and timely use of Gwilym Evans Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. If your friend then gives it a fair trial, he will be fully convinced, as others have been, that this is the Best Remedy of The Age. You or your friend may ask " What is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters ?" It is a Preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffering from Debility, Nervousness, and General Exhaustion, and if any value be attached to human testimony, the efficacy of this medicine has been suc- cessfully established. Its claims have been tested and proved by the mediFal profession and others, and corro- borated by the written testimonials of eminent men. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contain not only as suitable quantity of Quinine in each dose, but the active principles of the following well-known herbs— Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, and Dandelion. The use of Quinine is well kown but it has never been satisfactorily combined with these preparations, until after overcoming considerable difficulties, the proprietor was able to secure a perfectly uniform preparation, combining all the essential properties of the above plants in their greatest purity and concentration. It is now estab- lished as a family medicine,and is increasing in popular favour the more it is known and tested. Gwilym Evans' Qsinine Bitters is a tonic " Pick-me-up" scientifically mixed in happy proportions. Sold everywhere in bottles, 2s. 9d. and 4s. lid, each. Should any difficulty be experienced in procuring Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded carriage free for the above prices by the Sole Pro- prietors: The Quinine Bitters Manufactuiing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. We particularly caution our readers against imitators, and impress upon them the importance of always seeing that the name 1 " Gwilym Evans "is on the Label, Stamp, and Bottle without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>liVscliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance at the meeting of this Society on Sunday. when Captain Crosfleld presided and spoke upon " Patriotism," as described by John Ruskin. He had no sympathy with the spirit of Jingoism as commonly known,nor yet with a kind of Con- scription, nor did he believe in the kind of patriotism which said "I will not go myself to defend my country against an invader, but you go as my substi- tute." He did not object to be called a Conscriptionist, if by that term was meant every man to be willing, and ready under regular conditions, to go in for a regular course of training ; to be able to use the sword and gun. The men of England should be ready in the time of need, or in case of any invasion, to rise as one man, to defend their country's cause. He would deprecate any spirit of interfering with other countries in the management of their own affairs, but he would be ready to say to any country wno would interfere with our affairs " hands off."—Councillor James Evans gave an address upon "The Ideals of Life." He pitied the man who had few or no ideals, the man who when asked " What are you doing ?" said "I don't know." The P.S.A. and the Christian Church had an opportunity to make these men have a desire for ideals. They would then be better fitted and ready when the time,came, which the Captain had spoken about. He admired the Captain's energy and great thoughtfulness upon this question. He agreed with "no interference," and that the country ought not to seek a quarrel, but like the Quaker, he would he ready to say Hands off "to the intruder.—Mr. Warburton sang ." Remember Mighty God " and" My hope is in the Everlasting "in good style. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ.—The Secretary welcomed three new members, the total now being 494; 12s. 2d. was the contribution to the Organ Fund from the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sunday morning the Rev. J. Manning nreaehed from the text "Therefore I put thee in -ernembrance that thou stir up the gift of God, which is in thee." As the third Sunday in October is set apart each year by the Wesleyans as a day when a special appeal is to be made to Young People, the words of the old apostle Paul to his young follower Timothy. who was just about to undertake the responsible post of Pastor to an important Church,were especially suitable. Mr. Manning dwelt upon the responsibility of the position in which young people— " the heirs of all the ages "—find themselves to-day. In the evening the Rev. Sidney Pitt preached on the sub- ject of " Christ and the Social Question." The gist of the sermon is expresssd in the words "The Kingdom of God is within you." Great social changes may affect the outward conditions of our people—and Mr. Pitt paid tribute to all who labour to ameliorate the dread- ful condition of our poor—bnt they are powerless to produce real content and perfect happiness. While, on the other hand, if the dominion of Christ be accepted in the heart, the change works outwardly and is soon seen in the life. As the preacher naively remarked, "If the husband becomes converted and takes all his wages home on a Friday night, there is a social revolu— tion at once—in one household at least." On Monday night a meeting of the Wesley Guild was held in the 7decture Hall, when Mr. W. Webster gave a most interesting scientific lecture on " The Ant." Dr. Ferguson presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Rev. H. Donnie° at Liverpool. RECOGNITION MEETING.</head>
        <p>Rev. H. Donnie° at Liverpool. RECOGNITION MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Dunnico was on Monday publicly wel- comed to the city by the Free Churchmen of Liverpool. The proceedings opened with a convention at noon, over which the Rev. F. B. Meyer, of London (president Baptist Union of Great Britain and Irehmd), presided. Then followed a luncheon in the lecture hall, over which Mr. Samuel Smith presided, there being a very large gathering of the leading Free Church ministers of Liverpool and district, and including Mr. Seddon, M.P. for Newton Division ; Alderman Bennett, J.P., War- rington ; and Mr. John Bennett, church secretary. Mr. Meyer (who had to leave to catch an early train to London) just briefly mentioned that he wanted them all to combine public life with a very close walk with God. The country wanted public-spirited men, but also devoted men.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seddon, M.P., pleaded that all should believe the Labour party were working with the sole idea of better- ing humanity in general, and not look askance or with fear at a growing movement which was endeavouring to get back to first principles and believed in the brother- hood of man and the fatherhood of God. (Applause).</p>
        <p>, Mr. Dunnico suitably responded. In the evening Kensington Church was crowded, and the platform was filled with some of the most promi- nent Free Church religious leaders. Mr. John Thomas (Garston) in the chair. Mr. John Bennett (church secretary) read' the statement showing the unanimity of the call, after which the Rev. H. V. Thomas (secretary Lancashire and Cheshire Baptist Church Association), followed by Principal Bowser (of Nottingham). and Rev. J. S. Hughes (of Hill Cliff, \Carrington) testified to Mr. Dunnico's character and achievements. . Mr. Aked (Pembroke), said he had seen many recogni- tion meetings, but never one so joyous, so hopeful, so prophetic, and so inspiring as that. Mr. Aked read the following letter : Dear Mr. Aked,—At a recent meeting of the old students of Midland Baptist College.a request was made that you should convey the congratulations of the brotherhood to the church in Liverpool over which Mr. Dunnico has been called to preside. They believe that his gifts as a preacher, his strong social sympathies, and his high Christian character fit him for work in your great city. They will watch the progress of his work with prayerful interest, and they confidently hope that the united efforts of pastor and. people will, under the blessing of God, build up a rich church in Christian brotherhood, aggressive in work f9r Christ's Kingdom, and clear and forceful in its witness for righteousness.—With faternal regards, yours very truly (Signed) John Clifford, chairman; E. Carrington, secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>(copyrzot.) THE MERMAID'S. CAVE.</head>
        <p>(copyrzot.) THE MERMAID'S. CAVE.</p>
        <p>By C. GRAHAM WHYTE, Author of "Lady Rafferty's Vow," "Haphazard House," "The Priest's Curse," "A Woman's Honour," "Snapshots in Paris," &amp;c.; Szo.</p>
        <p>"Oh, no, I don't forget my promise, and I will tell you all about it presently. Make yourself corn! fortable though. Throw on a few more turf and build them up nicely, and when we get our pipes lit and the place looks a bit more snag-like, I will begin." I built up the turf carefully on the broad hearth, for Jack Brooke was fastidious about fires, and held that no man in Mayo could equal himself in making the turf blaze. I had accented his invita- tion for a fortnight's wild-fowl shooting in mid- 1 winter, and now my time was almost up, and Jack, to my great surprise, was becoming confidential. In his school days he had been a moody, reservedi sort of fellow, and as he grew older those , characteristics became more emphasised. An , opportunity now presented itself of seeing beneath the cold, cynical exterior of the man, and I was careful not to lose it.</p>
        <p>"There, that will do," he said, as I laid the last turf slantwise against the others. "Now I will m redee my promise., I feel a bit queer, though, when I think of what I am going to tell you to- night. It is a confession that will throw some light on my reasons for living in this wild, old place on the Atlantic, which you fashionable fellows reckon outside the bounds of civilisation. Look I here, I would sooner listen to the waves breaking out there at Dummy Point than to the music of your finest London orchestras! "But to my story. You remember my young brother Cecil. He must have been in the same form as you at Rathmines School. Well, four years ago next spring, he and I came down here and leased this place. We had to put the house in repair, and it cost some money to do it. Cecil was a bit run down in health through over-study, and the doctors had recommended rest and quiet- ness. He had a literary bent, as you may remember, and was always dabbling in books and papers. He called himself a worshipper of Nature, and nothing pleased him better than to wander about all day with his head in the clouds and a tote-book orta sketch-block in his pocket For my part I liked most the quiet evenings we spent in this room together, he writing at the table there and I lounging over the fire, smoking and listening to his literary productions when he read them aloud to me. We kept to ourselves a good deal, partly through inclination and partly because there was, and indeed still is, such a pronounced lack of</p>
        <p>' knowable ' people about here: "The coming of a stranger is quite an event in our little world, and when three years ago ea Colonel Burke snd his daughter Violet came to live at Ivy Lodge, the day of their arrival was a red-letter day in the annals of Dunany parish. About a week or so after they came Cecil and I were over at Westport, and we met the old Colonel at the club. He *as very chatty and entertaining, and during dinner disclosed to us some wonderful plan he had for developing the fishing industry in the west of Ireland. He was pleased with the interest we seemed to take in his scheme, and, before we left, invited us to finish the discussion, as he put it, at Ivy Lodge on the following Satur- day afternoon. There, the fire is falling to pieces, man. Let me settle it now."</p>
        <p>I saw that this break in the story was inten- tional, and I waited quietly until the spirit moved him again. "Well," he said, at length, "we went to Ivy Lodge on that Saturday afternoon, and here, my dear fellow, commenceth the romance. I.range, isn't it, how a little bit of sentiment craPps into every fellow's life, and stranger still how we love, at times, to turn our eyes inward and gaze upon those treasured pictures of the past which form the priceless legacy of our one, real affaire du caw? "The old Colonel and his daughter had come down the avenue to meet us, and we met them at the lodge gates. I am afraid I could scarcely describe her faithfully. Let me try to recall the picture. There they come, 'she leaning on his arm, the ,contrast perfect. He old, careworn, slightly stooped, grey; she young, fresh, graceful, fair. An oval face, features prominent and clear-cut as a cameo, large, grey, smiling eyes, fair hair which the sunlight warmed to golden. She was in mourning, too, and the sombre colour of her dress threw into relief the delicate clearness of her skin. lam describing to you what I must have seen, though, of course, I had not time to note all her attractions then. What struck me most, I remember, was the happy innocence and the atmosphere of joyousness which surrounded her. ' She seemed born to love and to be loved. I grant you I was hard hit, and all my cold, cruel thoughts of women vanished suddenly. But why delay. Cecil, with his sensitive, receptive nature, felt her influence even more keenly than I did, and from that day on we became, each in his own way, more and more her slaves. It was foolish, no doubt, extremely foolish. We should have come to some understanding and not have allowed our- selves to drift pleasantly towards the whirlpool. Often and often down there in the bog when waiting for the wild-duck I reasoned the case out, and every time I came to the conclusion that it would be better for either one or other of us to leave the county. I hinted as much to- Cecil, but he only laughed and said that Violet seemed to like us both equally well and would not, he was sure, care tp lose either of her faithful cavaliers. And so the months went on and the plot thickened.</p>
        <p>"It is only a truism to eay that when a man becomes fond of a girt his desire for possession is awakened, and he considers that his love gives hint a kind of hazy, indefinite claim on the object of his affections. He says, at least to himself, that she is his girl, and he thinks that every other man should intuitively acknowledge and readily acquiesce in this unexpressed, and to them unknown, claim. Men, too, are careful to hide from one another the secrets of their hearts. Cecil and I were no excep- tions to either rule. We talked to each other about Violet in an easy, frivolous way; and a stranger listening to our conversation would, no doubt, have classed our admiration as nothing more than the ordinary tribute which pretty women exact from men. But our hearts gave the lie to our lips, and a darkening shadow crept between us. We did not seek each other's company so much, and the pitiable ways in which, at length, we avoided spending the evenings together was positively shameful. Cecil found that he required absolute quietness for his literary work, and I, who was more to be blamed than he, encouraged him to keep to his own quarters upstairs. We knew we were foolish, but we were infatuated with our foolishness. Of course, we took every opportunity of seeing our idol. Cecil and she yachted a lot together, and by way of compensation she came out frequently for a drive in my dog-cart. I had a firm ally in the old Colonel, who, knowing I was an eldest son with some prospects, and perhaps with an eye to the girl's future, encouraged me in a quiet, unobtrusive way. "One day, however, matters reached the inevitable climax; our strained relations could bear no further tension, the thunder-cloud burst. On the previous evening Cecil had been over at the little church, assisting with the Christmas decora- tions, and he mentioned casually at breakfast that he intended helping Violet to give the pulpit some finishing touches that afternoon. I said I thought I would' go too, and he replied, rather warmly, that he supposed he could not prevent me. What occurred afterwards Ido not like recalling. Both of us got angry, and though we did not descend to ' Billingsgate ' we slanged each other roundly. Hot jealousy, repressed 'for months, darted out, and, forgetting for the time that we were brothers, we faced each other with all the hatred of jealous Ah, I think I can see his wild eyes blazing at me out of the tire there, and can hear again his sharp, stinging reproaches. He was -impatient of reproof of ' any kind, and, knowing this, I patronised him until I drove him nearly mad. If he struck me I knew I should have had him at my , mercy, and I incited him on with this object in I view. God forgive mg for being so intensely vindictive! Suddenly he seemed to gain control of himself, and his face became white and hard. Looking across the table there with his large, lustrous eyes shifting fiercely, lie held up his right hand and hissed out: Listen, John Brooke! You may say I am a fool or a madman, or anything else you like, but this day decides our fate! if Violet accepts me, then neither you nor any man living will take her from me. If she refuses me, then may the Lord have mercy on my soul.' "' Amen,' was my parting shot as he left the MOM. "I watched him from the window as he hurried down the garden path on to the road, and took the turn towards Ivy Lodge. Then I sat down at the table and commenced to fill cartridges with the</p>
      </div>
