      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</p>
        <p>73 mango annop zumna jiataut punon pus tun; alp u paxpu Atotiataos lug -Wm zoj noor o 200 etno uAtop 2uto2 Jo uonueluf powder in some of the cases, and double charges of shot in others. I gave it up and came over to the fire, lighted my pipe, and settled myself down for a good tbink. I tried to prove to myself that I had been very badly treated, and I grew hot at the remembrance of the cruel insults I had borne. I ; would demand an apology, a written apology, and if it were refused I would not allow Cecil to remain in the house. Thoughts of a duel flitted through my fevered brain, and I pictured myself wounding Cecil slightly, just enough to satisfy my injured honour. Then quieter thoughts supervened. The strong current of our old affection burst through the barriers which jealousy had raised, and I experienced a• sudden, sickening fear. I became restless and anxious. I felt I was a murderer at heart, and : that if anything happened to him, I alone would i be guilty before God. The idea gripped me and got on my nerves. I felt miserable as I thought I saw the lengthening shadow of approaching I trouble. Again and again during the afternoon _,,1 tried to chase the thought away, but the more endeavoured to avoid it the more the dark presenti- ment gripped my being. I was so much under its influence that I did not stir out of the house all day, and this omission of my customary exercise increased my nervousness. When seven o'clock came and Cecil did not turn up for dinner the suspense became intolerable. I could endure it no longer, and, crushing down my pride, and brushing aside all the objections which my- vanity suggested, I was about to leave the room and get my overcoat, when the sound of hurried footsteps on the gravel caused me to draw back. The hall door was open, and I heard footsteps in the passage. Then came a knock at my door, and, without waiting for permission, in rushed Violet's maid- of-all-work, Biddy. " The mistress told me to find you and give you that, sir, without delay,' she said, handing me a small, black-edged envelope. "In a state of suppressed excitement I turned my back on the girl, opened the note, and read: • Am in trouble. Come at once.—V..'</p>
        <p>_ There's something wrong,' Biddy exclaimed, as I placed the note in my breast-pocket, and I'm fearing it's Master -Cecil that's in trouble.' You can follow,' I said to her, abruptly. 'Miss Violet wants me, and I'm off.' "In less than a minute I was out on the broad road, running at break-neck speed towards Ivy Lodge. It will be the record run for the distance I thought as I dashed through the open gates and made a final spurt up the drive. "Violet stood in the porch awaiting me. " Oh, I'm so glad you'veame and so soon, too,' she said, as she led theeßy into the dining- room.</p>
        <p>_ " ' You got my note,' she exclaimed, 'and me.' have nearly killed yourself running to help me. " Yes, I got your note,' I gasped. 'But what's the trouble—Cecil ? '</p>
        <p>Yes, Cecil,' she answered, blushing deeply. ' But sit down and compose yourself and I will tell you how matters stand. You will not be able to help me unless you keep very cool. Listen ! ' And she held up a warning forefinger. He came here in the forenoon, and, finding I was not in, he went on to the church. I found him there, later on, and he seemed very strange and nervous. He was intensely critical and found fault with our whole scheme of decoration. In fact, I had the grouping on two of the windows altered to please him. He came back with me for lunch and then went away. About an hour ago he returned looking very ill-at- ease and more wretched than before. He told me he had been down at the boat-house having a look at the Sea-Mew, as he was thinking of taking her out for a sail one of these wild nights. I tried to dissuade him as gently as I could, but he only t..ecame more excited and commenced striding up i.nd down the room. Suddenly he stopped, and, looking straight into my face, told me of the row you and he had this morning. I said it was a great pity that anyone should come between you, arid I volunteered to act as peacemaker. At this he really got angry, and chided me with being fonder of you than of him. His hot, dark passion was fully aroused, and he said hard things of you, of me, and of all the world indiscriminately. He tried to force me into a confession, and I naturally resented it. It was too much to bear, and I, too, got angry and said I would not allow him to con- trol my likes and dislikes. Oh, how sorry I am now I. He said he knew I didn't care for him, and nobody cared for him, and his life was a burden. Then he rushed out of the room like a madman, and though I called after him he did not heed me. Oh, I know, I dceel, that he is going to do some- thing desperate, and We Juust save him, we must save him I .</p>
        <p>....... "There was silence for a moment, and then, holding myself well in hand, I said, quietly: And so you really care for him ? ' "Her beautiful face lighted up with that look of devotion which is love's greatest tribute. - "‘ Jack,' she said, I would die for him.'</p>
        <p>"I had heard enough. My own hopes were dashed to the ground, and I determined, then and there, to save the brother at all costs. "Ideas come rapidly at a time like that. I thought of the boat-house and Cecil's anxiety to see that the Sea-Mew was all right. I was about to speak when Violet said, excitedly Yes, I know what you are thinking about. I feel that he has gone to the boat-house, and has taken out the Sea- Mew and intends to sink her, or do something desperate out there at sea. We must prevent him. 0 Jack, we must save him!' "I am a strong man and hardened to women, but the expression of intense anxiety in that girl's moist eyes pierced me like an arrow. • "She suggested following him in her own boat, and I readily acquiesced. But I must hurry on. Ten minutes later we were at the boat-house and found the Sea-Mew gone. Violet's yawl, the Hawk, was lying on the slip, and with the help of a friendly coastguard, who happened to be going his nightly round, we launched her. Far out at sea when the moon shone we could make out the white sail of the Sea-Mew. The cdastguard lent me his glass, and I could see the little boat rising and falling on the ocean swell. I muttered something about an important telegram and asked him if he had been about when Cecil left. We were shoving off when he answered: ' Yes, sir, I was 'ere, and 'elped Mr. Cecil to get the boat. ready. 'E was talkin' about sea-nymphs and queer things an' said as 'ow e'd like to sail round by moonlight and see the Mermaid's Cave. Better reef, sir, before you get outside, as it looks dirty beyond the breakwater.' "Violet took the helm and I went forward to brace up the sails. We carried a jib and mainsail, and when I had made everything taut and had taken in two reefs in the mainsail I sat down beside her. The night was fairly clear though bitterly cold, and the moon was nearing the full. The wind was blowing strong in-shore, with fitful gusts, and indications of rougher weather. In one long tack we made the buoy at the breakwater, then putting about stood out to sea. Now we experienced the wisdom of the coastguard's advice. Our sails filled out to the utmost and rushed the boat from swell to swell. Up and down we raced, at one moment in the hollow, at the next on the top ,of a mountainous wave. Now our boat's nose was half-buried in the seething water, now it seemed suspended in mid-air. I confess lam not a good ' sailor, and I hate to feel the water swirling away from under the stern. It makes me think that the boat will never pull up and get level again. But that night I clenched my teeth and braced myself together for whatever might happen. 'Violet sat as if fixed to the boat, moving the tiller slightly now and then to keep the sails tilled. My admiration for her increased as I watched how adroitly she laid the yawl's head off and avoided being swamped by the big swells. She was a skil- ful sailor and could hold her own with any of the fishermen round the coast. I could not help gaining confidence when I saw the fixed look in her hand- some features, and the air of vsolution and preci- sion which accompanied her movements. She did not trust herself to speak until we had passed the breakwater and were well out in the bay. Then, I without taking her eyes off the sail, she said, abruptly: " Cecil will make the Mermaid's Cave before us if we follow him round Dunany Point. He must be clove to the Point now. There is only one alternative.' ' ' ' '</p>
        <p>"What's that ? ' I queried, with a sinking at my heart The Narrows,' she KM, quietly, and, looking into my face, she added: Are you afraid ? ' "I was indeed afraid, but I dared not confess it. The Narrows,' you know, is the name of that short, narrow channel which divides Dunany Island front the mainland. In calm weather it is pleasant enough to go through, when you have day- light and can see where you are going, but in stormy weather, even in daylight, it is dangerous to attempt it. At night in rough weather not a man down the coast would risk it. "'Are you afraid ? ' she repeated. And as I still hesitated, she said, firmly: You need not fear. I know every of the channel, and by going tbrouffil we wilt with the heln of God. intercent</p>
        <p>the Sea-Mew. I feel that Cecil's life is in the balance and that decides me. Because you are a man you calculate the chances beforehand, and because lam a woman 1 don't calculate. I belieNe we can do it, and I sin going to act accordingly. You agree ? ' a I nodded. "'Then make ready to go about.' "I obeyed with alacrity, and the Hawk swung round on the other tack.</p>
        <p>Get ready to lower the mainsail. Lower away,' were her next orders, as we neared the dark cliffs which flanked the entrance to the channel. I can manage better with the j::b only,' she said, as I resumed my seat by her side. "I felt a cold-shiver down my back as I looked ahead and saw the waves breaking on either side of the narrow passage towards which we were driving. The sliding movement of the water, quick as the fastest mill-race, fascinated me, and I clutched the seat for safety. I must say I did feel a bit dizzy as we shot into the shadow of the cliffs and rushed through the spray-screened opening into the moonlight beyond. Down the channel we raced, shaving a rock here, turning a corner there, and dashing straight through the centre of a swirling whirlpool further on. A dozen times, in as many minutes, we were almost gone. It seemed to me like a hideous nightmare, and I gripped the seat to satisfy myself that I was awake. At last the channel gradually widened, the roar of the sea behind us lessened, and we emerged into comparatively calm water on the other side.</p>
        <p>"Thank God we are through,' said Violet, earnestly, and we can save Cecil. now.'</p>
        <p>"We were by this time, running out under the lee of the island in fairly smooth water. Away in the distance we could make out the Sea-Mew rounding the Point and standing towards us. Mid- way between the boats lay the dreaded Mermaid's Cave. We hoisted the mainsail, shook out the reefs, and the speed quickened. Our short cut through the dangerous channel had given us a decided advantage in the race, and we passed the cave when the Sea-Mew was a good knot outside us. Violet steered straight for the boat. "' We will pass close to her stern,' she said. ' Get ready a boat-hook and grapple as we pass.' "L obeyed, and shouting to Cecil, who was hidden by his own mainsail, to stand by, I grabbed as we touched. The hook caught and the boats glided together. "Be careful, Jack,' I heard Cecil shout. ' Look out astern! She's going to jibe! Good Heaven's, Violet's down!' The next moment he had leaped into our boat and rushed astern. I followed him with my eyes, and to my horror saw that Violet had fallen senseless athwart the gunwale. "It was the boom did it. I saw it swing and strike her. Poor little girl; it is a nasty mark. Oh, what a brute I've been. Violet! Violet ' Cecil moaned as he lifted her gently and sat down in the stern sheets.</p>
        <p>"She did not answer, but lay white and still with her head on his shoulder and that cruel mark darkening her fair forehead. We saw that medical aid was urgently needed, and without any hesita- tion I cut the Sea-Mew adrift. Going about we headed for the shore and made for the pier at Crossdoney. We had to pass the Mermaid's Cave again as we tacked, and it was when we were close in and could see the fantastic, dissolving views which the white spray made in the moonlight, and hear the weird, fascinating wave music which echoed through the deep cavern that Violet awoke. Cecil,' she said, feebly, 0 Cecil! Cecil I ' Then lifting her head quickly she looked wildly towards the cave. Am I dreaming? Oh, there's that horrible place! I see their nasty, grinning faces under the cliff there. No, you will not get him. Jack and I will save him.' •</p>
        <p>"The effort exhausted her, and shedropped back unconscious on his shoulder. Cecil turned and kissed her lovingly. I had already told him part of what had occurred, and as the Hawk slipped through the water I told him the rest. It is unnecessary to dwell on what he did then. He did not say much, but, judging by the passing expres- sions on his face, his anguish must have been intense. As we came near the shore he said, hoarsely: Jack, I have been a brute both to her and to you. I did intend ending it in the Mermaid's Cave to-night. I guessed your motive when I saw you coming, but the devil of jealousy urged me to wait events and refuse to turn back unleis she confessed before you that she loved me. My heart must have been turned to stone. But when I saw the sail swing and the boom strike her, and she fell unconscious from the blow, the reaction came and I felt my heart bound towards her with all the•old fervour auk all the old love. I have been a brute to you, to id man, but with the help of God I will make amends. You must forgive me as you expect to be forgiven.' "Therels little more to tell. We got Ashore at Crossdoney, and, with the help of some fishermen, carried Violet up to the Rectory, which is close'jby the pier. The local doctor pronounced her suffering from a slight attack of concussion of the brain, accompanied by shock, and said she must have passed through some severe mental strain. He ordered rest and quietness, and under his care she recovered rapidly. Cecil was constantly with her, and when she became convalescent and was removed to Ivy Lodge he made a daily pilgrimage there, laden with books and magazines and a lot of etecteras which she appreciated. I, too, went over sometimes with the brother, and, after spend- ing a pleasant half-hour with the interesting invalid, I would adjourn to the smoking-room and play chess and talk deep-sea fishing with the old Colonel. I was best man when the little affair came off last spring, and I must confess that, though I had schooled myself well, I felt keenly miserable throughout the ordeal. I have, by persistent effort, succeeded fairly well in living that feeling down, and Violet tells me prettily that I am the best of big brothers. She is happy, Cecil is happy, and I am, well—fairly happy too. "And now I've told you the story, and there the fire's out; and, look here, I want you to grant me a favour. When you go back to town and hear the old set trying to outdo each other in stories of woman's faults and woman's failures, just chip in with this tale, alter the names a bit, and tell them from me that there are more Violets—sweet, true -hearted Violets—than they think in the world." . [Tux END.</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>THE BOROUGH ACCOUNTS. MR. E. ENGLAND'S EXPLANATIONS AND SUGGESTIONS. The Birtles Property and the Waterworks Contract. A public meeting arranged by Mr. E. England, one of the elective auditors of the borough, for the purpose of reporting to the ratepayers upon his recent audit of the Corporation accounts was field in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Wednesday evening. There was only a small attendance, Mr. George Walker presiding. The CHArttmAN thought it was a duty which even auditors owed to those who elected them, that they should report on the accounts which they had the opportunity of scrutinising. He also believed that if Town Councillors recognized the same principle and gave an account of their stewardship at least annually it would be to their advantage. It one thing could be accomplished as a result of that meeting it would be a power of good in the town, and that was that they should have more confidence in themselves and in those who represented them than they had at present, owing to the insinuations and aspersions that were so freely cast about from time to time. Mr. ENGLAND said it seemed to him that they had, as it were, been sending political doctors into the Town Council to manage the town's affairs. They had had the Liberal doctor, the Conservative doctor, the Labour doctor, the Independent doctor, and the Non- Trading doctor. It was for the ratepayers to make what choice they liked, but for him his duty was to review and to lay before them for their information and consideration the effect that these gentlemen had had upon the constitution of the town, not only on its financial position but also upon the state of the health of the municipality ; for the health, the conditions the people lived in, the financial position and the com- mercial position were all closely interwoven with each other. Of course, it should be clearly understood that the present condition of the town was not simply the results of several years, but of the work of the genera- tion whiclaihad preceded us. He thought the ratepayers should hava had the opportunity of reading the report which he understood Councillor Wilson (the Mayor's anditur-) had sent to the Mayor, because after all he considered the report belonged to the town, seeing that the ratepayers had paid for it. in fairness to Mr. Wilson,he would like to say that that gentleman did his fair share of the audit. In the report which he (Mr. England) published in the Press he stated : "The FINANCIAL POSITION OF THE CORPORATION. is undoubtedly sound. It has been my lot and privilege for many years to have been in close touch with public men and public offices of other towns, and I have no hesitation in saying that the Warrington Corporation financially compares very favourably with others." That, he knew, was a surprise to many people in Warrington for they had been led to believe otherwise, owing to the extraordinary reports which had been circulated, which unfortunately went up and down the country and had done the town a great deal of damage commercially and otherwise. He would now, therefore. compare War- rington's financial position with 69 County Boroughs of England and thus prove what he had stated in his report. The average total debt per head in those 69 County Boroughs in the year ending 1905 was £lB ss. Gd. Warrington's debt per head in 1905 was £l7 7s. 2d. ; it was now £l6 12s. sd. The average reproductive debt(which included tramways,gas &amp;c.,) per head of the population of the 69 County Boroughs was Ell is. 11d, whereas in Warrington it was £ll 7s. 3d. The average non-reproductive debt (including parks, cemeteries, hospitals &amp;c.,) per head of the 69 County boroughs was £7 3s. 7d., the figure for Warrington being £5 19s. 11d. The averag rates in 1905 of the County Boroughs referred to was 75. Bd. in the P„ Warrington's rates now being 7s. 4d. Warrington had been very much criticised by a gentleman who came from Manchester and who had done a great deal of work in that city. He (Mr. England) thought, however that that speaker who called away his audience by his oratory must have very often forgotten that he was in Warrington and imagined that he was in Manchester, because the debt per head in Manchester was £35 Os. Bd. In Liverpool the amount was £l9 123. Bd. The chief cause of the increase of IVarrington's rates a few years ago was the Education Rate,-the rates in 1902 being 6s. 6d. in the £,-and the cause of the reduction during the past two years was increased gas profits, electric light and tramway profits, the saving of £l,OOO ley more economical management of the tram- ways, and increased assessments and decreased expendi- ture in several departments. The liabilities of the borough amounted to £1,263,258 17s. 6d. which was secured on assets totalling £1,692,867 18s. 30., thus leaving a balance to the credit of the town of £429,609 Os. qd. It might be said that no one would give such a sum for our assets, but there were plenty of financiers in this country who would be only too glad, if they had the opportunity of taking over the town on the above figures, to work and manage it. He thought his hearers would agree that comparing Warrington with other town-for after all, in common fairness to ourselves that was the true test-Warrington could justly claim to be at least, taking one year with another, coraraercially speaking A WELL-GOVERNED TOWN. Referring to the remunerative condition of Corporation undertakings, Mr. England said he realized that a good many people did not believe in municipalising under- takings, but personally he did and he was glad to per- ceive that the trend of the times was to go further in that direction. Having read an extract from an article with reference to the municipal government of Berlin, Mr. England said that city's affairs were managed by experts. It was men of business experience who were wanted on English local authorities-not any men, but I men who understood the wants and requirements of a community and were able in a practical way to bring 'them to fruition. Mr. England then quoted figures as to the cost of the various Corporation departments to the town, pointing out that the gas works made a profit of £14,181 19s. Bd. • electricity, £2,762 19s. 7d. ; tram- ways, £162 7s. 4d. markets, £2,500 ; and Parr Hall, £146 9s. 10d. Continuing, Mr. England said that for STREET WIDENING purposes the Local Government Board allowed 50 and 60 years for repayment of loans, generally the latter period. This meant a charge on the rates in respect of each £l,OOO required with interest for one year, on the fifty years repayment, of £42 12s. 11d, or on the 60 years' repayment of £4O is. 10d. A penny rate produced on an average £915 so that if the Corporation was allowed 60 years to repay a loan that Id. rate would carry a capital sum for town purposes of £23,009 on the 60 years scale, £21,000 on the fifty years Beale, and £16,000 on the thirty years scale. In other words the Corporation could borrow £23,000 for street widening pnrnoses and it would only entail a charge of id. rate if 60 years were allowed. Continuing Mr. England argued strongly in favour of the provision of a • CATTLE MARKET which he considered would be of immense benefit not only as a contribution to the rates but as causing more money to be spent in the town. One authority on the subject had assured him that such a market would make a profit of £2,000 a year. Alluding to the question of THE BIHTLES PROPERTY - -</p>
        <p>_ Mr. England said he was sotry certain reports had been circulated. Who, he asked, should know more about the transaction than the late Mr. Garnett's son? Apart from that gentleman's statement, however, he (Mr. England) knew that Councillor Brough, who had been very ill, had said he was sorry that he could not attend the Council meeting when the matter was discussed as he could have more than confirmed every word Coun- cillor Wilkinson said. As a matter of fact. Councillor Brough along with other gentlemen had the second refusal of the property. The late Mr. Garnett was always a man of his word and it redounded to his credit that he stuck to his old apprentice and friend and very I properly considered him in the matter. It was common knowledge that Mr. Garnett had given Mr. Birtles his word that he should have the option many years ago. I He (Mr. England) was not going to deny Mr. Lowe's statement that he was acting for Mr. Garnett, but he knew that Mr. Garnett went to a leading tradesman and offered the property, stating, however, that he had given Mr. Birtles the preference. Mr. Hesketh eon- firmed the statefnent that he approached Mr. Garnett and that the latter said ; "No. I want Tom to take it. No one shall have it, till he has refused it." Mr. Birtles had actually been offered a larger sum for the Educa- tion Office than he received from the Corporation but had refused it, and he also waived a proviso in the original agreement with the Corporation that when the , street was made he could call upon the town to I pull down the rate offices so that everything should be in line. That showed that Mr. Birtles had some consideration for the town. He (Mr. England thought that if the Education Offices were converted into ordinary offices, a large income could be derived from them. Although there had been an outlay of £4,494 he contended that owing to the increased rates from the property, the improvement would not only not have costl,he town'a penny,but would boa source of increasing income as the years wont on. With regard to street widening, he (Mr. England) had suggested to the Borough Treasurer that if the property from Corporation-street to Market-gate and along Horsemarket-street and Buttermarket-street for a certain distance were pulled down, and GO years were allowed for the repayment of the cost of purchase, con- sidering the increased assessment it would be found when the sixty years had expired that the improvement had paid for itself. Mr. Fairhurst told him that he believed it would be found when the sixty years had Passed, that the improvement of Bridge-street from Rylands-street to Market-gate would not have cost the town a penny. Mr. England spoke OF THE HEALTH OF THE TOWN as being n grave matter. Dr. Gornall in his annual Ireport had pointed out that the death rate of Warring- ton was considerably higher than that of the other I towns of England and Wales : for every 1,000 persons II who died in England and Wales 1199 died in Warrington [This town, he added, was therefore of a character not so conducive to healthiness as most others, the fact not being apparently in great measure due t,o its position, but rather related to industrial conditions, and the associated overcrowding in noisesome slums, the care- lessness and ignorance of mothers, and to other like influences. Mr. England contended that some depart- ments had been neglectful of their duty in regard to health condition', and said that some courts where I people lived had not been paved at all. Continuing he said he thought the time had come when the</p>
        <p>• .-,.. =,-'•••'..•..7.1:::...."-'...7..';';".':--.7'''':. ..•.%..'.77.:.17-;:.'..,..';.'..,t'';:...,.:'e:•..;';,..,...::.:,..:,;,7:_7-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WHOLE FAMILY BENEFITS.</head>
        <p>A WHOLE FAMILY BENEFITS.</p>
        <p>Cuts, Burns, Scalp Disease, Poisoned Sores, Pains, &amp;c., Yield to Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>I Mrs. A. Y. Griffiths, of 32, Liverpool Lawn, West Cliff, Ramsgate, has met with some interesting proofs of Zara-Buk's wide range of usefulness in the home. She said to a local reporter "My little daughter suffered dreadfully with scalp disease for about six years. I first noticed an eruption on her scalp when she was only eleven months old. It developed into running sores, and I failed to check the trouble at all. The doctor too, failed to do any good, and I then began to go to different chemists, trying all sorts of ordinary ointments, medicines, etc. I bad to keep her head shaved, and the irritation was so bad that sleep was often out of the question for us both. I was told that no cure could be found for her, but eventually I tried Zam-Buk. The balm soothed the irritation, and she was soon sleeping beautifully each night. I dressed the scalp carefully and regularly with Zam-Buk for some time. The sores dried up at last, and finally the dry, thick, scaly skin disappeared, to reveal a clean, sweet, healthy scalp. Her hair began to grow again nicely, and now, thanks entirely to Zam- Buk, she is absolutely well. "My elder daughter had a had leg for some time, and one week's treatment with Zatn-Buk completely cured it. Through dressing this leg I got a poisoned hand myself, and one finger was so bad that the doctor informed me he would be compelled to draw the nail to save the first joint. However, I applied Zam-Buk, and within ten days the finger was perfectly healed. "My husband thinks Zara-Buk most valuable for swollen and painful toe joints, and I have used it suc- cessfully among my other children for cuts, bruises, sores, growing pains, etc. I have a lot of little ones, and Zam-Buk has been quite a boon to me. Our home shall never be without the useful little box of Zam-Buk" Follow the wise example of the Griffiths family. Winter is close at hand ; and a budget of trouble from "chaps "after washing day, sores, chafings and burns, and other superficial injuries call for Zam-Buk being constantly at hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Photographic Society.</head>
        <p>The Photographic Society.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of: the above society- at the Old Academy on Tuesday evening, a highly interesting lecture on " Picture making " was delivered by Mr. Walter J. Pearce, artist in stained glass and decora- tive painting. It was illustrated with lantern slides and proved instructive and entertaining. Mr. Cr. F. Taylor (president), presided over a fair attendance and thanked the members of the society for electing him president for the ensuing yea/. It was a high compliment to him, and he hoped he would prove a worthy successor to Mr. Mathias. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
