      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>TECHNICAL DAY SCHOOL</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL DAY SCHOOL</p>
        <p>should be free. The Corporation only received £220 Is. lld, in fees from it, and a great deal more than that could be saved by transferring the gas offices from Mersey-street to Sankey-street. Mr. England concluded by urging the improvement of the surroundings of the people and the bettering of their condition. (Applause.) Questions having been invited, Mr. CHRIMES said he was mach disappointed with what Mr. England had put before them. It was very much beside the mark. He understood that Mr. England invited them there to try and allay the excite- ment and unpleasantness which had arisen about the town's affairs, but he seemed to have carefully avoided all those points which had caused the agitation. The main objects of auditing were to detect irregularities, inaccuracies or incompetency if any existed, and he would like to know if Mr. England had succeeded in finding anything which gave colour to any statements made by the Non-Traders. There was the question of the Lawson contract. If Mr. England had gone care- fully though the borough accounts and had had access to all the documents and vouchers, as he was supposed to have under the Municipal Corporations Act, he (Mr. Chrimes) would like to knew if he had found whetuer there was any justification for the complaints made by the Non-Traders about alleged irregular payments or if he knew how much had been paid on account of the contract up to the end of the period covered by his audit. He would also like to know whether Mr. England had any dilficulty in seeing all the documents and papers which were necessary in order to make an effective audit. Several Councillors said they had never seen the Lawson contract—that they had asked and been refused. He would ask Mr. England whether he had seen any contracts, or whether he thought he had any right to see them. It was some- what outside the province of the meeting that he should allude to the death-rate and Dr. Gornall's report. Mr. ENGLA.ND : .The rates are affected by your death rate.</p>
        <p>Mr. CIIRINIES t If you consider it is relevant, all right. I would like to ask why the Council has neglec- ted to put into operation the Public Health Act with reference to smoke abatement.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. IsnEnwoon asked what the cost of the war between the traders and the Ship Canal Company was, and what sum the Corporation was mulcted in. They were assured by the Mayor that it would cost nothing. Another ME:NITER of the audience asked why the Lawson contract had risen from £24,500 to £72,000. Mr. ENGL A.ND said that during tbe period covered by the audit the cost to the town of the Ship Canal action had been £2,021 14s. 3d. He could not give. the total cost. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. ISHERWOOD : I believe the full amount is something like £B,OOO. Mr.BitansirAw : Are we going to get any of that back? The Mayor said the money would be refunded. Mr. ENGLAND : Yes, that was stated. Referring to the Lawson contract Mr. England said that during the , time covered by the audit not a penny had been paid to Mr. Lawson. He contended that the Council had blundered in their management of the dispute, because under the agreement it was stated that in case of a dispute either the Corporation or Mr. Lawson should call upon the County Court Judge to appoint an arbitrator. In the first place the Council should not have made such a great noise publicly when the bill came in but should have calmly considered it as businessmen. They should have left it to their legal adviser, the Town Clerk, and they would have practically fastened Mr. Lawson down to his first bill. Then they could have offered a certain sum in discharge and if he ref used it, the matter should have gone to arbitration. As it was an Investigation Committee was appointed and there was a great deal of talk. He believed it was in con- sequence of the blundering of the Corporation in its management of the matter that they had had the second bill. The auditors, continued Mr.England,found no inaccuracies in the accounts, except that in one case there was a penny wrong and that was a matter of addition. The book keeping system was exception ally good and had been copied by other towns. The CHAIRMAN said that with regard to the smoke nuisance, some of the manufacturers who were the greatest sinners were exempted by law and that was probably the reason why the firms who only sinned in a minor degree were not I called to account. The Lawson contract appeared to have been a most unfortunate affair and he was sorry ' the extras had not been challenged at the proper time. He believed the only effective way of managing Corporation affairs was to have a head official directly responsible to the Council and exercising control over all departments. Councillor BALL said he considered the Corporation ought to appoint a thoroughly qualified accountant to check the accuracy of the figures and then the elective auditors could conffne themselves to points of con- troversy such as the Lawson contract. The chief lack in municipal undertakings was that f one directing head to organize and control all the departments. Economy could be effected if the accounts of the gas department were directly under control of the Borough Treasurer. There was a great dud in the gas offfce, with its immense detail work,that if it were reorganized on a modern system there would be a considerable saving. He coacluded by moving a vote of thanks to Mr. England. Mr. EDWIN BRA.DSHAAV seconded and the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. FRANCIS DIXON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. FRANCIS DIXON.</p>
        <p>Suffocated by Gas. Another familiar figure has passed away in the person of Mr. Francis Dixon who at one time played a prominent part in Warrington's public affairs. 11,.! was seventy-seven years of age and for some time had been in a rather weak state. He resided with his son at 91; Folly-lane, and on Saturday night retired to rest as usual. On going to bed later, Mr. Dixon, jun., looked into his father's bedroom and everything appeared to be all right. Aeseven o'clock on Sunday morning, however, he detected a smell of gas and on going to his father's bedroom found the place full of the poisonous fumes. Mr. Dixon was unconscious and although artificial respiration wss applied he had passed away when Dr. Smith, who was immediately summoned, arrived upon the scene. The news of Mr. Dixon's tragic end created the greatest regret in tlie town where he was highly esteemed by a very large,' circle. A native of Coxwold, Yorkshire, his early business life was spent in Manchester. About the year 1885 he came to Warrington and took over the management of the galvanizing department at the works of Messrs. Pylands Bros., Ltd. By his ability l and conscientious devotion to duty he earned the !confidence and respect of his employers, and the late Mr. Peter Rylauds, HP,, was wont to speak of him in the highest terms. After serving the firm for over twenty years Mr. Dixon commenced business on his own account as a metal merchant. Latterly he had , lived in retirement. Mr. Dixon was a staunch Liberal , and an old election campaigner, and took a foremost ' part in the 1868 election. He entered the Town ICouncil after a contest as representative of the North- East Ward, and held the office for twelve years. He ; was warmly attached to the Wesleyan denomination, ! and for a considerable period regularly attended Bold- street Church, of which he was one of the oldest , members. He was of a kindly disposition, and lie r will be much missed by many who had enjoyed his friendship.</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST. Mr. S. Brighouse held the inquest in the Coroner's Court at the Police Station on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. Bailey was the foreman of the jury. John Featherstone Dixon, veterinary sergeon, son of the deceased, said his father lived with him. He was a retired metal merchant. Deceased went to bed at seven o'clock on Saturday night, and witness saw him then. There was no light in the room. At one o'clock on Sunday morning witness retired to rest, but, as usual, before doing so looked into his father's room. His father was breathing all right and there was no smell of gas in the room. At about seven o'clock witness got up and detected a smell of gas. He went to his father's room where the smell was much stronger. He opened the window and turned off the gas, which had not been lighted. Finding his father unconscious he tried artificial respiration, but without success, and when Dr. Smith arrived he stated that life was extinct. Witness was of opinion that some time during the night deceased got up and having turned on the gas went with apiece of paper to the hall gas for a light. The hall gas was out and witness thought his father went back to bed, shut the door, and forgot to turn off the gas. He had been weak and absent-minded for some time.. _ _</p>
        <p>In answer to the Coroner witness said his father had no financial worry and there was nothing whatever to trouble him. Dr. Smith said he had known dpeeasnd for some years and latterly he had been n failing health. especially as regarded his mental faculties. There was nothing in the way of melancholia ; his brain was suffering from the effects of senile decay. Death was due to asphyxia, the result of ' the inhalation of coal gas. The jury returned a verdict of" Death from mis- adventure "and expressed their sympathy with Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Dixon in their bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took- P-l-E;ce ll:iTaiwriTat the Warrington Cemetery on Wednesday. Prior to the interment a service was held in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, the Rev. J. Manning officiating. The chief mourners were : Messrs. John Featherstone Dixon, Thomas NV. Dixon (sons),Joseph Beardsall,J.B.Beardsall, S. Beardsall and J. Morecroft (nephews), and John Mushan (cousin). Amongst those also present were :—Councillor Barton, Messrs. E. J. Guest, Lunt, J. Hall and J. Johnson. The Rev. J. Manning also officiated at the graveside. In a brief address at the church the Rev. J. Manning said the deceased gentleman was one of the old company of Methodists, unfortunately fast dwindling away, who had been associated with Bold-street Church from its very foundation. He was active not only in Church life, but in political liie, and as a Liberal had played a prominent part in the political life of the town. ()I Mr. Dixon it could be truly written that he lived not entirely for himself, but tiled to do something for others. He served his day and his generation.</p>
      </div>
