      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKS' BOWLING ASSOCIATION. -- Presentation of Prizes.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS' BOWLING ASSOCIATION. -- Presentation of Prizes.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that on the occasion of the celebration of the Nelson Centenary last year Alderman. Bennett offered prizes to students at the Secondary school, for essays on the life of Nelson. These were won by Miss Pattie Woodcock, daughter of the Assist- ant borough treasurer,and Master Stanley E. Jones, 192, Wilderspool Causeway, and both received their rewards at the hands of the donor on Tuesday. The proceedings which took place at the Secondary School were under the presidency of Mr. E. S. A. Robson, (principal) who was supported by the Rev. John Youge, Alderman Bennett,and Mr. Moore Murray (director of education). Alderman BENNEST said his idea in presenting the prizes was to encourage an intelligent love of their country and a high spirit of national duty. The prize essays that he had seen seemed to be well-written, and in good English, and gave an excellent account of the life of Lord Nelson, but he should like to make one criticism, and that was that the moral lessons from Nelson's life had been forgotten in dealing with the facts. Alderman Bennett went on to speak of Nelson's strong, underlying sense of duty, his fearlessness, and his higher regard for honour than wealth. True honour predominated far above riches. This was an age in which they were apt to worship riches, and if the people of to-day had any idol at all it was a golden idol, and like the children of Israel they bowed down before the golden calf. He was sorry that he (the speaker) had been unable to get as prizes books concerning their duty to their town. The Beautiful Warrington Society Wns an institution through which they were enabled to accom- plish much on behalf of their town ; and as they grew up he hoped they would learn to have the interest of their town at heart, by engaging themselves in some practical way in matters calculated to promote the town's welfare. (Applause.) He surprised a Liverpool audience on Monday night by telling them that War- rington was the Athens of England, but he proved to them that in days gone by it was a centre of intellect and culture, and earned the title. He hoped that the rising generation would work towards the re-attain- ment of that position. (Applause.) The books presented to Miss Woodcock were Southey's "Life of Nelson," " Deeds which won the Empire." "The Expansion of England," and Rev. Canon Stevenson's " Spiritual Teaching of the Holy while those given to Master Jones consisted of " The Life of Nelson," " Harold," " Fights for the Flag," and "The Expansion of England." On the proposition of the Rev. J. YONGE, seconded by Mr. ROBSON, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Alderman Bennett for his gift of the prizes. During the proceedings the pupils, to the accompani- ment of their own orchestra, sang "The death of Nelson," "Chesapeake and the Shannon," " Rule Britannia," and the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>r ring t oona WTa rhrei npgr e Monday evening, the attendant proceedings being of an enjoyable character. The gathering was held at the oz n wWoonr kisn ' connection Association were thedistributed Bowling Green Inn, Bank Quay, Mr. J. Willett presid- ing over a good attendance. Mr. Willett was also called upon to present the prizes which he did after exnressing his satisfaction at the continued progress of , the Association. Mr. G. F. FREEMAN, in proposing "The Warrington Works Bowling Association, ' said he did not think there was an association within 100 miles that presented such good prizes for a handicap. (Hear, hear.) He proposed that the best thauks be accorded to the officers of the Association for the excellent way in which they had done their duty. (Applause.) They had a good committee, with Mr. W. H. Smalley as secretary, and Mr. J. Holbrook as treasurer and they had attended to show that they were interested in the welfare of the working men of Warrington. (Hear, hear and applause) The toast was received with musical honours.</p>
        <p>The CH.tißmAx, in reply, said the Association was now four four years old, and although they had had a few misfortunes, he did not think they were going to see the old firm sink yet. At the present time there were more firms subscribing than ever, and some were subscribing who had never subscribed before.(Applause) During the evening songs were given in a creditable manner by Messrs. J. Houghton (winner of the first prize), G. Ball, jun., R. Rae, and N. Cook. Mr. G. Lomas accompanied on the piano. Light refreshments were provided.</p>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST. The prize winners were :-1, J. Houg'aton ; 2, A. Gleave ; 3, E. Shaw ; 4, A. Shaw; 5, J. Appleton ; 6, J, Massey ; 7. W. Ball ; 8, J. Simister ; 9, T. Ellison ; 10. W. Shaw ; 11, J. Hutchinson 12, W. Smellie ; 13, Joe Gleavo ; 14, E. Gleave ; 15, G. Wilcock ; 16, G. Ball, sen.. 17. W. Jones ; 18, It. Hitchmore ; 19, R. Marrow ; 20, G. Clive ; 21, C. 'Holden ; 22, J. Andrews; 23, W. Randalls ; 24, W. Gleave ; 25, W. Lawton : 26, F. Mills; 27, I. Makin; 28, J. Woods ; 23, T. Harrison ; 30, J. Shaw ; 31, H. Watkins ; 32, P. Hankinson ; 33. J. Willett ; 34, T. Lee ; 35, W. Millman ; 36, 11. Daintith; 37, G. Warburton ; 38, J. T. Bartley ; 39, J. Timmins ; 40, D. Gibsen ; 41, R. Edginton ; 42, A. Middleton ; 43, W. Holloway; 41, T. N. Fielding; 45, James Woods; 45, J. Hardman ; 47, F. Willoughby ; 48, 1. Deakin ; 49, W. Daniels ; 50, Ted Gleave. After presenting the prizes the CHAIRMAN said that fifty prizes for a handicap " wanted some finding." He thought that all would be satisfied, as when they got through the second round they received a prize of the value of :is. (Hear, hear.) As long as he (the speaker) had anything to do with it he thought the association would go on increasing. (Applause.) He would never forget the old club, and would always do his " level best " for it. (Applause.)</p>
