      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ONE DEATH LEADS TO ANOTHER.</head>
        <p>ONE DEATH LEADS TO ANOTHER.</p>
        <p>Two deaths occurred within a few minutes of each other on Saturday night on the North-Eastern Railway at North Seaton. Robert Bercan, of Abington, in attempting to jump from a train while in motion fell on the line and was literally eat in two. In consequence of this accident another train on North Seaton viaduct had to be storpcd. Thinking the station bad been reached a miner named Thomas Green opened the carriage doOr, stepped out, and fell over the parapet to the river bank below, a distance of ninety feet, dislocatizg his neck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>Chapter 8</p>
        <p>Disinfect and cleanse</p>
        <p>Doing two good things at once is good, if each is well done.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha both cleanses and dis- infects at the same time.</p>
        <p>It does more too. It saves wash-day' ccal 13,-.cause if does best work with cold water.</p>
        <p>You may use warm water in very cold weather, for comfort's sake, but it is not necessary except for comfort. Get your money back from the i dealer f not s'atistied.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHY BY LIGHTNING.</p>
        <p>A remarkable instance of what may be called photography by lightning is reported from the Italian town of Udine. A young girl named Zorzi had taken refuge from a thunderstorm under a mulberry tree, which was struck by lightning, and she sustained some slight injury. It was afterwards found that there remained on her heft arm a clear impression of a branch of the tree. It was on what may be called a yellow- ish ground, the skin having been turned to that tint, but the impression itself was bluish—prob- ably electric blue. It is as ineradicable as tattoo marks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>CIIARTING• RIVER BOTTOMS.</head>
        <p>CIIARTING• RIVER BOTTOMS.</p>
        <p>rNst ingenious apparatus for drawing the pro- file of a river bottom is used by Italian engi- neers. It consists of a wheel affixed to the bot=tom of a graduated rod, which is fastened verti- cally at the side of a boat in such a manner that as the boat advances up Cr down or across stream the wheel at the end of the rod runs continually upon the bottom, the rod rising and falling with variations of level. By noting the depth at chosen intervals of time, the elements are obtained for tracing an accurate representa- tion of the sinuosities of the bottom of the river, and the variations of depih. In the sav- ing of time this simple apparatus possesses an enormous advantage over the ordinary method of soundings.</p>
        <p>WINDMILLS FOR ELECTRIC CURRENTS.</p>
        <p>The use of windmills for generating electric currents has given favourable results in the tests for the Danish Government made at Askow by Professor La Cohn The four-wing wheel proves to be the best form. With such a wheel having 15 square yards of surface, he obtained 2 h.-p. from a wind of 20ft. per second, and 10 h.-p. from a wind of 35ft. per second. As reserve power, when there is no wind, a small petrol motor has been more satisfactory and less expensive than a storage battery. The Askow plant cost about £950, including land, and the annual expense of operation is £33. It feeds 450 incandescent lamps, with some arc lamps and motors. A smaller outfit, costing about £2OO, would be useful for farm purposes, and a num- ber of these windmill plants are already in Denmark. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>tACTERIA _OF THE SOIL.</head>
        <p>tACTERIA _OF THE SOIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. F. H. Gilbard, F.1.C., F.C. S., lecturing c.t Winchester House, London, before the Na- tional Amateur Gardeners' Association on the " Bacteria of the Soil," pointed out that without bacteria plant life would be practically impossible. Bacteria, said Mr. Gilbard, was at the root of the whole subject of agriculture. Bacteria were, in fact, essential to the healthy growth of flowers and vegetables, and before the amateur gar- dener could ...hope to see his plants take strong and vigorouis growth he must set himself to the culture of bacteria. Given only a sterile soil, even with plenty of water and fresh air, plant 111 3, said Mr. Gilbard, would soon wither and .die. The impression prevailed, except among the initiated, that manure would do all that was necessary for the gardener's purposes. That was erroneous, for until the manure rotted, provid- ing thereby a culture for bacteria, little or no benefit resulted to the plants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>RONTGEN RAYS.</head>
        <p>THE RONTGEN RAYS.</p>
        <p>Dr. V. H. Rutherford, M.P., in an article on the X-rays in Cassell' s Magazine, writes as fol- lows: In 1845 Professor Röntgen of Wfirzburg experimenting with a Crookes tube found that. a cardboard screen covered with crystals of barium , platin.xyanide became luminous within several inches of the Crookes tube. On interpos- ing his hand between the tube 'and the above screen, he saw that the shadow of the bones ap- peared on the screen. Using a photographic plate instead of the screen he found it affected by the rays, and a photograph of the skeleton produbed. Xurther, he discovered that the rays travelled through wood, ebonite, aluminium, &amp;c., but not through lead. With becoming modesty. he simply ,Aialled this new form of radiation X-rays. Fir a time it was thought that the rays might be accounted for by the same theory as light which comes from the sun, by to-and-fro oscillations of ether travelling with tremendous_velocity ; but, as the X-rays defy de- flection or polarisation, this theory no longer holds the field. At present, then, we see darkly; but the day may come when a great light will be shed upon 'this almost insoluble problem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LIQUID AIR IN COMMERCE.</head>
        <p>LIQUID AIR IN COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>We are now really promised liquid air on com- mercial terms. It is to be turned out by thou- sands of gallons per diem at one-sixth the pre- sent market price, with the probability of its ultimately being reduced to a fraction over id. per gallon. The result is arrived at purely by mechanical means, without added chemicals or other adventitious aid. The apparatus, we learn from the Times, is the invention of Mr. Knudson, a Dane. In his process atmospheric air is first of all , purified and freed from dust, and it is then compressed in a three-stage sir compressor to 2,5001 b. to the square inch. Liquid air, as an explosive for coal mines, has already been tested, and can be used with perfect safety. where fire damp or other explosive gases exist. It is also considerably cheaper than most other safety ex. plosives. Liquid oxygen in the steel and iron industry can be used for the welding of steel pipes, &amp;c. Oxygen and nitrogen can also be separated from liquid air and sold retail at ss. per gallon. The use of nitrogen for agricultural (manuring) purposes opens yet another field. The Maturing of liquors will also be helped by liquid air, as also the preservation and purifica- tion of milk. The use of liquid air as a motive power has been demonstrated to be quite practi- cable for small powers for a number of different purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>biAGNETIC DERRICK.</head>
        <p>A biAGNETIC DERRICK.</p>
        <p>An electro-magnetic derrick for handling iron and steel is a novel piece of machinery in use at Cleveland, Ohio. An. American official report says the magnet consists of a large iron disc sup- ported by chains, Which may be fastened to the 'hoop of a derrick or crane block. To the top of the disc is applied an • electric plug device con- nected with insulated wires, which by an in- genious auxiliary pulley arrangement are led to a controller at a convenient point at the base of the derrick or in the operator's cab. The disc is lowered over the material to be lifted. and the current turned on, and in this way enormous 'lads of material may be gathered together and ld by the Magnet as long as the current re- mains connected. Different forms of the disc are made for handling pig iron, heavy melting stock, such as crop ends, butts, steel risers, small cast- ings, tin scrap, whether loose or in bales, shear- ing. scrap, rod scrap; bolts, nuts, punches, ritet spikes, rail ends, machine borings, flats, sheets, and, in fact, almost any, form of iron or steel which affords a • surface sufficiently large for the magnet to act upon. When the ' material has been carried to the place where it is to be de- posited the cUrrent is turned off, and the magnet at once releases the load. Scrap tin has always been an ugly form of material to handle sati.)- factoriiy, hut by the thse of this device it may be disposed of as rapidly and as easily as heavier stock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>COLOUR – SENSE IN ANIMALS.</head>
        <p>COLOUR - SENSE IN ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>Some interesting experiments in reference to the sense of colour in animals are described by Dahl in the Yatunri.:senschaftliehe Worhen- schrift. The writer offered to a monkey sweets coloured with certain dye, and bitter sub. stances coloured with a different dye. . After a few attempts the monkey learnt to refuse the bitter submtances without trial, presumably. re- cognising them by their colour. By varying the experiments, it was found that the • a.nimai. could distinguish all colours except dark blue. And it is pointed out, on the authority of Mayer, that many savage tribes are unable, to distAiguish dark blue from black. Children also, it is said, are lonirer in being able to distinguish this colour than any other. • It would appear a natural inference of th 3 above that animals, lower in the scale of lifo would be less sensitive to colour than the mon- key. Darwin's- theory of sexual selection, on the other hand acquires that birds, fishes, and even in,ects should have a very keen •appreciation of colour. "For the brilliant colours frequently ex- hibited by these, which must otherwise be a disadvantage, are supposed to be the result of the appreciatioli and selection of the opposite sex. But the case of the hedge Sparrow, for ex- ample, has; suggested to some ornithologists that this bird is colour blind. For its brilliant .blue eggs are in marked contrast to the mottled prey of the cuckoo's, so often placed beside thorn. And yet, so far as observation goes, the hedge sparrow notices no difference.. • And while the • theory of sexual so' -ction among insects, and the view that we owe the beautiful colours of cur flowers, to their selective action, requires them to possess a colour senso at least equal to our own, there is some doubt as to whetner they appreciate colour at all. Professor Plateau, for example, has concluded. from an examination of their carious • compound eyes, as well as from careful observation of their. habits in the field, that they are colour blind.</p>
      </div>
