      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SOAP COMBINE.</head>
        <p>THE SOAP COMBINE.</p>
        <p>Developments Up to Date.</p>
        <p>So far as is known the firms within the Combine are as follows, but negotiations on the subject are still proceeding :—Vinolia Company, Joseph Watson and Sons, Joseph Crosfield and Sons, William Gossage and Sons, Tyson, Richmond and Jones, Lever Bros., Hodgson and Simpson, Edward Cook and Co., Christopher Thomas and Bros., J. Barrington and Co., Ogston and Tennant. These are the larger concerns, but over a third of the trade is outside the Combine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Mr. Lever's Position.</head>
        <p>Mr. Lever's Position.</p>
        <p>Interviewed in the House of Commons on Thursday afternoon, Mr. Lever informed a Press representative that there is not a word of truth in the report that he is to resign his seat in Parliament in order to devote himself to the Soap Crabine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>LETTER FROM MESSRS. JOSEPH CROSFIELD AND SONS.</head>
        <p>LETTER FROM MESSRS. JOSEPH CROSFIELD AND SONS.</p>
        <p>The Cause of the Combine.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR OF THE "EX.iIILNER.".I</p>
        <p>Sir,-:-The statements appearing in certain daily papers regarding the Soap Combine are so very mis- leading that perhaps a few words as to what are the real facts may be of interest to your readers. For some time now, owing to insufficient supplies there has been a steady and unprecedentedly rapid rise in the price of raw materials which has culminated at the present moment in what may veritably be called the "famine prices." The seriousness of the resulting situation, so far as the soap manufacturer is concerned, may be judged from the following table, which shows the fluctuations during the last two years in his prin- cipal raw material. I give the lowest price first, the hightest (to-day's) second and last what may be called the normal price over along period of years. Lowest. To-day. Normal. s. d. s. d. s. d. Tallow S.A. 25 0 31 6 24 0 per cwt. Cocoanut Oil 24 0 34 0 23 0 „ Palm Kernel Oil 23 6 34 0 22 0 Cotton Oil 13 0 23 6 16 0 Palm Oil 22 0 29 0 21 0 „ Rosin 8 0 12 0 7 0 For many years the retail selling price of household soaps has been based on the normal price of materials, and consequently when materials are high the soap- maker gets little or no profit. At famine prices, such as obtain to-day, he is fortunate if he avoids losing money. Last August ba fore the Combine was formed a general advance was made in the price of soaps. The advance we made in our leading specialities was 235. 9d. nett per ton, and I may mention that the retailer in many cases has since advanced his price id. per tablet, which equals .£4 13s. 4d. per ton. The prices fixed in August are those in force to-day, and conse- nently to say that the Combine has raised prices is a deliberate mis-statement. AN a matter of fact. but for the savings effected by working together and some favourable contracts for materials still running, we should have been obliged before this to again raise our prices, and it is only a question of time before we are compelled to do so if raw materials maintain their present level. High prices of raw materials are the last thing, the soapmaker wants but, unfortunately, this is a matter out of his control and I am afraid we are in for a period of high prices for some very considerable time to come, for we have to reckon with the fact that increasing quantities of tallow, cocoanut oil and cotton oil are now used for edible purposes. So far from our soaps being supplanted by outside firms we have had an altogether unprecedented demand for soaps this autumn, and this demand is not yet over though the slack season of the year for the soapmaker is at hand.</p>
        <p>I think what I have stated will show how little reliance can be placed on any statements appearing in the daily press concerning our trade.—Yours J. J. CROSFIELD. October 26th, 190 G.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, Missionary Anniver- sary: Preachers, 10.30 Rev. E. J. Gawne ; 6.30, Rev. W. Mellor.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church : Preacher, 10.30 and 6.30, Rev. J. Yonge. Evening subject." Jesus the Divine rest giver:, Bold-street Wesleyan Church : Preachers, 10.30 Rev. J. Manning ; 6.30, Rev. S. Pitt, Golborne-street Baptist Church Bible Class Anniver- sary : Preacher, Rev. G. J. Cliff°, Loughborough. Subject 10.30, "The Book of books " ; 6.30," The fatal flaw." - _ _</p>
        <p>--Latchford Baptist Church : Preacher, Rev. J. F. Matthews. Subject 10.30, "Co-operative service 6.0," Co-operative prayer." Gospel flail, Foster-street : Special addresses at 10.30 and 6.30 by Mr. W. Macfarline, evangelist. Mr. Macfarline will conduct a mission throughout the week and until November oth, the services to be held each evening at 7.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Wycliffe Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at this Church on Sunday, when services of thanksgiving for the safe ingathering of the harvest were held. The preacher was the Rev. G. W. Gervis, of Golborne,who drew some admirable lessons from the harvest. lb the evening the choir gave an effective interpretation of " 0 Lord, how manifold " (Barnhy). Mr. W. El. Payton presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Padgate Wesleyan Church. Completion of the New Tower.</head>
        <p>Padgate Wesleyan Church. Completion of the New Tower.</p>
        <p>i</p>
        <p>al.</p>
        <p>Padgate Wesleyans are greatly indebted to the Bennett family who for many years have most gener- ously supported the church. The latest girt has been made by Mr. William Bennett who provided the neces- sary funds to improve the atanearance of the building by the addition of a handsome tower. The erection of this was recently completed and on Thursday, at special services which were held, it was dedicated. The pro- ceedings were rendered additionally interesting by being combined with the annual missionary meeting. In the afternoon a large congregation assembled to hear a sermon by the Rev. Dr. Moulton. whilst in the evening there was a crowded attendance for the annual mAeting. On this occasion Mr. William Bennett pm- sided and was supported by the Revs. Dr. Moulton, T. F. Nicholson and W. Mellor.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, who was nccorded a very hearty. recep- tion, spoke strongly in advocacy of foreign missions. The interest he had taken in this branch of the church's work had been stimulated by the extraordinary outburst of enthusiasm and generosity at the recent conference at Nottingham and he would like that inspira- tion to spread over the whole of the churches of the land. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The Rev. Dr. Moulton, haring spoken in high terms of the chairman's generosity,also sepported missionary work. He appealed for a larger patriotism than that of which they often heard and which consisted of a desire to paint the earth's surface red. He would remind those whose hearts warmed at the thought of the greatness of our empire and who were impressed with its dignity and responsibility that the British empire was not the only great empire the world had seen and he would ask what about the permanence of our position ? Was the day coming when our country was going to be nothing bnt a memory of forgotten greatness. Rome fell because of her selfishness, and if we wanted to preserve our empire from the same fate we must preserve it from selfishness. If the day came when our imperialism became simply a desire to gain power then as surely would the day come when our nation and empire would only be one more name added to the long list of those nations whose supreme power had for ever passed away from them. What would be the thing by which the British people would be remembered when the world had entered into a greater understanding of God? It was the work which the nation had done in spreading the knowledge of the Gospel of Christ. The British and Foreign Bible Society was a possession which would be remembered when kings and warriors were forgotten and the work which the nation had done for the cause of Jesus Christ would outlive every other of the nations achievements. (Applause). An interestim,s address on Mission work in India was given by the Rev. T. F. Nicholson whose personal reminiscences aroused and maintained the interest of his auditors. _</p>
        <p>The collections were on behalf of the Foreign Missionary Society.</p>
