      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church Finances. Sir,—Mr. Jones' statement, in reply to my letter which appeared in your issue of September 2Sth, calls for very little comment, as he tacitly admits the exist- ence of funds other than the one upon which there was a deficiency, i.e. the " general fund." I think it must be evident to all that the expression "church finances" was therefore, misapplied in the circular which called , forth my first letter. The facts are these :—The Church is responsible for all debts incurred by all the organisations in connection with it and in consequence alt funds have latterly been placed with one common treasurer, "the church treasurer." Therefore all these funds constitute "Church financrg," and while I adrnit a deficiency on the "general fund "there were funds in hand in the- other accounts at the date I mentioned which nearly balanced this deficiency. I now say "nearly balanced" as old out- standing accounts have since been presented of which I had not the slightest cognisance when Mr. Donnie° made the statement in Parr Hall, referred to in my previous letter.—Yours faithfullv, ARTHUR P. FREEMAN. 137, Liverpool-road. Warrington. October 25th 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Election Issues</head>
        <p>The Election Issues</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is of the greatest importance that the rate- payers should clearly perceive what are the issues to be decided on the first of November next. The political organisations cannot be used for the purpose of defeat- ing Non-Trading candidates just because they are Non- Traders -vitlaout degrading those organisations. For ' the issue is not political, it is above all parties. The cause of good government and disinterestedness in public life is the cause of Christianity reduced to practice in public life. Yet Non-Traders have been accused of being actuated by animus and bringing the town into disrepute. When policemen bring a great number of offenders before the magistrates what should we think of a man who told us that the police were actuated by animus and bringing the town into disrepute? It is not the Non-traders but the proceedings and irregular- ities of the Corporation that have disgraced the town. Then again we are told that Non-Traders have hindered progress. The Non-Traders have hot hindered the pro- gress of the town, but they have checked extravagance and brought to light irregularities. The important question for the ratepayers to consider is how to improve the methods of the Corporation and to promote good government for that is of vital import- ance to the welfare of the town.—Yonrs &lt;to., S. KENYON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Mr. Morton and the I.L.P.</head>
        <p>Mr. Morton and the I.L.P.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l note that" Gasworker "still professes to be unconvinced that the so-called Liberal Labour members are free agents and asks why then, have they not acted independently? To this I reply that they hare acted independently and for "Gasworker" to suggest the con- trary is a reflection on the Unions that send and pay them. But if your correspondent and his friends had come and heard Mr. Havelock Wilson, M.P., they would at least have been able to judge for themselves from information given at first hand whether there is any " independence "in the much derided Liberal Labour members. I will be bound to say that they would not have left the hall without a sense of shame at the base ingratitude they continually display towards men who have given the best part of their lives to the service of their fellow-workers. Then " Gasworker " asks " why have they attached themselves to the Liberal party ?" This I have already answered. It is not so much that these men "attach themselves " as that Liberalism naturally takes them in just as the new Labour section is absorbed by the Liberal party to which it gives its support on all important items of legislation. The third question put by " Gasworker" is "Why they (the Liberal Labour members) have barefacedly supported Liberal capitalist candidates who have trodden on the workers when a living wage was demanded ?" I reply, for much the same reason that the Socialists barefacedly supported a Socialist capitalist candidate in the person of Mr. flyndman at Burnley; because, although a capitalist, be professed to be in sympathy with the aims of the Socialist party, and having money at his command he would be in a position to assist the moverpent more effectually than a Socialist Labour candidate. And it should be remembered that no dis- tinction can be drawn between Socialist, Liberal or Conservative capitalists in respect to the question of "treading "on the workers when a living wage was demanded ; they are all under, the same economic laws and all equally guilty either directly or indirectly of treading on the worker. The most ludicrous argument against the independ- ency of the Liberal Labour members is " Gasworker's " reference to the fact that before the election Mr. Fenwick, M.P.:, was prevented from attending a Pro- gressive Labour League meeting by the crack of the Liberal Whip. The party Whip is simply a device for making party action more effective. Any member can disobey the Whip but naturally his party allegiance would prompt him to respond to the party call excepting under very special circumstances. In Mr. Fenwick's case his vote in the House was more important than his engagement in Warrington and this our League recognised. By the way, has not the Independent Labour section a Whip? And is it " Gasworker's " idea that when their Whip is "cracked" the real and only genuine members immediate- ly scatter themselves far and wide just to show their own "independence" and to ridicule an official who would curtail it ? The Liberal Labour members could do this only they have more sense. "Gasworker's" remarks on the respective merits from a Labour point of view of the Liberal and Conservative parties show, I think, that he entirely fails to grasp the true meaning of the terms. Yours truly, JOHN MORTON, Hon. Sec., Progressive Labour League.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Camp at Prestatyn. What the Boys Think.</head>
        <p>The Camp at Prestatyn. What the Boys Think.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l feel that a few extracts from letters received from some of the lads who were out at Camp for the last week, sent in response to an offer of a prize for the best, may be of interest to your readers. Most of the letters took the form of descriptions of Life at Camp or appreciations of the time the boys had had, and the discrepancies between the various accounts are in many cases distinctly amusing. The journey to Prestatyn was evidently much enjoyed and Warrington people will be pleased to note the impression made by the country near home. " The first station we stopped at was Frodsham, and the scenery on our way was most beautiful," as one boy says. The hour of arrival at Camp seems to be a matter of unoertainty, varying in different accounts between 5 and 7 p.m, but there was no doubt as to the tea they found awaiting them, which is recalled by most and unanimously approved. The first evening great excitement reigned, and although one account states that ".at half-past eight we had our supper and went to bed, the beds were very cosy and we were all soon sound asleep," yet if ques- tioned the officers would probably agree with the boy who says that " We got very little sleep on account of being in strange beds,"or the more extreme statement "We had no sleep that night, and were up and dressed about four o'clock in the morning," because, as we learn, "we were anxious to see what was going on outside." So with the officers ! (It was about 3.30 a.m. and a grey morning). Though walks x•tere a great feature of the holiday and same good anefr were taken, nobody, to the chief officer's knowledge, ever accomplished the feat claimed by one lad of having walked "round the Welsh Mountains." It is very gratifying to find from the letters how much impression was made en the children by the natural beauties of the country,there being many miter- ences to the waterfall of Dyserth,the mountains and the extensive view obtained from them, arid a fine sunset we had one evening. Bathing was enjoyed twice , one lad however found a single (rose sufficient : " As soon as I got in T got a mouth-full of salt water, which did not taste very nice so I came out and dressed." The shore was a splendid playground and the children were always happy there with spades and buckets, wading, and finding shells and crabs, that were frequently stowed away in the pocket with a view to taking home. When it was wet the large Pavilion, given this year by Lady Greenall. was invaluable as a playroom, and games were played there till the weather cleared, which it always conveniently did in time for meals. The meals were very simple but gave great satisfaction, the whistle announcing them always being greeted with uproarious cheers, and the quality of the food was much appreciated, nearly every letter containing mention of one or more dishes that were considered particularly excellent,suc-la as "Meat and potatoes," "Bean soup," and the" Cocoa for breakfast." The toffy shop opened only after dinner was a great event of the day,and the value of a bank for the safe keeping of what pennies the lads had brought with them was occasionally impressed by the loss of acoia or two that had not been safely deposited. It is very delightful to see from frequent remarks in the letters how much the boys cared to have the o'ficers play games with theni, and many great matches of cricket were played between tea and supper against a side captained by our redoubtable cook, a personage of great importance, now well know to some of the boys from previous years at camp. "A man came running down the line," as one boy says recounting the events of the first evening (probably bran.lishing a stick) " telling us to be quiet and listen to what our officers said who should it be hut our old friend the cook who used to play cricket with us." On Sunday the day of return, the camp was in a fine state of excitement, but " we went to Church, the Catholics went to the Roman Catholic Church, while the rest went to Prestatyn end Menden Churches." Though glad to be going home all but two out of one hundred and thirty-eight were sorry to leave camp and hope to come again, and 'think that the general expres- sion of feeling can be best conveyed by quoting in full one of the shorter letters received. I take pleasure in writing to you and thanking you for my trip to Prestatyn,as I enjoyed myself very much.</p>
        <p>. •</p>
        <p>The first thing on my arrival I had a good tea which e did enjoy, and after a good game of cricket with th other boys, and a good time of it we had until bedtime' and good beds we had, and the bathing was lovely, and the teachers saw that everything was right, and oh, what a jolly time I had of it on the mountains and I went to see the waterfalls and it was splendid. ;Ind I may tell you that I bought some camp rock and choco- late for my little brothers and sisters and they were pleased with them and so was father and mother."— Yours Sz.z., THOROED D. LEE. October 23rd, 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 27, 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>LIBERALS AND LABOURISTS</head>
        <p>LIBERALS AND LABOURISTS</p>
        <p>The Liberal Party as Practical</p>
        <p>Ref orniers.</p>
        <p>• [SPECIAL ARTICLE.] Liberals who have had any definite experience of tl# attitude of the Socialist-Labourists in the con- stituencies have not been surprised by the recent policy adopted by Mr. Keir. Hardie and his supporters. It is true that there are the strongest possible reasons why all genuine Progressives should remain united, and that no political issue has arisen in connection with the Bills now before Parliament which justifies a division in the ranks. But unfortunately some of the Socialist leaders are more concerned to extend I their own organisation than to pass moderate reforms, and would welcome the break-up of the Liberal party because it stands for progress upon the sure and porudent lines of our past national development. Happily this is not the aim of the whole of the thirty members who make up the Independent Labour group, , for a number of them are much more in sympathy with the Liberal party than with the ultimate aims of the Socialist propaganda. ,There is, indeed, no very clear reason why the members returned under the auspices of the Labour Representation Committee I should be expected to endorse either Mr. Keir Hardie's or Mr. Hyndman's political creed, for the trade unions generally are not yet committed to the extreme tenets of Sociglisui. The pledge to act independently of the political parties does not necessarily imply hostility to Liberalism, sod as a matter of fact on most of the current topics of the day Independent Labourists and Liberals are found in the same lobby. iThe cooler heads in the party recognise the itactical mistake of weakening the position of the Government at a time when important Labour I Bills are before Parliament, and before an opportunity has been given to the Cabinet to carry out its pro- gramme of financial and administrative reform. Common political prudence would suggest that the , Trade Unionist members should co-operate with other Progressive politicians until some vital, difference of opinion arises or the reforming zeal of the Government has spent itself. To wantonly accentuate political differences at the present juncture is to encourage the House of Lords to mutilate the Labour Bills now before Parliamant, with the possible result that neces- sary and valuable reforms may be deferred for several sears to come by a serious constitutional crisis. We know that the Labour and Radical members assert in vain-glorious fashion that they are ready to try a fall with the House of Lords, but practical politicians— although they recognise that such a struggle may become necessary—are not anxious to precipitate it, for they realise that it would absorb the political energies of the progressive forces of the nation for a long series of years. Unfortunately the disposition to magnify differences is not confined to a single section of the Progressivel forces, but is also being exhibited by a section of Liberals. It may be admitted that the needless loss , of a seat at Cockermouth, and the threatened opposition to the re-election of Mr. S. T. Evans in the Principality were provocative inci- dents, but it should be remembered that nothing is more likely to strengthen the hands of the extremists in the Labour party than ebullitions of temper on the part of Liberal politicians. The conflict between the two opposing tendencies in the Labourl camp will not be ended to-day or to-morrow, and in the long run it may be hoped that prudent counsels will prevail. At the General Election there was a more or less definite understanding that the Liberal Trade Unionist candidates in the mining areas should have the united labour vote, and Mr. Bell and some other Liberal Labourist candidates were also accepted by both sections of Labourists. This spirit of accom- modation was also extended to certain Liberal candi- dates and was reciprocated on the Liberal side by the support of a number of the nominees of the Labour Representation Committee. This policy of conciliation which reduced three-cornered contests to a minimum was frankly encouraged by Mr. Herbert Gladstone,who was then the chief Chip of the Liberal party, and, in spite of some individual objections, had the general support of Progressive politicians. Now, however, that the General Election is out of the way there seems a desire in certain quarters to put an end to a give and take policy and to fieht the battle out in the constituencies. Strangely enough, on the Liberal side this attitude is encouraged by two of the sub- ordinate party Whips. The Master of Elibank and Mr. J. A. Pease are no doubt estimable officials, but it is certainly a novelty in the political world for two of the Junior Whips of a party to lay down its lines of policy. This is usually left to the members of the Government, or where questions of organisation are concerned to the Chief Whip in 'consultation with the Prime Minister. Mr. Murray's intimation that unless his views meet with official acceptance Sir Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman must find another Scottish Whip does not err either upon the side of tact or courtesy, and in former times would have been promptlf followed by dismissal. In these days, how- ever, more latitude is given both to Cabinet Ministers and the minor officials of the party, but it need scarcely be pointed out that the indiscretions of Mr. Murray and Mr. Pease can only impair their influence in the lobby of the House of Commons. It is manifest that the recent epidemic of what is termined " plain speaking " must tend to increase the difficulty of working together for common purposes, though there is really no'good reason why there should not be a great deal of friendly co-operation for years to come. As Mr. Haldane very well put it in his speech at Peebles, on most present day social questions Liberals and Labourists are agreed,and there are many useful reforms which can be carried out by a Liberal Government. Nor does the Libetal party wish to hinder, even if it could, the advocacy of Socialist opinions. Freedom of speech is a cardinal principle of Liberalism, and those who believe that private property should not exist and that land and capital should be nationalised will properly advocate their opinions, while Liberals will meet these arguments when they assume practical shape. But at the present moment very much may be lost by accentuating differences, and the true policy of Liberals and Labourists is to address themselves to the tasks of the hour and not to encourage the forces of reaction by division and strife. It is the glory of the Liberal party that it has not been content with noble ideals, but has sought to realise them in the widening of the basis of Government and the improved condition of the people. The nineteenth century will always be memorable in the annals of the nation as a period of political and social progress, and it is now a matter of history that the impulse which made reform possible had its origin in Liberal principles of government. It is enough to glance at the reforms of the past seventy or eighty years to realise how much has already been accomplished by Liberal statesmanship. It is true that in some instances—as in the Reform Act of 1867 —Conservative Governments stepped in and passed measures which they had previously opposed, but it is indisputable that such legislation was directly due to the Liberal pioneers who prepared the way for reform. Without entering into det til, the great reforms of the Victorian era may be summarised under three or four headings. First of all there was the widening of political power —not in a single Act, but in three successive instalments of reform. It would have been easy to cavil at each of the Reform Bills as illogical and incomplete, but the Liberal party are content to make the best of imperfect measures, and to use each of them as the starting point of a fresh campaign. The same thing was true of the extension of religious freedom, the reform of local government, and the gradual breaking down of fiscal barriers. The conflict in each case was long and arduous, but as practical reformers the Liberal party took all that they could obtain, while never losing sight of their ultimate goal. The advanced political reformers of our own times may well imitate the patience and persistence, as well as the zeal, of these early reformers. And if pro- gress has been slow, in no country of the world has it been more sure than in this island Kingdom. Our modern reformers are apt to forget the strength of the social and political forces opposed to them. It is true that the last General Election was a great triumph for the democracy,but in the future, as in the past, there will be period.; of reactirm, and if fresh advances are to be made it will be necessary for Liberals and Labourists to lay aside their differences and to unite in support of urgent /dorms for which the public opinion of the country is ripe. To waste energy and temper in fighting one another is to court defeat at the hands of the common enemy. Let the Labour members regard themselves if they will as the advance guard of the army of progress, but at the same time they will do well to remember that victoiy is only possible when they have the support of the general body of Liberal opinion in the country.l And in this connection it should not be forgotton that the vast majority of the Liberal members are in full sympathy with a generous programme of social and political reforms. As Mr. Lloyd George put it in his</p>
        <p>%</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <p>• r •</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>50 POINTS ! It was never thought for a moment that Warrington would reach the half-century on Saturday against such a team as Huddersfield. The most that was expected or hoped for was that the 30 points average for home matches would be maintained. But the Warrington men excelled any display they have given for years and fairly revelled in goals and tries until the total was a round 50, against Hudders- field's solitary 3. Nothing like it has been seen for thirteen years—since 1893—when Warrington put up their record score of 59 points to nil against Rugby. The homeFters were worth every point on Saturday, but Huddersfield scarcely deserved to suffer to the extent they did.</p>
        <p>The secret of Warrington's phenomenal success was apparent to anyone who looked closely with the object of discovering it. Method, more than men had to do with the rout of the visitors. Warrington have gradually schooled themselves into a style of play Modelled on New Zealand tactics, with the same result. The secret is in the art of passing and running versus punting and pushing. When a Warrington man got the ball on Saturday, he started to run towards the Huddersfield goal and got as far as he could before parting to an eager club-mate. Forwards followed the example of backs, and some of the cleverest combination on Saturday was displayed by front-rank men who have discovered that there is a use for hands as well as feet in the new Rugby game. Warrington are backing up better in every match, so that if a man gots tackled with the ball he has generally someone within reach to pass to. Huddersfield operated more on old-fashioned lines with the result that they were often left standing when Warrington were getting tries. They played smartly, but the attack was developed too much on the orthodox plan to be successful against a speedier team such as Warrington. Several times on Saturday the home attack was organised 'so quickly from a smart kick up and pass that the defenders were quite out of place and unable to do anything to check the movement.</p>
        <p>In the first half, Warrington got four goals, four tries (20 points) to Huddersfield's single try, yet the game was very well contested. At certain stages the visitors looked like holding their own, and were unfortunate in not getting another score. They were frequently in the Warrington half, but even then it was just as likely as not that a sudden break-away by the home backs would result in five points. On resuming operations, Warrington completely ran the visitors off their feet. No less than six goals, six tries were put on in the forty minutes, and some• of the attacking movements were carried out like light- ning. The score went up by leaps and bounds till the 30 was soon left far in the rear. Then came 40, and five minutes later 50 capped the season's exploits. Huddersfield always came up smiling after each blow, and played with any amount of pluck and good temper. I only hope they will profit by the demonstration that was given them, and smite some other club hip and thigh.</p>
        <p>Fish deserves a paragraph all to himself. He broke all records for the Club, including his own, by scoring 9 goals, 4 tries, 30 points during the afternoon. Eight of his goals came from successive attempts. It will be remembered that against Goole in 1900, Fish obtained 7 goals, 5 tries, 29 points, so that he only improved on his previous best by a single point., Fish was the Fish of old on Saturday. He was in scintillating form and his runs reminded us that there is only one man who can go through a whole team from touch-line to goal- posts without ever being grassed. The Huddersfield men might as well have tried to catch 'a fish in his native element with the naked fingers, as get hold of the slippery Warrington threequarter on Saturday. He was positively great, and as the "Leeds Mercury" says, " Here at least is one Northern Union player who would have played for England against the Springboks had there been no split in the ranks." Other men on the home side who specially dis- tinguished themselves were Lees, Berry, Thomas, and Boardman. On the Huddersfield side there was not much chance of glory, but it should be said in all fairness that two men stood out conspicuously. Bartholomew, late of Morecambe, played a magnifice,nt game at full back, and but for his heroic efforts and clearances the score must have been considerably larger. On the left wing too, Wade was both speedy and clever. It was a fine ran by him that led up to the visitors' only score.</p>
        <p>The following is the League Table up-to-date : For Agst. P. W. L. L. D. G. T. r. G. T. P. centage Hall fax 6 0 1 25 25 125 10 13 59 92•65 Wig 7 6 1 oan Leeds Runcorn ITgton 7 5 1 1 31 38 176 10 8 44 78'57 Barrow Broughton Hunslet Re ighley 1 1 20 25 115 10 11 53 75.00 York 4 2 0 13 19 83 11 16 70 6666 Swinton Oldham Batley Widnes 6 3 3 0 11 15 67 13 19 83 50.00 Dewsbury Salford Rochdale Bradford Leigh Hull 7 2 5 0 17 22 100 25 30 140 28.57 Hud'aleld Pontefract Waleflld T St. Helens Hull R. 12. Bramley 8 U 8 0 4 5 23 28 43 185 10000 Liverpl.C. ... 8 0 8 0 6 6 20 43 74 309 100'00</p>
        <p>Warrington have climbed a little higher, and in the course of the next few weeks they should be very near the top. Halitax reign there alone without a single defeat, but I think they will come down a few pegs when they begin to cross the border.</p>
        <p>To-day, the second round of the Lancashire Senior Cup is to be played off. No anxiety need be felt on Warrington's account seeing that they are at home to Barrow. The latter have a percentage equal to War- rington's in the Leauge, but I fancy they will find opponents at Wilderspool of different calibre to any they have yet met. How will the average go on I wonder ? Warrington should not be far off the thirties again, with all due respect to the North Lancashire men. The other matches in the competition are Oldham v. Rochdale' Broughton v. Swinton, and Salford v. Runcorn. The latter is the only one which suggests possibilities of a surprise.</p>
        <p>Warrington will unfortunately be without Isherwood who injured his shoulder last week. The vacancy will be taken by Cartwright, a well-built and promis- ing " A" teatner who gets his first chance with the seniors. Naylor comes in the forwards vice Belton, and Brooks replaces Jordan on the wing. There will be an opportunity of comparing Berry with the County full back, Gifford of Ba:row. I have no fears for the Warrington man.</p>
        <p>Tha Warrington selection is : 13erry, full hack ; Fish, Cartwright, Taylor; and Brooks,.threequarters ; Lees and Hockenhall, half-backs ; Boardman, Preston, Thomas, Heath, Harmer, and Naylor forwards.</p>
        <p>The." A " team will bring off their fixture with Salford "A" at 2.15, and the team chosen should be quite capable of avenging the defeat at the Willows a few weeks ago : viz : Knight, full back ; Lambert, Jones, Jordan, and Landor, threequarters ; Lloyd and Kaye, half backs ; Shugars, Swift, Jolley, Forster Bradshaw and Belton, forwards.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire and Yorkshire County match takes place on Saturday next at Salford. Only one Warring- ton man has been chosen —Naylor—but Fish, Isher-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>RISE OF DEMOCRACY.</head>
        <p>THE RISE OF DEMOCRACY.</p>
        <p>ITS MESSAGE TO LIBERALISM. Rev. G. Harvey Cook's Interesting Address to the Junior Liberals.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warri•ngton Junior Liberal Association were afforded au intellectual treat on Monday evening when the Rev. C. Harvey Cook read a highly interesting and instructive paper on " The rise of democracy and its message to Liberalism." There was a good attendance preside I over by Mr. Joseph Hawthorn. The Rev. C. HARVEY Coot( said democracy was not the form of Government that had risen. It was primal, original, natural. The various other forms, monarchism, oligarchisin, aristocracy were the things which had a rise. Democracy was in the beginning— world without end—not, of course, democracy as we had it, yet still a democracy. We should not understand our own democracy unless we understood the genesis and history of ancient democracies. The message of democracy was exactly THE SAME TO BOTH GREAT PARTIES in the State. It had not one message for the Liberal party and another for the Conservative, but the same for both. Let it be clearly grasped that in what he had to say the application was not to one party but to both —not to one Association but to both. Tracing the word " democracy " to its Greek source, Mr. Cook pointed out that it meant Rule of the People. Historically they needed to remember the ancient qualifications which surrounded this rule of the people. It was in its primitive form government by the citizens, but in an altogether different sense to Our idea of govern- ment by the citizens. In Greek democracies it was the citizen of a certain standing only who had the power of Government, with whom rested the executive. The early forms of democracy, though so different to modern dtmocracy, yet were always in principle if not in fact—often not in fact because in Greece Perciles, the great democrat, was simply the boss and the democracy under him was purely a one man Government; so also in some of the periods of Roman history, as with Cmsar—but in principal dome- racy had always been opposed to at least three things : —monarchy, the rule ef one ; aristocracy, the rule of the nobles or dominant class ; and oligarchy, the rule of the few. That earlier democracy had a very limited idea as to who were "the people" went without ques- tion. No slave was enfranchised, only citizens of certain standing. Our later day legend" Government of the people by the people for the people" had a significance which Greece and Rome never even faintly realized and which, with all our hulla-balloo about it, we scarcely valued. , The features of early democratic Government which distinguistied it from the form with which we were familiar were: that only the citizens could appear, that slavery existed and the slaves had no political rights and few civil ones,and the democracy was limited in number and only included the city population. Mr. Cook proceeded to sketch the promihent examples of early democracy afforded by Greece and Rome and afterwards dealt with the growth of democratic government among the Germanic Vibes which overthrew the Roman empire.</p>
        <p>MODERN DEMOCRACY', it could net be too emphatically stated, differed essen- tially from the ancient and medieval forms of it. Our democracy was a new creation in politics. No longer I did it consist of a body of burgesses belonging to a city, limited in number and having to appeal and appear, for ' themselves. 111illions inhabiting all parts of the country and the Empire and Britain beyond the seas were in power by the system or representative government. The government of such countries as Great Britain, New Zealand, Australia, Canada, America. was a vast undertaking, and could only be accomplished by the representative system. Germany, France, Italy, Spain, and every other country that had more or less of systematic representation found that only by that means was there any approximation to government of the people, for the people, by the people. That various countries had monarchies, like England, Germany, Spain, Italy, that other countries had republics, like France and America, did ndt affect the proved fact that the system of representation seemed as near perfect at least in principle whatever it might be in operation, that human genius could Lame. The abolition of slavery, serfdom and other nnfree conditions stamped the countries and the nations where the democratic principle was given its highest exposition. Some countries having a certain kind of;representative system did not possess that feedom of residence, of conscience and profession which we had more or less realised. But in all the nations where there was a,oy pretence to democratic government the trend of it was to abolish all privileged classes. We had reached the present point in our political genesis through centuries of severest struggle, and those who took short views of history, or who formed their judgments on passing events or the evidence of 50 or 100 years, were not in a position to give any authoritative statement as to whether we were bettering of worsening. We had, continued 'lr. Cook, inrparted into the word democracy a meaning which by all that was just and equitable ought not to exist. Democracy avAS NOT THE RULE OE A CLASS even thongh it be the working class. Democracy was the rule of the people, and the people was more than a particular class of them. This native and necessary distinction of the word was overlooked by nearly every- body. Democracy had no more to do with the working classes tlisn the rich Classes. Democracy stood for a principle, not a grade of society. Democracy was the rule of the people, not the rule of the artizan, or mechanic, or middle class, but tho rule of the people— the rule of all, for all, by all. We could not too quickly root out of our minds the false notion that democracy meant the rule of the folk who were not wealthy. Democracy was not a class title—the antithesis of aristoracy, oligarchy, or monachv—but the joining together of all the people in the State in power of , government by the people. It was the whole of the people, not a section of them, banded together and exercising the executive power of Government ; and that was not understood and because not understood, politicians talked of the democracy as if it were a thing of clasq' a division of Society, whereas it was the whole of us taken together as a unit. Aristocracy was exclu- sive, it was a class distinction, a division of society ; but democracy was inclusive, it. knew no class distinc- tion, it had no divisions, it embraced the whole. He could not plead for the Government of all by a class, even though that class be the one to which be belonged.</p>
        <p>, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>STOCK AND SHARE MARKETS.</head>
        <p>STOCK AND SHARE MARKETS.</p>
        <p>That was neither a just nor equitable principle. Class government by what so many called democracy •would be as nauseous to him as class government by what was called aristocracy. The great task before the modern nations was TO WELD SOCIETY INTO A TRUE DENIOCRACY wherein rich and poor, high and low, should work together for the weal of all, when all classes should co-operate for the advancement of all and none work singly for his own grade. Until we shook off the class incubus which we had attached in our mind to what was meant by democracy, we should, as Mrs. Poyser would say, "get no forrader." The growth of the real as distinct from the veneer democracy was exampled in 1,000 different ways in the social life of our time. It had brought improvement in the art of printing, it had brought cheap and sometimes nasty books, it had brought industrial betterment, opened up technical capacities and resources, given men, not of a class but of all classes, mastery over nature in a way never before dreamed of. The leaven of democracy bad leavened not only a class, but was leavening the whole* limits of society. Our railways, penny pest. electric tramways, telegraphs, telephones, were not the result of the mere rise of a party in the State miscalled, mischievous in its misnaming, democracy, but were the result of the working in the whole of the fabric of society of those great ideals which were resident within it. Lie almost grew helpless when he noted how few seemed to really grasp the true significance of what was meant by democracy and democratic govern- ment. It was not the overthrow of one class by another, not the rule of one section by the others, but the binding close together in the holy bonds of humanity " those whom God bath joined together.", So long as politicians sought to divorce the union which democracy stood for, so long would we have the frightful schisms which marred the good estate of the State. He was not deluded into believing that if they put the workingman into power he would watch pablic interests more carefully , than the leisured class. That he would keep his eye vigilantly on his own and his class's interest as the aristocrat had done, he (Mr. Cook) felt as surely per- suaded as if he had tangible evidence and data to sup- port. his thesis. It was only human. He did not look with any pleasure to the incoming of a class democracy. His outlook and vision embraced thewhole mass of men indissolubly wedded in the bonds of firm necessity, each to each and all to all. Not in change of class but</p>
        <p>rusrsc OF' CLASS lay our salvation. We wanted an educated electorate. Such vast masses of constituents as ours were too easily controlled by caucuses, too easily influenced by special interests. Party watchwords hid real issues ; popular passions upset plans of experienced policy ; demagogues ousted statesmen ; and narrow selfish prospects destroyed great visions and " when there is no vision the people dies." Democracy, as it was, was but a lusty infant, but a lusty infant which fed and trained and educated had a great function in modern history—that function being nothing less than the re-modelling of Society and Government on the prin- ciple of all for all by all. He said at the outset that the message of democracy was the same to both great parties in the State, and he meant it. The message of Labour to the Liberal party might be different from the messages of Labour to the Conservative party. The message of the so-called Democratic Federation and all other such movements might be of one significance to Liberal and another significance to Conservative. These, however, must not be confused with the message of democracy. They were side issues of parties within the democracy. The message as he read it, written by unseen hand in hieroglyphic on the walls of our State by democracy was this : "Be it known unto all parties alike that not in them lies the benison of the future, but in the whale of society, whether Liberal, Labour, Conservative or others, working together for the common wealth." Society abounded with earnest and educated persons who</p>
        <p>STOOD ALOOF PROM ALL PARTIES but who,if some organization only gave them oppor- tunity, would place all their power and abilities at the service of the State, that was democracy, but who would not serve a party, nor join a wing. The democracy that was coming would not only insist on the rights of man but on the duties of man. We were in veritable twilight as yet in this great march of men. Loud voices were raucously rasping on the rights of man; only here and there a soft lilting of the duties of man was heard. The message of democracy was to all classes alike: "Set your house in order, toil not for your own class, spin not for your own comfort, but take wider view, larger vision, fold all men in your robes. Correct the faults of your Liberal partizanship, 'by more catholic standards. Correct the faults of your Conservative party by the same catholic out-look. Think not that in the triumph of Liberalism, democracy has come to her own, nor that in the triumph of Conservatism democracy is defeated. She is more than either or both. She is the people, not those who are I. L. P's or Progressive Labourites or any other. She is the patient people, long suffering and acquainted with grief from whom the so-called practical politicans of all faiths among us bide their faces because there is no beauty to be desired in her. And yet the people are coming to their own. The privileged classes are beginning to see that privilege is not agreeable with ethical humanity. The unprivileged are seeing that unprivilege confers no patent rights to take from men that have and give to them that have not, but both are beginning to awaken to a new moral sense—a consciousness of belonging to each other, or being indissolubly linked up together,that privilege is an anachronism and that might is by no means right." In so far as all the parties in the State I were pressing forward the coming of democracy he was ' proud of them all, and they were all agencies working together for good. Conservative and Unionist, Labour and Democrat. Liberal and Radical, marchei under various flags' but there was one flag over them all which if they forgot they were not worthy to be called citizens of England, and that was the hag" democracy." They would never understand democracy if they were not something more than a Liberal or a Conservative. Radical or a Labour man, nay just as far as the party spirit controlled their mind or will, they lost power to grasp what democracy was, lie was not opposed to party government, but he was opposed to any narty imagining that it represented democracy and the other party did not. Democracy was too' big a thing to be pocked into any party. Whether the people should be a great democracy or a little one—that was the question of the hour. The ideal of democracy as all of us working in love for all of us was an ideal calculated to chasten men b conceitsi and awaken ethical considerations which 'he party shibboleths could ever coin. Democracy was altogether other than State Socialism. ['hat meant mathrial good by force, whereas democracy meant betterment by moral suasion, betterment by the exercise or those moral , energies which lay at the root of all proper political propaganda and progress. (Applause.) The CRAIRNIAN said it was a matter for regret that many professing Christians had hitherto opposed the progress of democracy, but to-day we wore living in healthier times. An interesting discussion was Vilma part in by Wessrs Ford, R. Lloyd Jones, H. Gray, Burt, Ft tiny, Davies, Dale, Gray and Sherbarn and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Rev. C. Harvey Cook.</p>
