      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-10-27_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>WARING'S NEW GALE.ERIES.</head>
        <p>WARING'S NEW GALE.ERIES.</p>
        <p>The phenomenal interest occasioned by the opening of Waring and Gillow's New Galleries in-0 xford-street, London, has evidently developed into a permanent character. The volume of business transacted has con- siderably surp,tssed the Directors' Tiost sanguine expec- tations. The many acres of magnificent showrooms, model houses and specimen rooms are dully thronged with delighted customers. Adruirslau is evinced in a practical manner, and the transactions generally in the forty departments of artistic decora.tion and house equipment afford emphatic evidence of the wisdom find foresight displayed in the conception and organisation of an unparalleled enterprise. In the furnishing and beautifying of the modern borne all classes are catered for. That Waring's capacity and resources are adequate to cope with any possible expansion of the business however great, has been convincingly proved by the past four months experience. And the buying public, grateful for the opportunity of procur- ing the most artistic furnishing at incredibly low rates are testifying their appreciation by anent-ding to the unique and incomparable galleries an unstinted measure of well-merited support. Concurrestly with this vast development of the private trade, it is pleasing to note that the firm fully maintains it premier position for carrying out big public contracts. It has just been selected against all corners to decorate two ocean liners for the leading ship-builders in Italy, and it has just completed the principal work on the Khedive's new yacht, In connection with the Waring-White Building Co., which has constructed the now Liverpool Cotton Exchange in record time, it has secured yet another triumph of skill, experience and resources.</p>
        <p>THE MILAN EXPOSITION: HONOURS FOR A SVELL-INOWN FIRM.</p>
        <p>, 1 The awards of the adjudicators at the Milan Exposition I have recently been announced. The well-known talking machine manufacturers, the Columbia Phonograph Company, General, come out with highest honours in their class, carrying off the much coveted Grand Prize. Not only is this triumph memorable in itself, but in relation to earlier successess it possesses exceptional significance. This is the third time in succession that Columbia productions have gained the highest possible awards at the great world expositions. At Paris in 1000, ,1 they secured the Grand Prix; at the St. Louis Exposi- tion of 1931, their merits were recognised by two Grand Prizes and three Gold Medals; and now at Milan, in the very heart of the artistic world where musical judgment is ispecially critical, the Columbia Grapho- ' phones and records have won the greatest possible dis- tinction. This series of successes is quite unapproached by any other talking machine makers. To have thus demonstrated the pre-eminence of their productions, in face of world-wide competition, on three separate and independent occasions, is a feat of which any firm might well be proud; and the Columbia Phonograph Company, who, by the way, have now moved into their new premises at 61, 63, Oxford-street, London, W., will doubtless experience one result in a further increase of inquiries for their "Price Book '2O" which gives full particulars of the Columbia cylinder and disc Grapho- phones and Records.</p>
      </div>
