        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL BLBOTIONS, 190 b. TO THE ELECTORS OF IHE LATCHFORD WARD. • LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I have consented to be nominated for your representatlion. For four or five years I have taken a prominent part In the most important moral movement which has been seen in Warrington since its incorporation —the strenuous and costly struggle for civiJ purity. At sixteen great public meetings In the Parr Hall and at contested elections in every ward but one, the ratepayers have endorsed the platform of the reformers. Sound public policy has always dictated that there should be no personal interest accruing to neblie repre- sentatives as tne result of their membership of public bodies. The present Government and the House of Commons, In reference to the conduct of their respective members, are scrupulously exacting In this regard ; your present Member of Parliament and your late Conservative Member in January last clearly and categorically approved the princle that "no representative elected to any public bodyshould derive pecuniary profit Or pay- ment, dfrectly or indirectly, by reason ef his membership of such body. The Municipal Corporations Acts specifically enact that trading, or the receiving of profit by members of Corporetions, renders such members ineligible to hold office. Of the seven years covered by the late Trading Inquiry, Alderman Bennett held the office of Mayor's auditor for five year. and. received £l5O, and for the last two years of the period Mr. A. J. Jolley held the position and received £5O. The Non-Traders challenged these payments as clearly illegal and the electors of eairtlela Ward, in November 1902, rejected Mr. A, J. Jolley, who at the time of his defeat was acting as the Mayor's Auditor, by the enormous majority of 790 votes bince that memorable election no one has accepted any lee as Mayor's Auditor until this year. Mr. W. J. Wilson, the retiring candidate for your Ward, having sought oat and informed Llouneillors Pemberton and Lord that he would not take any payment, accepted the office and, a few weeks ago, contrary to his own clear declaration, received from the Borough Treasurer £3O as payment tor his services. This payment being, as I believed, highly improper as well as illegal. I immediately announced through the press that the moral issues involved would have to be fought out at the forthcoming election. In pursu- ance of _that announcement a caudidate was at once selected Who owing to imperative medical advice, has had to abandon his candidature and, in the absence of any other substitute, I have no alternative but to take his place. The supreme issue, upon which I desire your verdict are (1) Whether Councillor Wilson can henceforth act as an independent 'representative of the ratepayera ; whether he has not in short male himself a mere pawn or tool of the party from wnona he has received the office which has yielded him the £3O be has received, and whether, it so, he is a fit and proper representative for your Ward. (i) Whether you are not satisfied that the chief " evils which have disgraced our Municipal government in Warrington have arisen from and are the inevitable result of the mixing up of tee affairs of the town with the party politics of the nation, and of the Beate ; that the lines upon which national parties divide have no necessary connection with the business of a town ; and that snob connection opens the way for political corn- poets, and behind these " for countless schemes of public plunder and civic corruption." Audit so (3) Whether it is not time that you ceased electing representatives for purely buslness duties merely because they are Conservative or Liberal, or blue or red. The issue, ladies and gentlemen, is in your hands. I have not sought nor do I covet election. I shall not personally canvass a single burgess, but If you approve my past and present public work,it you think I can serve you, I will undertak s, if you vote for me, and I should be returned, to do what I can, with a single eye to your best interests and your faithlui service. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen. Your obedient servant, JAMAS DODDS. October 19th. 1905.</p>
