        <p>ELIIOTIONS, 1906. TO THE ELECTORS OF 6T. AtisTlN'o WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, Having been requested by a large number of Rate- payers, including many tradekmen, workingmen, and all classes in the Ward, I have (fomented to be nominated as a candidate for St. Austin's Ward. If you honour me by electing me, I shall at all times do my utmost to deserve your confidence. I shall always oppose unnecessary and extravagant expenditure and support true economy with efficiency. My only object will be to promote the prcsperity, pro- gress, and the best interests of the town. I am, Ladles and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, JAMES MAW WELL. 32, Market Street, Warrington. ,</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL. ELHOTIOIki. 1906. TO THE ELECTORS OP THE BEWSEY WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I find that I have been nominated as a Candidate for your Ward, in which I reside, at the forthcoming Municipal Election. This has been done entirely without my knowledge, and I feel that it would be very nugratet ul if I did not do my best in return to win )our confidence and support. If, therefore, you honour me by electing me as your representative, I promise to serve you to the best of my I stand unfettered by any pledges, and unallied with any party. I shall, if elected, endeavour to support all movements for the improvement of the town, the purity of public life, and the efficient, economical administra- tion or every branch of Corporate business. As you ate aware, the working of the Education Act falls in a very great measure upon the members of the Town Council, and lithe new Education Bill passes into law the importance of these duties and responsibilities will be much increased. This lea subject in which I am particularly interested, and if elected. I will do my utmost for the advance- ment of Educatio-t in the town, and to guald from injustice the just claims of parents. I am, Ladles sad Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant. OWEN TOOLE. 152, Winwick-road. Warrington.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. ' MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. NOVEMEER 19:6.—T0 THE ELECTORS OF EMMY WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, At the unanimous invitation Of the Liberal Ward Committee and of the Progressive Labour League endorsed by the Executive of the Liberal Association. I have pleasure in offering myself as a Candidate for the representation of the Ward in the Town Council. Being a resident and ratepayer in the Ward my intelests are identical with those of the large body of electors, who may rely on my giving their needs the same care and attention I should give to my Own. It would be my endeavour while safeguarding efficiency, to secure such economies and reforms of a alministration as would reduce the burden of the rates to a minimum, thus promoting the greatest possible extension of the industry of the town. I fully recognise the importance of keeping the Council free from all taint of profit-seeking by its members and should endeavour to maintain such a standard of honour in the Council as would guarantee its purity In this respect. It would be my endeavour to secure for the Working Classes the utmost benefits of the public services, to make education free as regards school fees at the earliest date possible. and by the application of such powers as affect Housing Conditions, sanitation. Unemployment, &amp;c. ; to alleviate distress, improve industrial conditions, and raise the standard of living of the community. I am, Ladles and Gentlemen, Yours Faithfully, JOHN A. J. MORTON. 117, Bewsey-road, Warrington.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. mUNICIPAL ELECLION. NOVEMBER Ist., 1906.—T0 THR ELUCTORS OF ORFORD WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN. On the lit of November you will have to give your judgment upon my conduct during the few months I have had:the honour to represent your important Ward In the Town Council. It has been my chief endeavour to ascertain what were the best interesta of the general ratepayers and especially of the large party of workingmen in the Ward, and I have on every occasion voted accordingly. I confidently refer you to my votes. indeed I sin satisfied you will find that my work in the Council has been more favour- able to labour and to the struggling poor than that of the Socialist Membere of the Council, who on many occasions have not shewn the back-bone that is required in dealing with the proposals of the official set and the officials. Although a Liberal in Imperial matters. I have become more and more cJnvinced that we should be better without party politics in the Council. and that members should have neither party nor personal interests ta serve. I have therefore worked as a member of the Non-Trading Part y whose representatives I have found to be deter- mined to stop all extravagances, and to still further reduce the rates. If you return me I shall give my loyal sqpport to that party, balleying 'that in so doing I am bestserving your interests. \ ' Your Obedient Servant, CUNNINUTON. 32, Longford Street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. M UNICIPAL Ei.xurios, 1906.—T0 THE BURGBSSES OF ORFORD WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN. Having been requested by a large and influential body of Ratepayers of both political °Onions and elm o by the Conservative Ward Committee to become a Candidate for election as your representative on November Ist next, I have consented to do so. Having resisted in the Ward for upwards of ten years. and in the town for 22 years., can justly claim to know the requirements of this important Workingmen a Ward. I am a determined advocate of economy with efficiency In the spending of all public moneys. The rates, through the extravagance of the Town Council. have become almost unbearable. It will be my aim to keep the expenditure within reasonable boun4s, in order that a reduction in the rates might be brought about. On the qu.istion of Municipal Trading by Councillors end others connected with the Corporation, I hold strong views, and if elected will do everything in my power with a view to putting an end to this state of things. In cruclusion. If you do me the honour to elect me as one of your representatives to the Town Cannon, I can assure you that your confidence will not be misplaced and it will be my ambition to serve the Ward and town faithfully, to the utmost of my ability. . I remain, Ladles acid Gentlemen. Yomrs Obediently, W. McaIRIL SLOANE. 57, Orford Avenue, October 23rd, 1915.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. NIUMCIPAL E;l4r GI TONS; 1906.-TO THE BURGRSSRS OF ST. JOHN'S W ARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN Having been requested by a large number of rate- payers, and at the unanimous wish of the Conservative Ward Committee, 1 offer myself for election as your representative in the Town Council. I do not believe in the categorical promises made by many candidates when seeking the suffrages of the burgesses, for in many cases those promises are forgotten as soon as the election is over. I do not tnerefore pro- pose to compile a list of matters that shall receive my immediate attention, if elected, but you may confidently rely upon it that,if you do me the honour to return me as your representative, it will be my earnest endeavour to serve the best interests of the ratepayers generally. and those of St. John's Ward in particular, without regard to any class or section. The great Interest I have taken for a number of years In Friendly Societe work, which goes so much to benefit the working elasses,is too well known to require mention here. With regard to the vital necessity for absolule recti- tude in municipal life, I am opposed to any member of Lisa Council engaging in any illegal or immoral trading and so benefiting from his position. I am well known to the majority of you, and should always be willing to hear any suggestions and give my heartiest support to the just complatu:s of the rate- payers. In appealing again for your kind support I can only say that if you are of opinion that I can be of service to my fellow townsmen as a member of the Town Council, and by your votes place me at the head of the poll on the lit of Novereber next, I shall always endeavour to merit the confidence placed in me. I am, Ladles and Gentlemen. Your Obedient Servant, THOMAS S. STMIL, Supnyside, Marshhouse-lane, Warrington. 20th October, 19C6.</p>
        <p>Town. BALT, wARD.—TO v,...attitiulUCl II tut 1. ea. tiILEUTLUOI* (NOVEMBER) 1906. OF THE ABOVE WARD. LADIES AND GEISTI.EMEN• THE ELECTORS t have been receiving numerous deputations for the last ten days from all sorts and conditions of voters In the Ward to allow myself to be nominated as a Candi- date at the Election to be held on the first of November next, wad it has been urged upon me that I have been in Warrington all my life, and that for many years I have not taken any active Interest in Politics or Municipal matters. I was reminded that I represented Howley Ward in the Council for three years, and was never rejected by the voters of that ward and that I ought to have been now one of the Senior Aldermen of the Borough, as I had always been considered to be Interested in the Municipal affairs from a native's point of view for the past twenty- two years. Under such heavy pressure brought to bear upon me that I ought to again resume an active interest in the Concerns of the Borough, and to try (in part) to put a stop to the continual running down of everything in connection with our Town's affairs, and not to prevent every good Improvement. I have consented to stand, and therefore hope that you will give me your support on Thursday week next, and if elected I shall do every- thing to the best of my ability in toe good Interests of our much maligned Town. I may asict that I do not believe In politics being introduced into the Council, and I therefore Until act entirely Independent of all parties. remain. Yours faithfully, ARV-tuft BROWNE. Warrington, 23rd October. 1906. •</p>
