        <p>, POPULAR PENNY CONCERTS. Mr. James Dodds' Advice to Young People. Bold-street Wesleyan School was filled to its utmost capacity on Saturday evening, when the second popular penny concert of the season was held. An admirable pro- gramme was submitted, and the audience showed its appreciation in no half-hearted way, encores being fre- quent. Songs were very pleasingly given by Miss Griffiths, Mr. A. Griffiths and Mr. Plumpton, whilst Messrs. Woods and Noden rendered two enjoyable duets. A pianoforte solo by Miss Stirling was well received, and selections by the Bold-street Quartette Party proved highly popular. Mr. Alfred Grounds was responsible for the humorous element, and his two clever new sketches created a great deal of mirth. Mr. Frank Hickman, who had arranged the programme, acted as accompanist. Mr. JAMES DODDS presided and at the conclusion of the first part of the entertainment said he had been asked to say a few words at that stage and hardly knew how he could better occupy five minutes than in giving a little adviceto the many young people before him. It was very proper they should have play; it was well they should have change of occupation, and recreation such as they were then enjoying, but he hoped they would value and mark time, and with all diligence work them- selves forward in the world. Diligence included all the virtues a learner or student could have, the qualities of conduct that lead on to the acquirement of instruc- tion and improvement. It had been well said that youth was the golden season of life; it was indeed the seed time. If they failed to sow, or sowed tires instead of wheat, they could never hope to arrive at much. Let them get into habits of study and work, waste no time, make up their minds upon some special pursuit, and they might rest assured that with the improved and improving educa- tional facilities they might surely hope to succeed, whether in commerce, in literature, in science or in a profession. Education and culture, mental and moral development, together with correct c onduot, which was the supreme test of Christian character, were the attain- ments which would more and more, as time moved on, be looked upon as farsuperior to mere wealth or aristo- cratic birth. (Applause.) Let them work while young, while their minds ware fluid or plastic before they began to h‘rden as they gradually would to the con- sistency of rock or iron. The habits of an old man could not be altered; he would, as he began proceed and go on to the last. He asked them however, in their e forts for their own promotion, not to become selfish or mean and concluded with an anecdote showing the self-denial nd generosity of a ragged school boy. • MR. CHAMBERLAIN I STARTLED THE DOCTOR. When in America Mr. Chamberlain consulted a doctor w ho put pressing question as to cigars. " Mr.Chamber- ain," said he," I think, if I may say so' that yousmoke a great deal too fnuch." "Perhaps I do," was the imperturble reply, "but then, you must remember, I don't take any exercise "—an original view of the case which completely dumbfounded the physician.—" Lady Dorothy Nevill's Reminiscences."</p>
