        <p>recent Cardiff speech, it is for the Liberal party to: cope seriously with the social conditions of the people, to attack boldly the root causes of pove.rty and degradation, and to economise the national resources, so that before many years have elapsed an honest sustenance may be provided for deserving old age. It is upon these lines that Liberalism and Labour can triumph over reaction, and bring about a happier era for all classes of the population.</p>
        <p>wood, and Lees are all on the reserve. Warrington play Wigan the same day, and unfortunately for the visitors they will have to release Leytham and Jenkins for the County. Warrington are in luck's way, unless the reserves happen to be called up. Hockey. As explained in my notes last week the Stockton Heath first eleven were without a match. This was very unfortunate, as some stiff games are to be played within the next few weeks, and a little more practice would have done no harm. The second eleven were playing Western,and managed to win 5-2, after an even game. 'There is not much to be said about the game, as the ground was in bad con- dition and wanted rolling. Western played the three back game, which against a forward line who use their brains is not a very effective defence, To sum the game up I should call it "vigorous if not scientific." The goals were scored by W. Richardson 3,A. H. Rathbone 2. -- This week the Oxton teams will be met, and as Stockton Heath will only have a moderate side in the field, both teams will do well to avoid defeat. The fol- lowing are the teams :-Ist Eleven (at Oxton) R. G. Pierpoint (goal), E. E. Woods and E. Pierpoint (backs), E. Fairclough, R. liallews and E. R. Jones (halves), C. Smith, S. Baxter, R. Pierpoint, W. Richardson and H. Dolan ; referee, W. Carter ; train, 12.50, Central Station. -- 2nd Eleven (at Arpley), E. Pairclough (goal), P. B. Hawtiu and B. Silcock(backs), H.W. Guest, J.Houghton and 0. de Jong, C. S. Walker, NV. Lionel, A. H. Bath- bone, H. Owen and A. Hodgkiason ; referee, N. W. Bellamy ; bully off, 3 o'clock. -- Latchford returned to their last season's form on Saturday, when they jonrneyed to Sefton and defeated them by 3 goals to .1. It cannot be said that Latchford have really done themselves justice in their previous matches this season. The results-2 draws and the loss of one game appear to better advantage when it is stated that there is only the difference of a goal in the respec- tive scores, viz., 7 for and S against. However, Latch- ford were in their element on Saturday, and did extremely well in defeating their opponents by such a margin. -- To-day Latchford entertain Waterloo and will be represented by the following team :—Goal, r. Wilkin- son full backs. C. Hale and 1. Dixon ; half backs, D. Travis, J. B. Wilkinson and W. B. Cobb ; forwards, W. Spenseley, E. F. Chile, IL Warburton, F. Grounds, and J. Callon. Latchford 2nd had to submit to their first efeat this season at the hands of Sefton 2nd. This can scarcely be wondered at, especially against a strong team like Sefton, when there are six recruits in the Latchford team: Minards scored first for Latchford, and soon after Sefton equalized. Half-time arrived with each team a goal to their credit. Soon after the re-start Banks netted the ball for the homesters. From this point the visitors outplayed their opponents, and put on 4 goals in a short space of time. Only a good dis- play by the goal-keeper saved them from a heavier defeat. To-day the 2nd Eleven are due at Sale with the folloWing team :—Goal, .T. Appleton ; full backs, A. Albinson and W. H. Pickles ; half backs, J. Wilkinson, G. H. Heald and A. Shakeshaft ; forwards, W. Minards, A. Starkey, E. Banks, A. Allender and C. Hackney.</p>
